33948de8d092,within_group,CentralAfrica,Azande,Abangombi,Agiti,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,permanent joking relationship,,"/Azande_Baxter_1953_76_KS:p::/Azande_Baxter_1953_76_KS::In a modified form this joking relationship is extended to clansmen, known as bakulemi; and between certain clans, such as the Abanvra, Abangombi, Akalinga, and Agiti, a permanent joking relationship has grown up.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Azande_Baxter_1953_76_KS:p,427
53f3493e8b75,within_group,CentralAfrica,Azande,Abangombi,Akalinga,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,permanent joking relationship,,"/Azande_Baxter_1953_76_KS:p::/Azande_Baxter_1953_76_KS::In a modified form this joking relationship is extended to clansmen, known as bakulemi; and between certain clans, such as the Abanvra, Abangombi, Akalinga, and Agiti, a permanent joking relationship has grown up.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Azande_Baxter_1953_76_KS:p,426
a6f825b4e343,within_group,CentralAfrica,Azande,Abanvra,Abangombi,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,permanent joking relationship,,"/Azande_Baxter_1953_76_KS:p::/Azande_Baxter_1953_76_KS::In a modified form this joking relationship is extended to clansmen, known as bakulemi; and between certain clans, such as the Abanvra, Abangombi, Akalinga, and Agiti, a permanent joking relationship has grown up.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Azande_Baxter_1953_76_KS:p,423
a786da9b2057,within_group,CentralAfrica,Azande,Abanvra,Agiti,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,permanent joking relationship,,"/Azande_Baxter_1953_76_KS:p::/Azande_Baxter_1953_76_KS::In a modified form this joking relationship is extended to clansmen, known as bakulemi; and between certain clans, such as the Abanvra, Abangombi, Akalinga, and Agiti, a permanent joking relationship has grown up.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Azande_Baxter_1953_76_KS:p,425
bc494343b0cb,within_group,CentralAfrica,Azande,Abanvra,Akalinga,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,permanent joking relationship,,"/Azande_Baxter_1953_76_KS:p::/Azande_Baxter_1953_76_KS::In a modified form this joking relationship is extended to clansmen, known as bakulemi; and between certain clans, such as the Abanvra, Abangombi, Akalinga, and Agiti, a permanent joking relationship has grown up.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Azande_Baxter_1953_76_KS:p,424
734c5feea0bd,within_group,CentralAfrica,Azande,Abauura,Abangombi,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,Joking Relationship,,"/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1933_390-392c_KS:p::/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1933_390-392c_KS::As the behaviour pattern between blood-brothers is extended to the members of their clans, an extension discussed in the following section, a ‘Joking Relationship’ may grow up between two clans who may insult one another with impunity. I do not think that this [ ] is common in Zandeland, but it appears to happen sometimes, as, for example, between the Abauura and the Abangombi,",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1933_390-392c_KS:p,432
5e74eb153f89,within_group,CentralAfrica,Azande,Akalinga,Agiti,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,Joking Relationship; permanent joking relationship,,"/Azande_Baxter_1953_76_KS:p::/Azande_Baxter_1953_76_KS::In a modified form this joking relationship is extended to clansmen, known as bakulemi; and between certain clans, such as the Abanvra, Abangombi, Akalinga, and Agiti, a permanent joking relationship has grown up. || /Azande_EvansPrittchard_1933_390-392c_KS:p::/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1933_390-392c_KS::As the behaviour pattern between blood-brothers is extended to the members of their clans, an extension discussed in the following section, a ‘Joking Relationship’ may grow up between two clans who may insult one another with impunity. I do not think that this [ ] is common in Zandeland, but it appears to happen sometimes, as, for example, between the Abauura and the Abangombi, the Akalinga and the Agiti, and in one or two other instances, who jest in this manner without offence being taken by either party,",0.9,0.9,2,2,/Azande_Baxter_1953_76_KS:p; /Azande_EvansPrittchard_1933_390-392c_KS:p,428;433
20b88aeb5dae,within_group,CentralAfrica,Azande,Prince Ngere,his small pages,person,group,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,playful accusation,,"/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1957_707_KS:p::/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1957_707_KS::Prince Ngere calls one of his small pages and says « This boy is your lover! » « Oh no master, if it were indeed thus how would we escape your anger and live? »",0.75,0.75,1,1,/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1957_707_KS:p,438
f3b559d8434e,within_group,CentralAfrica,Azande,Zande princes,his favourite wives,group,kin_role,banter,mixed,bantering conversation,,"/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1957_707_KS:p::/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1957_707_KS::One of them told me that Ngere had this kind of bantering conversation with his favourite wives as he sat with them round the evening fire: « That son of mine, you have made a love-bond with him! » « Oh, no master! » « Yes you have, you are merely mocking me. » « That son of mine who is always in the outer court, what is he always waiting for? He has a lover among you. » « Oh master, how you joke with us. »",0.8,0.8,1,1,/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1957_707_KS:p,437
7eec4f6048a6,within_group,CentralAfrica,Azande,a commoner,a prince,group,group,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,sanza,sanza,"/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1962_218_KS:p::/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1962_218_KS::A commoner may speak or act a sanza towards a prince, but unless it is an entirely harmless joking kind of sanza or a highly complimentary one he runs a grave risk in doing so.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1962_218_KS:p,440
d2055a744c3b,within_group,CentralAfrica,Azande,bakulemi,bakulemi,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,,bakulemi,"/Azande_Baxter_1953_76_KS:p::/Azande_Baxter_1953_76_KS::In a modified form this joking relationship is extended to clansmen, known as bakulemi; and between certain clans, such as the Abanvra, Abangombi, Akalinga, and Agiti, a permanent joking relationship has grown up.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/Azande_Baxter_1953_76_KS:p,422
c7ac2f357a4a,within_group,CentralAfrica,Azande,blood-brother,blood-brother,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,Humor partners; joking greeting / warning (sanza),Humor; sanza,"/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1933_390-392a_KS:p::/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1933_390-392a_KS::“The pattern of behaviour between blood-brothers is one of intimacy and equality. One way in which intimacy and equality are expressed between the Humor partners is by each publicly insulting the other.... Thus one will shout to the other: ‘Ai friend! may they cut into your witchcraft! You are witches, you of the Akalinga clan! ’ To which the blood-brother replies: ‘He! may some one spear you! who told you that we are witches, my friend? You’ve just had a good meal over there so you come on a full stomach to pick a quarrel with me without cause. That’s a bad joke; you’re a nasty sly fellow.’ || /Azande_EvansPrittchard_1962_221_KS:p::/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1962_221_KS::In old days also a man’s blood-brother would try to warn him as he entered court that there was trouble brewing for him there and that he had better get away quickly. He did this by greeting his blood-brother in the joking manner of greeting that obtains between blood-brothers, that is, by shaking his hand, and in doing so pressing a finger into the centre of the hand or scraping it with a finger.",0.9,0.9,2,2,/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1933_390-392a_KS:p; /Azande_EvansPrittchard_1962_221_KS:p,429;442
f78353395be2,within_group,CentralAfrica,Azande,blood-brothers,blood-brothers,kin_role,kin_role,insulting_license,symmetric,licence to chaff,,"/Azande_Baxter_1953_17_KS:p::/Azande_Baxter_1953_17_KS::Evans-Pritchard, 16, 1933; blood-brothers have licence, which is not permitted to others, to chaff each other freely in this way.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/Azande_Baxter_1953_17_KS:p,420
ce1580fee254,within_group,CentralAfrica,Azande,blood-brothers,blood-brothers,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,buga,buga,"/Azande_Baxter_1953_76_KS:p::/Azande_Baxter_1953_76_KS::The behaviour which blood-brothers are expected to assume towards each other is the opposite of that required towards kin and in-laws, and they stand to each other in a sort of joking relationship. They abuse each other with impunity, saying things that would normally be considered causes of deep quarrels. || /Azande_EvansPrittchard_1962_214_KS:p::/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1962_214_KS::He continues in the language of the joking relationship—what Azande call buga—between blood-brothers: ‘my witchcraft is so active inside me that I might have killed you (I was so longing for a drink of beer); now the rubbing-board oracle would not accuse me (of witchcraft)’. || /Azande_EvansPrittchard_1962_223_KS:p::/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1962_223_KS::With certain persons, particularly with blood-brothers, it is permissible to use a joking language, called buga, in which things may be said which would not be proper if said to other persons.",0.917,0.95,3,3,/Azande_Baxter_1953_76_KS:p; /Azande_EvansPrittchard_1962_214_KS:p; /Azande_EvansPrittchard_1962_223_KS:p,421;439;445
fe670929003e,within_group,CentralAfrica,Azande,chief,his old Zande deputies,group,group,joking_relationship,symmetric,,bakumba,"/Azande_Seligman_1932_503_KS:p::/Azande_Seligman_1932_503_KS::There is no familiarity between a paramount chief and his sons and younger brothers, who fear their father greatly and are subjected to severe discipline. ... Between a chief and his deputies there is not the same strain; a chief is often seen joking with his old Zande deputies,",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Azande_Seligman_1932_503_KS:p,451
6b0f7a73bcd1,within_group,CentralAfrica,Azande,commoner,prince,group,person,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,sanza,sanza,"/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1962_223_KS:p::/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1962_223_KS::One uses it because subterfuge is advisable—it is not merely that one wants to score a point and to hurt, one also dares not say openly what one wants to say because of the relationship involved, the wife to husband relationship, that of commoner to prince, ...",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1962_223_KS:p,447
25b5a22e10d5,within_group,CentralAfrica,Azande,friend,friend,group,group,joking_relationship,symmetric,eye-signaling teasing (sanza),sanza,"/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1962_221_KS:p::/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1962_221_KS::Another wants to make malicious fun of him, so he catches the eye of a friend and by moving his own head round he directs it to the exposure.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1962_221_KS:p,443
9a1c5fb10406,within_group,CentralAfrica,Azande,husband,wife,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,spousal teasing via sanza,sanza,"/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1962_221_KS:p::/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1962_221_KS::A man says about his wife, whom he dislikes, in the presence of other women ‘this stupid dog, I keep it only because of my cough'. ... The inner meaning of the words is ‘this stupid woman [his wife], I keep her only because she hoes my gardens'. Were the wife to understand this she would make a row, but should she do so then or later, someone in the meanwhile having explained it to her, her husband would feign innocence, saying that he was speaking only of his dog and meant no more than he said.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1962_221_KS:p,444
6e062372fe75,within_group,CentralAfrica,Azande,man,his in-laws,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,sanza,sanza,"/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1962_223_KS:p::/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1962_223_KS::One uses it because subterfuge is advisable—it is not merely that one wants to score a point and to hurt, one also dares not say openly what one wants to say because of the relationship involved, the wife to husband relationship, that of commoner to prince, that between blood-brothers, that of a man to his in-laws.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1962_223_KS:p,448
1c5aaebc6bc2,within_group,CentralAfrica,Azande,men who frequent court,men who frequent court,group,group,joking_relationship,symmetric,,sanza,"/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1962_223_KS:p::/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1962_223_KS::Men who frequent court are there to serve their own interests and in competition to win the favour of their prince, and they are well-known for their skill in getting in sharp, sly little digs at one another in the form of apparently innocuous jokes and ...",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1962_223_KS:p,449
4182cfc42e1d,within_group,CentralAfrica,Azande,old commoner favourites of a prince,a prince,group,group,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,sanza (harmless),sanza mbaro,"/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1962_218_KS:p::/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1962_218_KS::A harmless sanza, what Azande call a sanza mbaro, a sanza in fun, is the habit of old commoner favourites of a prince of placing their hands before their mouths when speaking, especially when beginning to speak to him.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1962_218_KS:p,441
5daaa2c325f0,within_group,CentralAfrica,Azande,some one with whom you are on joking terms,your relative,group,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,blood-jests,,"/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1933_390-392b_KS:p::/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1933_390-392b_KS::‘It is thus they say about jests of this kind: if some one with whom you are on joking terms comes with a long sad face to tell you some story about your relatives such as ‘Don’t you know that your relative has died?’ you should take care not to be caught. He is jesting about your relative saying that he is dead, and you must not be taken in. Blood-jests, they say, are awful lies.'",0.85,0.85,1,1,/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1933_390-392b_KS:p,431
bef903c7a247,within_group,CentralAfrica,Azande,that blood-brother of mine,this elder brother of his father,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,unclear,blood-jests,,"/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1933_390-392b_KS:p::/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1933_390-392b_KS::He passes on to see this elder brother of his father and says to him: ‘Father, it is you I have come to see because that fellow over there, that blood-brother of mine, said yesterday that you were dead. ’",0.7,0.7,1,1,/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1933_390-392b_KS:p,430
1093c0027fa1,within_group,CentralAfrica,Azande,wife,another man's wife,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,mixed,sanza,sanza,"/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1962_223_KS:p::/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1962_223_KS::For example, a wife says in sanza to another man’s wife in the presence of her husband ‘the renown of your home is greater than ours’. In fact, she means the opposite and is having a dig at the owner of the homestead who, she well knows, hears what she says.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1962_223_KS:p,450
bf08ae2e6863,within_group,CentralAfrica,Azande,wife,husband,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,sanza,sanza,"/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1962_223_KS:p::/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1962_223_KS::The gbigbita sanza, the bad sanza, is more common. One uses it because subterfuge is advisable—it is not merely that one wants to score a point and to hurt, one also dares not say openly what one wants to say because of the relationship involved, the wife to husband relationship, ...",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1962_223_KS:p,446
41b78f7b0d2f,within_group,CentralAfrica,Azande,witch-doctors,members of their audience,group,group,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,,makama,/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1937_376_KS:p::/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1937_376_KS::Witch-doctors hand it to members of their audience at seances to discover whether they are witches (see p. ). They hold the sheath and present the cone to the people they want to test. Those who fail to pull out the cone are suspected of witchcraft though the test is regarded lightly by every one and rather in the nature of a joke.,0.7,0.7,1,1,/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1937_376_KS:p,436
f8d6f827991d,within_group,CentralAfrica,Azande,witch-doctors,witch-doctors,caste,caste,joking_relationship,symmetric,collegial joking,,"/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1937_206_KS:p::/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1937_206_KS::They chat and joke among themselves about a variety of secular subjects, though the affairs of their profession are also sometimes discussed.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1937_206_KS:p,435
dc9b6044ef45,within_group,CentralAfrica,Azande,young nobles,witch-doctors,age_set,group,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,test (joke) on witch-doctors,,"/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1937_185_KS:p::/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1937_185_KS::Young nobles, and sometimes commoners of influence also, will test witch-doctors by playing a joke on them. Nobles often play this trick.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Azande_EvansPrittchard_1937_185_KS:p,434
9aeaced09713,within_group,CentralAfrica,Barundi,a married son,his mother,kin_role,kin_role,insulting_license,non_symmetric,mock and shame,,"/Barundi_Albert_1963_209_KS:p::/Barundi_Albert_1963_209_KS::From her married son, a woman expects presents. If he is too poor to give gifts, she will mock and shame him. If he arrives at her home empty-handed, if he comes back from a job in Uganda or the city without something for her, she will be enraged.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Barundi_Albert_1963_209_KS:p,483
7453a2e290f9,within_group,CentralAfrica,Barundi,a mother-in-law,her son-in-law,kin_role,kin_role,avoidance,symmetric,umufasoni (noble); no jokes and no quarrels,umufasoni,"/Barundi_Albert_1963_209_KS:p::/Barundi_Albert_1963_209_KS::A mother-in-law and her son-in-law, no matter what their caste, address each other as umufasoni, noble. Their relationship is one of highly formal respect, politeness of the highest degree, no jokes and no quarrels.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/Barundi_Albert_1963_209_KS:p,485
1f8bd98ab5bd,within_group,CentralAfrica,Barundi,grandparents,grandchildren,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,mixed,joke with,,"/Barundi_Albert_1963_209_KS:p::/Barundi_Albert_1963_209_KS::Grandparents joke with their grandchildren, spoil them, protect them against their parents' anger, and, if they are not believing Christians, they expect their grandchildren will in due course perform the ceremonies in honour of the imizimu, the spirits of dead grandparents.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/Barundi_Albert_1963_209_KS:p,484
ea3bad3b6efc,within_group,CentralAfrica,Mbuti,bachelors,hunting band members,age_set,group,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,buffoon (scapegoat) role,,"/Mbuti_Turnbull_1965_12_KS:p::/Mbuti_Turnbull_1965_12_KS::Being a bachelor made my integration into family life somewhat easier than it might have been otherwise, and my reciprocal duties were not heavy, mainly involving my working with other bachelors... I found myself pushed into another category that I subsequently discovered to be a perfectly traditional one for incompetent hunters and some others--that of buffoon. This was very convenient, as it meant that I was the recipient of threats, ridicule, jokes and gossip, all of significance, and being made a general scapegoat I was also the center of a number of disputes.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/Mbuti_Turnbull_1965_12_KS:p,849
7118284e017c,within_group,CentralAfrica,Mbuti,children,adults,age_set,age_set,insulting_license,non_symmetric,ridicule/public teasing by children,,"/Mbuti_Turnbull_1983_44_KS:p::/Mbuti_Turnbull_1983_44_KS::Already, in the bopi , the children become involved in disputes, and are sometimes instrumental in settling them by ridicule, for nothing hurts an adult more than being ridiculed publicly by children.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/Mbuti_Turnbull_1983_44_KS:p,852
59f707d93da6,within_group,CentralAfrica,MbutiTurnbull294KS,Mbuti,karé,ethnic group,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,,karé,"/MbutiTurnbull294KS:p::/MbutiTurnbull294KS::Again the Mbuti employ the joking relationship to their advantage. They invoke claims upon kpara, karé , and “kin” without the slightest compunction, but when the same claims are reversed, they either deny or ridicule them.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/MbutiTurnbull294KS:p,846
251e3aac8130,within_group,CentralAfrica,MbutiTurnbull294KS,Mbuti,kpara,ethnic group,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,,kpara,"/MbutiTurnbull294KS:p::/MbutiTurnbull294KS::Again the Mbuti employ the joking relationship to their advantage. They invoke claims upon kpara, karé , and “kin” without the slightest compunction, but when the same claims are reversed, they either deny or ridicule them.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/MbutiTurnbull294KS:p,845
71509744b341,within_group,CentralAfrica,Rwandans,Ego,"WiFa, WiMo, HuFa, HuMo (parents-in-law generation)",kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,Parents-In-Law And Children-In-Law,,"/Rwandans_Maquet_1961_56_KS:p::/Rwandans_Maquet_1961_56_KS::WiFa, WiMo, HuFa, HuMo were addressed by the term used for the grandparents. We know that Ego's conduct towards his grandparents was extremely familiar. He had to use the same term for in-laws and was not allowed to pronounce their name.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Rwandans_Maquet_1961_56_KS:p,957
5d23546d47a4,within_group,CentralAfrica,Rwandans,Ego,female maternal cross-cousins,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,great familiarity,,"/Rwandans_Maquet_1961_49_KS:p::/Rwandans_Maquet_1961_49_KS::As has just been indicated, Ego could enjoy with his female maternal cross-cousins great familiarity expressed in practical jokes, pleasant talk, and erotic games.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Rwandans_Maquet_1961_49_KS:p,956
1a35f19cc13a,within_group,CentralAfrica,Rwandans,Ego,the daughters of his paternal uncle,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Rwandans_Maquet_1961_45_KS:p::/Rwandans_Maquet_1961_45_KS::Ego considered the daughters of his paternal uncle as sisters with whom there was a marriage prohibition. There was consequently a certain reserve between them which, however, did not prevent them from exchanging obscene jokes at a celebration or a gathering,",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Rwandans_Maquet_1961_45_KS:p,955
994d1752cf6b,within_group,CentralAfrica,Rwandans,Ego,wife's sisters and parallel cousins (assimilated to his wife / privileged partners),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,privileged sex partnership,,/Rwandans_Maquet_1961_56_KS:p::/Rwandans_Maquet_1961_56_KS::Ego's conduct towards his wife's relatives of the same generation depended mainly on the privileged sex partnership. The determination of those who could be partners was conditioned by the simple principle which assimilates a person to his siblings and parallel cousins.,0.9,0.9,1,1,/Rwandans_Maquet_1961_56_KS:p,958
8b8c7724027a,within_group,CentralAfrica,Rwandans,Ishaza,Mbiriri,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationship,,"/Rwandans_Newbury_1980_395_KS:p::/Rwandans_Newbury_1980_395_KS::Furthermore, the Ishaza have a joking relationship with the Mbiriri which differentiates them and maintains the differences between them.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Rwandans_Newbury_1980_395_KS:p,959
3fb4dc928302,within_group,CentralAfrica,Rwandans,"appointed men of letters, storytellers, bards, genealogists, and historians","habitual frequenters of the court, lords, courtiers, and employees",group,group,banter,symmetric,,,"/Rwandans_Pages_1933_179_KS:p::/Rwandans_Pages_1933_179_KS::The appointed men of letters, storytellers, bards, genealogists, and historians carefully watch their diction. Among them conversation is never dull, for jokes and witticisms abound.",0.7,0.7,1,1,/Rwandans_Pages_1933_179_KS:p,960
cd058370997e,within_group,CentralAfrica,Rwandans,grandfather,granddaughters,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,great familiarity and joking,,/Rwandans_Maquet_1961_44_KS:p::/Rwandans_Maquet_1961_44_KS::The grandfather called his granddaughters his little wives and his grandsons his love rivals. The reason given for this familiarity is that it helped to cheer up the old people and to train the young ones in socially enjoyable talk.,0.9,0.9,1,1,/Rwandans_Maquet_1961_44_KS:p,952
c2134bf88ae1,within_group,CentralAfrica,Rwandans,grandfather,grandsons,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,great familiarity and joking,,/Rwandans_Maquet_1961_44_KS:p::/Rwandans_Maquet_1961_44_KS::The grandfather called his granddaughters his little wives and his grandsons his love rivals. The reason given for this familiarity is that it helped to cheer up the old people and to train the young ones in socially enjoyable talk.,0.9,0.9,1,1,/Rwandans_Maquet_1961_44_KS:p,953
2bf8eeccfd47,within_group,CentralAfrica,Rwandans,"habitual frequenters of the court, lords, courtiers, and employees",mountain dwellers and those who inhabit the frontiers of the realm,group,regional group,teasing,non_symmetric,,,"/Rwandans_Pages_1933_179_KS:p::/Rwandans_Pages_1933_179_KS::Considering the good taste that distinguishes the familiars of the king and the inhabitants of the central province of Nduga, it is not surprising that they should be shocked and amused by the lingo of the mountain dwellers and those who inhabit the frontiers of the realm.",0.6,0.6,1,1,/Rwandans_Pages_1933_179_KS:p,961
0122db58d321,within_group,CentralAfrica,Rwandans,sisters,brothers,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Rwandans_Maquet_1961_45_KS:p::/Rwandans_Maquet_1961_45_KS::Sisters and brothers were playmates during their childhood. After puberty their relations were restricted, but certainly not to the point of avoidance.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Rwandans_Maquet_1961_45_KS:p,954
4f9c494854d5,within_group,CentralAfrica,Rwandans,young Tutsi,companions of the young Tutsi as well as older people,age_set,group,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,,,"/Rwandans_Maquet_1961_118_KS:p::/Rwandans_Maquet_1961_118_KS::By jeers and scoffing, companions of the young Tutsi as well as older people, tried to make him angry and if he reacted emotionally, he was punished by further jokes. So finally he was able to remain apparently quiet and polite in any circumstances which would have elicited more violent reactions from people not thus trained.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Rwandans_Maquet_1961_118_KS:p,951
b3b249ffcf46,within_group,CentralAfrica,Suku,Ego,his grandfather,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking,,"/Suku_Kopytoff_1964_107_KS:p::/Suku_Kopytoff_1964_107_KS::Thus, the relationship between Ego and his grandfather is a joking one (as it is with any true or classificatory grandparent), but in this case it is entirely devoid of any economic, legal, or ritual functions, this grandfather being a member neither of Ego's matrilineage nor of his kitaata.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Suku_Kopytoff_1964_107_KS:p,979
dc24fd6bfa41,within_group,CentralAfrica,Suku,patrilateral cross-cousin,ego,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,jokes,,"/Suku_Kopytoff_1971_86_KS:p::/Suku_Kopytoff_1971_86_KS::a person can shift from the slot of patrilateral cross-cousin (with whom one jokes, and whom, if female, one can marry) to the slot of “father” or “father's sister” (whereupon one stops joking and starts respecting and marriage becomes forbidden).",0.95,0.95,1,1,/Suku_Kopytoff_1971_86_KS:p,983
7d28a7638e1a,within_group,CentralAfrica,Suku,the boys,anyone using their old names,age_set,group,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,,,/Suku_Kopytoff_1965_456_KS:p::/Suku_Kopytoff_1965_456_KS::They have now acquired new names and may collect a fine from anyone using their old ones.,0.85,0.85,1,1,/Suku_Kopytoff_1965_456_KS:p,982
97fa642db40e,within_group,CentralAfrica,Suku,the boys,their parents,age_set,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,,,/Suku_Kopytoff_1965_456_KS:p::/Suku_Kopytoff_1965_456_KS::Some of this hazing may border on physical cruelty; much of it consists in forcing the boys into admitting imaginary incestuous relations and making sexual jokes about their parents—behavior that would be unthinkable under normal circumstances.,0.9,0.9,1,1,/Suku_Kopytoff_1965_456_KS:p,980
5a4ebe604476,within_group,CentralAfrica,Suku,the boys,women,age_set,group,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,,,"/Suku_Kopytoff_1965_456_KS:p::/Suku_Kopytoff_1965_456_KS::During their residence, they learn various dances and songs (most of which are derisive of women) and they are exposed to systematic hazing by their elders.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Suku_Kopytoff_1965_456_KS:p,981
e89c229dad43,within_group,CentralAfrica,Teda,men,women,group,group,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking during wedding / conjugal banter,,"/Teda_LeCoeur_1950_6_KS:p::/Teda_LeCoeur_1950_6_KS::They exchange jokes, the women reproach the man for his violence, the men reproach the woman for having resisted. ... With the grain that is then gathered, the young wife, assisted by her friends, cooks the first conjugal dish. The father of the wife generally gives his son-in-law a gift called: cenofor, equal or superior to those that he had received from him. The marriage ceremonies have ended. But during the weeks that follow, the two spouses still receive many gifts called kobori which, for the young woman, consist chiefly of household objects given her by the other women of the two families. The couple build their hut near that of the woman's mother, and the husband generally waits one or two years, or even until the birth of his first child, to pay the luli, which permits him, if he wishes, to install his household near his own mother.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/Teda_LeCoeur_1950_6_KS:p,1026
e8c43bea6af5,within_group,EasternAfrica,Amhara,Aläqa Gäbre-Hanna,Emperor Menelik,person,person,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,licensed teasing / privileged familiarity,,"/Amhara_Messing_1956_69_KS:p::/Amhara_Messing_1956_69_KS::No one else would have dared such a remark, but he was popular with Emperor Menelik and especially with the Empress Taitu, and could tell them certain frank truths and tease them with irony in a semi-polite manner.",0.7,0.7,1,1,/Amhara_Messing_1956_69_KS:p,407
2cce098de6c9,within_group,EasternAfrica,Amhara,Aläqa Gäbre-Hanna,Empress Taitu,person,person,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,licensed teasing / privileged familiarity,,"/Amhara_Messing_1956_69_KS:p::/Amhara_Messing_1956_69_KS::No one else would have dared such a remark, but he was popular with Emperor Menelik and especially with the Empress Taitu, and could tell them certain frank truths and tease them with irony in a semi-polite manner.",0.7,0.7,1,1,/Amhara_Messing_1956_69_KS:p,408
ffb87f0ed536,within_group,EasternAfrica,Amhara,elder brothers,younger brothers,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationship,,"/Amhara_Messing_1985_195_KS:p::/Amhara_Messing_1985_195_KS::Elder brothers may and do discipline their juniors, despite the superficial “joking relationship” between siblings of the same sex.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Amhara_Messing_1985_195_KS:p,409
fdd09c61c263,within_group,EasternAfrica,Amhara,inferiors,superiors,group,group,joking_relationship,mixed,joking relationship,,"/Amhara_Levine_1964_255_KS:p::/Amhara_Levine_1964_255_KS::The egoistic element in the hierarchical pattern is further modified by a sort of joking relationship that sometimes develops between inferiors and superiors. The harsh interrogation of inferiors at times shades into a sort of wry banter; sons or servants may be called “thief” or “scoundrel” in an undertone of unmistakable affection. At times, moreover, the direction of the jesting may be reversed. Amhara servants are noted for playing practical jokes on their masters, which the latter usually appreciate when the jests are clever.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/Amhara_Levine_1964_255_KS:p,404
a9d115b9c328,within_group,EasternAfrica,Amhara,little girls,boys,group,group,joking_relationship,symmetric,waza,waza,"/Amhara_Messing_1985_263_KS:p::/Amhara_Messing_1985_263_KS::Sometimes somewhat older girls meet boys with whom they have established an experimental “joking relationship” ( waza ), and play house, building little huts out in the fields during the dry season, within the hamlet in the rainy season.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Amhara_Messing_1985_263_KS:p,410
8207d2320750,within_group,EasternAfrica,Amhara,older boys,boys of eight or nine who aspire to deaconhood,group,age_set,teasing,non_symmetric,mock-examination (hazing),,"/Amhara_Messing_1985_263_KS:p::/Amhara_Messing_1985_263_KS::Boys of eight or nine who aspire to deaconhood at the next New Year holy days are mock-examined by older boys who have achieved that status. The candidate must carry each of them several times around the churchyard, and must recite and chant liturgical prayers for them for hours.",0.7,0.7,1,1,/Amhara_Messing_1985_263_KS:p,417
71e6936bd4a2,within_group,EasternAfrica,Amhara,servants,masters,group,group,joking_relationship,mixed,practical jokes / jesting,,"/Amhara_Levine_1965_255_KS:p::/Amhara_Levine_1965_255_KS::At times, moreover, the direction of the jesting may be reversed. Amhara servants are noted for playing practical jokes on their masters, which the latter usually appreciate when the jests are clever.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/Amhara_Levine_1965_255_KS:p,406
96c7053a6276,within_group,EasternAfrica,Amhara,sons,superiors,kin_role,group,joking_relationship,mixed,joking relationship,,/Amhara_Levine_1965_255_KS:p::/Amhara_Levine_1965_255_KS::The egoistic element in the hierarchical pattern is further modified by a sort of joking relationship that sometimes develops between inferiors and superiors. The harsh interrogation of inferiors at times shades into a sort of wry banter; sons or servants may be called “thief” or “scoundrel” in an undertone of unmistakable affection.,0.9,0.9,1,1,/Amhara_Levine_1965_255_KS:p,405
04ea13a43e74,within_group,EasternAfrica,Amhara,zar society,sibling devotees,group,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,sibling jokes,,"/Amhara_Messing_1985_323_KS:p::/Amhara_Messing_1985_323_KS::In either case they enjoy recreation and amusement with the “sibling” devotees, with whom they can gossip and enact sibling jokes, such as playfully accusing each other of minor oversights in the service of the zar (it often takes this form).",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Amhara_Messing_1985_323_KS:p,419
0971766f1af0,within_group,EasternAfrica,Bagisu,maternal grandparents,grandchildren,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Bagisu_LaFontaine_1959_28_KS:p::/Bagisu_LaFontaine_1959_28_KS::With the maternal grandparents there is a form of joking which is common: they use the terms for sibling of the same sex and spouse to one another and may demand small gifts in an abrupt, rough manner.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Bagisu_LaFontaine_1959_28_KS:p,454
f96b97fa84a9,within_group,EasternAfrica,Bagisu,members of different lineages,members of different lineages,lineage,lineage,joking_relationship,symmetric,bukulo / bakulo,bukulo,"/Bagisu_Heald_1989_163_KS:p::/Bagisu_Heald_1989_163_KS::In the 1960s, bukulo was a hereditary joking relationship which held between members of different lineages and linked members as joking partners (bakulo) to each other.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Bagisu_Heald_1989_163_KS:p,452
d368ff1ecd14,within_group,EasternAfrica,Bagisu,minor lineage,minor lineage,lineage,lineage,joking_relationship,symmetric,bagulo,bagulo,/Bagisu_LaFontaine_1959_26_KS:p::/Bagisu_LaFontaine_1959_26_KS::A special tie is that known as bagulo which involves two lineages in a joking relationship. Members of lineages who are bagulo joke with one another and may seize one another's property with impunity.,0.9,0.9,1,1,/Bagisu_LaFontaine_1959_26_KS:p,453
437430a0c72a,within_group,EasternAfrica,Bagisu,paternal grandmother,grandchildren,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,/Bagisu_LaFontaine_1959_28_KS:p::/Bagisu_LaFontaine_1959_28_KS::This joking may also be indulged with the paternal grandmother; it is rarely used to the paternal grandfather.,0.85,0.85,1,1,/Bagisu_LaFontaine_1959_28_KS:p,455
054518d5c72b,within_group,EasternAfrica,Banyoro,a man,his brother's wife (baramu / baramukati),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,baramu / baramukati,baramukati,"/Banyoro_Beattie_1958_16_KS:p::/Banyoro_Beattie_1958_16_KS::A man calls his wife's sisters, and his brothers' wives, baramu (sing. muramu), or baramukati (i.e. baramu women). ... Thus a man thinks of his relationship with his female baramu as intimate and friendly; they are ‘like wives’. He can talk and joke familiarly with them.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Banyoro_Beattie_1958_16_KS:p,474
b642c6726076,within_group,EasternAfrica,Banyoro,a man,his wife's brother (baramu / also baisezara in some contexts),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,mixed,baramu (male),baramu,"/Banyoro_Beattie_1958_16_KS:p::/Banyoro_Beattie_1958_16_KS::He also uses the term baramu for his wife's brothers, and his sisters' husbands. ... But a man's male muramu is at the same time either muko to the agnatic group to which that man belongs, or else he is one of the group of that man's baisezara. In either case he must be treated with something of the politeness and ceremony ...",0.85,0.85,1,1,/Banyoro_Beattie_1958_16_KS:p,475
1d751aafd5ee,within_group,EasternAfrica,Banyoro,a man,his wife's sister (muramu / baramu),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,baramu / muramu,muramu,"/Banyoro_Beattie_1958_16_KS:p::/Banyoro_Beattie_1958_16_KS::A man calls his wife's sisters, and his brothers' wives, baramu (sing. muramu), or baramukati (i.e. baramu women). ... Thus a man thinks of his relationship with his female baramu as intimate and friendly; they are ‘like wives’. He can talk and joke familiarly with them.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Banyoro_Beattie_1958_16_KS:p,473
f3c1f3db5128,within_group,EasternAfrica,Banyoro,banyinarumi,baihwa,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,mixed,,banyinarumi / baihwa,"/Banyoro_Beattie_1958_332_KS:p::/Banyoro_Beattie_1958_332_KS::This affectionate intimacy also extends to the mother's brothers, banyinarumi.... A sister's son may take certain liberties in his nyinarumi's house; he may help himself uninvited to food, and may take or borrow almost everything he wants. He may joke familiarly with his mother's brother's wives, though he may not sleep with them.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Banyoro_Beattie_1958_332_KS:p,478
b1d687719269,within_group,EasternAfrica,Banyoro,banyinarumi's wives,mwana / mwihwa,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,,mwana / mwihwa,"/Banyoro_Beattie_1958_332_KS:p::/Banyoro_Beattie_1958_332_KS::A sister's son may take certain liberties in his nyinarumi's house; he may help himself uninvited to food, and may take or borrow almost everything he wants. He may joke familiarly with his mother's brother's wives, though he may not sleep with them.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Banyoro_Beattie_1958_332_KS:p,479
77799eb82808,within_group,EasternAfrica,Banyoro,bwihwa relationship (man/nephew),group of his mother's agnates,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,mixed,bwihwa,bwihwa,"/Banyoro_Beattie_1958_18_KS:p::/Banyoro_Beattie_1958_18_KS::There is, then, a manifest ambivalence in the bwihwa relationship. For in it are focused a man's relations with the whole group of his mother's agnates...and to this group he is at one and the same time a child whom they have borne, and an ‘outsider’.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Banyoro_Beattie_1958_18_KS:p,476
d9e5696a1cb7,within_group,EasternAfrica,Banyoro,grandchild,grandfather's wives,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Banyoro_Beattie_1958_338_KS:p::/Banyoro_Beattie_1958_338_KS::Banyoro sometimes say that a grandfather is ‘like a brother’. Consistently with this, a youth may jokingly call his grandfather's wives his wives. This terminological usage need not always have a primarily sexual reference (though it does sometimes, e.g. in the case of a brother's wives).",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Banyoro_Beattie_1958_338_KS:p,481
61f535a6b753,within_group,EasternAfrica,Banyoro,grandparent,grandchild,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Banyoro_Beattie_1958_338_KS:p::/Banyoro_Beattie_1958_338_KS::The relations between grandparents and grandchildren are friendly and intimate, contrasting strongly with the father-child relationship. It is permissible for a grandchild to touch his grandfather's hair or beard, which he may not do in the case of his father. A boy may joke familiarly with his grandparents, and they are said to spoil and over-feed their grandchildren and treat them indulgently.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/Banyoro_Beattie_1958_338_KS:p,480
d4ccbe7b0abe,within_group,EasternAfrica,Banyoro,grandparents,grandchildren,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,friendly/intimate joking,,"/Banyoro_Beattie_1960_55_KS:p::/Banyoro_Beattie_1960_55_KS::The relations between grandparents and grandchildren are friendly and intimate, contrasting strongly with those between fathers and sons. A boy may joke and play with his grandfather in a way which would not be permissible with his father, and Nyoro grandparents, like their Western counterparts, are often said to “spoil” their grandchildren.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Banyoro_Beattie_1960_55_KS:p,482
260c3067e116,within_group,EasternAfrica,Banyoro,muko,isezara,kin_role,kin_role,insulting_license,non_symmetric,rules / inequality of relationship,muko; isezara,"/Banyoro_Beattie_1958_15_KS:p::/Banyoro_Beattie_1958_15_KS::He may not address his wife's father by his personal name (though his wife's father may so address him); he must address him by terms expressing their different statuses, such as waitu, munyoro, or mukama wange (‘sir’, ‘lord’, or ‘my master’), or by his mpako or praise-name.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Banyoro_Beattie_1958_15_KS:p,472
83ff6bb74baa,within_group,EasternAfrica,Banyoro,mwihwa,nyinarumi's wife,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,privileged familiarity,,/Banyoro_Beattie_1958_20_KS:p::/Banyoro_Beattie_1958_20_KS::He may not 'climb on to nyinarumi's bed' or sleep with his wife (though he may joke familiarly with her).,0.9,0.9,1,1,/Banyoro_Beattie_1958_20_KS:p,477
64b5c42e5edb,within_group,EasternAfrica,Bena,cross-cousin,sibling in-law,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,freedom of expression / lack of restraint,,"/Bena_Swartz_1968_42_KS:p::/Bena_Swartz_1968_42_KS::Thus, a person with whom one is on good terms and who is thought to be a relative who is about one's own age and general status will be addressed by cross-cousin or sibling in-law terms implying freedom of expression, lack of restraint concerning joking and matters of bodily functions (i.e. sex and elimination can be freely and frankly discussed).",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Bena_Swartz_1968_42_KS:p,490
98e4d84477c0,within_group,EasternAfrica,Bena,father,son,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,,,"/Bena_Culwick_1935_199_KS:p::/Bena_Culwick_1935_199_KS::Again, a half-grown youth addressed (half jokingly, it must be admitted) a young man only a few years his senior as “father,” because the latter had taken to wife the young sister of the former's mother. But joke or no joke, the “father” had to feed his “son” when called upon by him to do so, when they were both travelling with the writers.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Bena_Culwick_1935_199_KS:p,488
4d3c24dabcd9,within_group,EasternAfrica,Bena,grandparent,grandchild,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,comradeship / freedom from restraint,,/Bena_Swartz_1968_42_KS:p::/Bena_Swartz_1968_42_KS::A much older putative relative will be the object of grandparental terms again implying comradeship and freedom from restraint.,0.85,0.85,1,1,/Bena_Swartz_1968_42_KS:p,491
f2cd0ada9411,within_group,EasternAfrica,Bena,mama (elder brother),yaya (maternal uncle),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,unclear,,,"/Bena_Culwick_1935_199_KS:p::/Bena_Culwick_1935_199_KS::Towegale inherited his seventh wife, Samweti binti Mbago ( ), from a man whom he called his mama (elder brother), but who was really an absurdly young yaya (maternal uncle), for Towegale's mother also called him brother.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/Bena_Culwick_1935_199_KS:p,486
22a2f0f00431,within_group,EasternAfrica,Bena,maternal uncle,cross-cousin,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,unclear,,,"/Bena_Culwick_1935_199_KS:p::/Bena_Culwick_1935_199_KS::Similarly, Ndaliwali was called Binti Bilali's (39) maternal uncle because she was of suitable age to marry his son Mtengera, but really Ndaliwali and she were cross-cousins of the same generation.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Bena_Culwick_1935_199_KS:p,487
a38986984b93,within_group,EasternAfrica,Bena,mother's brother,boys,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,mixed,best friend and counsellor,,"/Bena_Culwick_1935_337_KS:p::/Bena_Culwick_1935_337_KS::The male relative whom the Wabena regard with most affection is, then, not their father but their mother's brother, on whom the boys in particular look as their best friend and counsellor.  ...  This is the more noticeable in that the Mbena is a great tease among those with whom he is thoroughly at his ease.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/Bena_Culwick_1935_337_KS:p,489
f4616729afe1,within_group,EasternAfrica,Bena,vatani,vatani,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,vatani,"/Bena_Swartz_1968_44_KS:p::/Bena_Swartz_1968_44_KS::Conversely, anyone classed with the vatani can be treated more freely and although the rather strong pressure for joking behavior noted for other groups toward people in this category (see footnote 5) is absent, there is sometimes horseplay and the exchange of mild teasing and ‘friendly’ insults among vatani.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Bena_Swartz_1968_44_KS:p,492
53e52182f32f,within_group,EasternAfrica,Chagga,boys,an unpopular boy,group,kin_role,teasing,non_symmetric,,,"/Chagga_Raum_1940_279_KS:p::/Chagga_Raum_1940_279_KS::Boys usually carry a few in the folds of their garments. They are sometimes thrown in fun at a child looking in another direction. Occasionally such an attack develops into a concerted action, punishing a boy for his unpopularity, his boasts, his unfair methods of play, and his betrayal of pasture secrets.",0.6,0.6,1,1,/Chagga_Raum_1940_279_KS:p,502
1aca34b7f151,within_group,EasternAfrica,Chagga,bride,younger sister,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,,,/Chagga_Guttman_1926_80_KS:p::/Chagga_Guttman_1926_80_KS::It is customary that the bride ask her younger sister to remove some of the beer surreptitiously from the first barrel while the men come and go to fill the barrel gradually. ... This joke is a token of her love.,0.8,0.8,1,1,/Chagga_Guttman_1926_80_KS:p,499
c584f8564773,within_group,EasternAfrica,Chagga,members of an age-group (young men),girls of his own age,age_set,age_set,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,joking relationship,,"/Chagga_Raum_1940_348_KS:p::/Chagga_Raum_1940_348_KS::Like the Nuer, the Chaga stands in a joking relationship with girls of his own age, but for the latter this does not preclude marriage, whereas for the former it does.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Chagga_Raum_1940_348_KS:p,505
e889301c90b9,within_group,EasternAfrica,Chagga,novices of the play-group,older boys / experienced boys,age_set,age_set,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,introductory training / practical jokes,,"/Chagga_Raum_1940_264_KS:p::/Chagga_Raum_1940_264_KS::The novices of the play-group have to undergo a severe introductory training in the form of practical jokes. ... A species of bird called kilepuru, they are told by the experienced boys, can be caught if approached with closed eyes. The novice gropes about for one and after some fumbling opens his eyes. The birds are gone and he is mocked: ‘Why on earth did you open your eyes!’",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Chagga_Raum_1940_264_KS:p,500
5861063a9a8e,within_group,EasternAfrica,Chagga,nurses,the girl that wriggles,kin_role,kin_role,teasing,non_symmetric,,,"/Chagga_Raum_1940_279_KS:p::/Chagga_Raum_1940_279_KS::When children discover ants marching along, the nurses among them are ordered to put a finger into the column. The girl that wriggles about uneasily is bitten, while the experienced girl keeps still and is not attacked. The former is laughed at for having eaten the baby's food.",0.7,0.7,1,1,/Chagga_Raum_1940_279_KS:p,501
0c0ed07bbed9,within_group,EasternAfrica,Chagga,two elders (representing opposing parties),two elders (representing opposing parties),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,Lupariane mfo (ceremonial scolding/toy fighting),Lupariane mfo,"/Chagga_Guttman_1926_143_KS:p::/Chagga_Guttman_1926_143_KS::The two elders, with the ditch between them, begin to scold each other violently and to incite each other into a fighting spirit. ""Lumae sonu, via kindo kja sonu, kuiho-se msiki lurentse kifa kjakefo — Let us eliminate the disgrace. Say something disgraceful. There is no sib sister anymore on whose account we would have to be bashful."" The other elder follows up with a dirty joke.",0.76,0.76,1,1,/Chagga_Guttman_1926_143_KS:p,496
cb937ba290c5,within_group,EasternAfrica,Chagga,young men,girls of one's own age,age_set,age_set,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationship with young women,,"/Chagga_Raum_1940_330_KS:p::/Chagga_Raum_1940_330_KS::Manly conduct demands detachment from the female sex and the younger age-groups. For this reason, young men should avoid jesting with their juniors or womenfolk. The only exceptions are the wife of the mother's brother and girls of one's own age, who have been taught not to revile men.

The implications are deeper. The joking relationship with young women facilitates the contact of potential marriage partners.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Chagga_Raum_1940_330_KS:p,503
7ff544b11cfb,within_group,EasternAfrica,Chagga,young men,wife of the mother's brother,age_set,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationship,,"/Chagga_Raum_1940_330_KS:p::/Chagga_Raum_1940_330_KS::The only exceptions are the wife of the mother's brother and girls of one's own age, who have been taught not to revile men.

The similar relationship with a member of the parental generation, the mother's brother's wife, is a mechanism to counteract the awe of her husband which has been inculcated.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/Chagga_Raum_1940_330_KS:p,504
42fef0d55b97,within_group,EasternAfrica,Chagga,young men,young men,age_set,age_set,joking_relationship,symmetric,momo (joking form of address),momo,"/Chagga_Guttman_1926_305_KS:p::/Chagga_Guttman_1926_305_KS::At the present time, a new designation for the members of the same age grade makes its appearance in the Kibosho district: momo, which in the Kibosho dialect means heifer. It started as a joking form of address among young men, and is now the fashionable word of Kibosho.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Chagga_Guttman_1926_305_KS:p,497
c7f1f5a6003b,within_group,EasternAfrica,Ganda,the peasants,the chiefs,group,group,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,joke-telling to chiefs,,"/Ganda_Mair_1934_163_KS:p::/Ganda_Mair_1934_163_KS::In the old days, the peasants would visit the chiefs and use all sorts of humorous phrases, thus pleasing them greatly. They made feasts for them and gave them gifts.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/Ganda_Mair_1934_163_KS:p,550
31b409124c7b,within_group,EasternAfrica,Gikuyu,agemates,other mariika,age_set,group,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,,,"/Gikuyu_Davison_1996_91_KS:p::/Gikuyu_Davison_1996_91_KS::With agemates, you can joke about things like that—where you spent the night and who was there. But with those of other mariika, you had nothing to talk about.",0.7,0.7,1,1,/Gikuyu_Davison_1996_91_KS:p,558
9917a4ae6074,within_group,EasternAfrica,Gikuyu,children of both sisters,children of both sisters,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,cousin play / sibling-like behavior,moro; mware we tata,"/Gikuyu_Kenyatta_1953_16_KS:p::/Gikuyu_Kenyatta_1953_16_KS::Children like to visit or to be visited by their aunt, and whenever such a visit is made it is always an occasion of rejoicing and feasting. The children of both sisters address one another as moro or mware we tata (son or daughter of my or our aunt), the behaviour pattern between them is almost like brothers and sisters.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/Gikuyu_Kenyatta_1953_16_KS:p,563
a0a3b03a4ea1,within_group,EasternAfrica,Gikuyu,circumcised girls,uncircumcised girls,age_set,age_set,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,,,"/Gikuyu_Davison_1996_173_KS:p::/Gikuyu_Davison_1996_173_KS::One became mature. It was very important in those days, because when one got circumcised, one would not joke about with uncircumcised girls and they would give us respect.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Gikuyu_Davison_1996_173_KS:p,553
a8cfe2276ba0,within_group,EasternAfrica,Gikuyu,circumcised young women,uncircumcised young men,age_set,age_set,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Gikuyu_Davison_1996_173_KS:p::/Gikuyu_Davison_1996_173_KS::Once circumcised, a young woman could talk and joke with young, uncircumcised men, but we did not move with them sexually until one got married.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/Gikuyu_Davison_1996_173_KS:p,554
90c20c9fb7ff,within_group,EasternAfrica,Gikuyu,girls,young uncircumcised boys,group,age_set,joking_relationship,unclear,joke around,,"/Gikuyu_Davison_1996_120_KS:p::/Gikuyu_Davison_1996_120_KS::Girls continue to joke around with younger boys even when they are grown. ... And now, young uncircumcised boys are being called “men” by girls, so they feel grown-up and can joke about with the girls.",0.7,0.7,1,1,/Gikuyu_Davison_1996_120_KS:p,552
135497ee4c35,within_group,EasternAfrica,Gikuyu,girls who went for Irua,girls who went for Irua,age_set,age_set,joking_relationship,symmetric,,Irua,"/Gikuyu_Davison_1996_173_KS:p::/Gikuyu_Davison_1996_173_KS::And another thing, there are no mariika today. The girls who went for Irua stayed friends and would talk until dark, asking each other questions… about the day and joking with each other.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Gikuyu_Davison_1996_173_KS:p,555
2ee2241074f6,within_group,EasternAfrica,Gikuyu,grandparents,grandchildren,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationship; play and joking,,"/Gikuyu_Kenyatta_1953_16_KS:p::/Gikuyu_Kenyatta_1953_16_KS::When the grandparents are not living near the homesteads of their daughters or sons, the children pay frequent visits to their grandparents and stay with them for some time. Sometimes it becomes difficult to get the children to return to their parents homesteads, for they feel more free in playing and joking with their grandparents than they would with their own parents. || /Gikuyu_Middleton_1953_25_KS:p::/Gikuyu_Middleton_1953_25_KS::A child is named after one of its grandparents and “in religious ceremonies the children are treated
in the same manner as their grandparents.” A boy is called “my husband” by
his grandmother, and a girl “my co-wife”; the grandfather calls his grandson
“my equal” and his granddaughter “my bride.” It is implicit in Kenyatta's
account that this is a joking relationship.",0.925,0.95,2,2,/Gikuyu_Kenyatta_1953_16_KS:p; /Gikuyu_Middleton_1953_25_KS:p,561;564
2f48cd076a51,within_group,EasternAfrica,Gikuyu,members of the same mariika,members of the same mariika,age_set,age_set,joking_relationship,symmetric,playmates / licensed joking among same mariika,mariika,"/Gikuyu_Davison_1996_83_KS:p::/Gikuyu_Davison_1996_83_KS::People of different mariika would not abuse each other or even greet each other. But you, if you were of my same age group, when we met we would greet one another and laugh and tell jokes or do anything we wanted together.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Gikuyu_Davison_1996_83_KS:p,556
579b9f41020d,within_group,EasternAfrica,Gikuyu,mother's sister (tata),children of her sister (nieces and nephews),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,avuncular indulgence / privileged familiarity,tata,"/Gikuyu_Kenyatta_1953_16_KS:p::/Gikuyu_Kenyatta_1953_16_KS::The mother's sister is called tata, the same as the father's sister, but the relation between her and the children of her sister is entirely different. She is looked upon by the children in the same way as their real mother. The affection and indulgence that she gives to the children and the sympathetic attitude towards them is even greater than that the children can expect from their own mother.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Gikuyu_Kenyatta_1953_16_KS:p,562
9f5cd1cd191c,within_group,EasternAfrica,Gikuyu,people who know,young man dancing with his cousin,group,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Gikuyu_Kenyatta_1953_160_KS:p::/Gikuyu_Kenyatta_1953_160_KS::During festival dances, where people from various districts meet and dance together, it is not always possible to recognise a relative who comes from far away, and a man may sometimes be seen dancing with his cousin by mistake. When this happens, people who know will start laughing and joking to warn the young man of his error.",0.6,0.6,1,1,/Gikuyu_Kenyatta_1953_160_KS:p,560
692be1ceef09,within_group,EasternAfrica,Gikuyu,the ones who did not have their ears pierced,those who had their ears pierced,group,group,avoidance,symmetric,,,"/Gikuyu_Davison_1996_116_KS:p::/Gikuyu_Davison_1996_116_KS::The ones who did not have their ears pierced were like fools, because they were trying to cut themselves off from the community. We would not joke with them, nor could they joke about with us.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Gikuyu_Davison_1996_116_KS:p,551
0ac0750c3955,within_group,EasternAfrica,Gikuyu,those who had been circumcised,those who had not been circumcised,age_set,age_set,joking_relationship,symmetric,circumcised vs uncircumcised teasing,karigũ; kahiĩ,"/Gikuyu_Davison_1996_90_KS:p::/Gikuyu_Davison_1996_90_KS::Wamutira: But nowadays, things are better because nobody calls another karigũ [silly uncircumcised girl] or kahiĩ [same applied to a boy], and so there are no fights between the circumcised and uncircumcised.

Muthoni: Now, you can't tell the difference between one who is circumcised and one who is not. Long ago, if you were of my age group and we met and my ears were pierced, you would never joke with me or abuse me until yours were pierced, too.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Gikuyu_Davison_1996_90_KS:p,557
f3d31e153f5c,within_group,EasternAfrica,Gikuyu,wife,husband's brother,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,nickname/reciprocal endearment,muthoni,"/Gikuyu_Middleton_1953_25_KS:p::/Gikuyu_Middleton_1953_25_KS::An affine is referred to as muthoni “which means one who is bashful or
polite”; the term applies to “all clan members” of families linked by marriage. Kinship Terminology
Muthoni is apparently used between all affines except that a husband's
brother is “given a nickname as a sign of endearment” by the wife, and he
reciprocates, and the26 husband addresses his wife's sister as maramu.",0.7,0.7,1,1,/Gikuyu_Middleton_1953_25_KS:p,565
4364dea08b80,within_group,EasternAfrica,Gikuyu,young men of his own age-group,young women of her age-group,age_set,age_set,joking_relationship,symmetric,,kohingwo; gocambio,"/Gikuyu_Kenyatta_1953_160_KS:p::/Gikuyu_Kenyatta_1953_160_KS::The fine for this is nine sheep or goats and three big, fat sheep (ndorome) as the kiama fees. Besides this, the man is made a social outcast or “sent to Covertry” (kohingwo) by all the young men and girls of his own age-group. ... She pays a fine by providing a feast to the men and the girls of her age-group. She is also liable to ridicule (kohingwo and gocambio).",0.7,0.7,1,1,/Gikuyu_Kenyatta_1953_160_KS:p,559
a1d91c0642cf,within_group,EasternAfrica,Gusii,'tamer',the novice (omoisia [uncircumcised boy]),person,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,,,"/Gusii_Mayer_1952_19_KS:p::/Gusii_Mayer_1952_19_KS::Now the ‘tamer’ catches the penis firmly in a leaf, the inside of which has been coated with a paste of leaf-ash and water. The ash is caustic; the resulting pain is ‘a really nasty one’, making the novice cry out in agony.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/Gusii_Mayer_1952_19_KS:p,590
796e8d31a7bd,within_group,EasternAfrica,Gusii,Grandparents,grandchildren,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,sexual joking / jovial teasing,,"/Gusii_Levine_1966_162_KS:p::/Gusii_Levine_1966_162_KS::Grandparents initiate sexual joking with young children, and insults, including sexual abuse, flow back and forth between them in a manner which is almost as relaxed as that of agemates. A child must never strike his grandparent, however, and he tends to obey the older person's imperious commands despite the surface egalitarianism. || /Gusii_Levine_1966_187_KS:p::/Gusii_Levine_1966_187_KS::The interaction between grandparents and grandchildren can also be described as playfully abusive. || /Gusii_Levine_1979_12_KS:p::/Gusii_Levine_1979_12_KS::After menopause, in her forties, she should have circumcised grandchildren, and her primary interests are focused on her son's and daughter's children, some of whom will live with her, visit her, joke with her.",0.867,0.9,3,3,/Gusii_Levine_1966_162_KS:p; /Gusii_Levine_1966_187_KS:p; /Gusii_Levine_1979_12_KS:p,572;575;582
aa21d1ab8d4e,within_group,EasternAfrica,Gusii,a Nyansongo man,his wife's brothers and sisters,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Gusii_Levine_1966_52_KS:p::/Gusii_Levine_1966_52_KS::In like manner, a man has an egalitarian, joking relationship with his wife's brothers and sisters; with the latter, elaborate sexual joking is carried on.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/Gusii_Levine_1966_52_KS:p,579
8bee7c4de5b9,within_group,EasternAfrica,Gusii,boys and young men,girls,age_set,age_set,teasing,symmetric,sexual joking / courting teasing,,"/Gusii_Levine_1959_978_KS:p::/Gusii_Levine_1959_978_KS::Each marketplace has its day of the week when activity is greatest and trading goes on; this is when girls dress up and go in groups to sell some family produce and be seen by boys. The boys and young men also attend, singly and in groups, looking for attractive girls. If she is friendly and laughs, however, the boy is encouraged and may begin some sexual joking. If the girl is favorably inclined to him she will respond in kind, usually using terms of obscene abuse.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Gusii_Levine_1959_978_KS:p,571
e69194475fe6,within_group,EasternAfrica,Gusii,boys circumcised in the same year,boys circumcised in the same year,age_set,age_set,joking_relationship,symmetric,pals,,"/Gusii_Levine_1966_187_KS:p::/Gusii_Levine_1966_187_KS::Boys circumcised in the same year, who regard each other as “pals,” consider the joking abuse which they hurl at one another as the most important indication of intimacy.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Gusii_Levine_1966_187_KS:p,574
a01e020a51e1,within_group,EasternAfrica,Gusii,bride's affinal group,groom's affinal group,group,group,insulting_license,symmetric,,,"/Gusii_Levine_1966_52_KS:p::/Gusii_Levine_1966_52_KS::It included an additional payment of cattle, aggressive interaction between the affinal groups in the form of wrestling matches, dancing contests, and reciprocal insulting of the bride and groom, as well as the placing of anklets of iron on the bride to indicate her married status.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Gusii_Levine_1966_52_KS:p,580
b623d6842c3e,within_group,EasternAfrica,Gusii,brother/cousin (momura ominto),brother/cousin (momura ominto),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,momura ominto,"/Gusii_Mayer_1965_33_KS:p::/Gusii_Mayer_1965_33_KS::The two men had never met before, but proceeded to ‘find out’ that they were to ‘call each other brother/cousin’ (momura ominto). This showed that the stool could be used; it was offered and accepted.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/Gusii_Mayer_1965_33_KS:p,595
13468d91c8a9,within_group,EasternAfrica,Gusii,caller (guest),old woman called 'grandmother' (magokoro),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,,magokoro,"/Gusii_Mayer_1965_33_KS:p::/Gusii_Mayer_1965_33_KS::After a lengthy preliminary process of ‘finding out’ he stepped forward and shook her hand, explaining for our benefit that ‘it seems I must call her “grandmother” (magokoro)’.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Gusii_Mayer_1965_33_KS:p,596
fa36eadfe4e5,within_group,EasternAfrica,Gusii,clansman,brother,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Gusii_Levine_1959_972_KS:p::/Gusii_Levine_1959_972_KS::With his “brother,” “grandfather,” and “grandchild,” he may make sexual jokes and discuss sexual matters, but with members of adjacent generations he must avoid all mention of sex.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Gusii_Levine_1959_972_KS:p,567
f3b75d57a284,within_group,EasternAfrica,Gusii,clansman,grandchild,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Gusii_Levine_1959_972_KS:p::/Gusii_Levine_1959_972_KS::With his “brother,” “grandfather,” and “grandchild,” he may make sexual jokes and discuss sexual matters, but with members of adjacent generations he must avoid all mention of sex.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Gusii_Levine_1959_972_KS:p,569
afbc2f056302,within_group,EasternAfrica,Gusii,clansman,grandfather,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Gusii_Levine_1959_972_KS:p::/Gusii_Levine_1959_972_KS::With his “brother,” “grandfather,” and “grandchild,” he may make sexual jokes and discuss sexual matters, but with members of adjacent generations he must avoid all mention of sex.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Gusii_Levine_1959_972_KS:p,568
968ca2ae0dfa,within_group,EasternAfrica,Gusii,clansman,members of adjacent generations,kin_role,kin_role,avoidance,symmetric,,abansoni,"/Gusii_Levine_1959_972_KS:p::/Gusii_Levine_1959_972_KS::With his “brother,” “grandfather,” and “grandchild,” he may make sexual jokes and discuss sexual matters, but with members of adjacent generations he must avoid all mention of sex. Persons of the latter group are said to be abansoni, in whose presence one experiences ensoni, sexual shame.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Gusii_Levine_1959_972_KS:p,570
d8c4f1e33311,within_group,EasternAfrica,Gusii,grandchild,grandparents (real or classificatory),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Gusii_Levine_1966_33_KS:p::/Gusii_Levine_1966_33_KS::The rules governing relationships between clansmen not of adjacent generations require less restraint and involve less embarrassment; they lack the elements of sex avoidance and respect which characterize classificatory parent-child relations. This lack of restraint is particularly striking in relations between an individual and his real or classificatory34 grandparents. He may discuss sex and insult them jokingly. In fact, the characteristic pattern of interaction in this relationship involves obscene insults, humorous inquiries about sexual activity, and deprecation of the other's sexual competence.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Gusii_Levine_1966_33_KS:p,577
70e5034ab985,within_group,EasternAfrica,Gusii,his parents-in-law,his parents,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Gusii_Levine_1966_52_KS:p::/Gusii_Levine_1966_52_KS::They may not enter the house of their son-in-law. They do, however, carry on a joking relationship with his parents.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Gusii_Levine_1966_52_KS:p,578
4f29f8d8292a,within_group,EasternAfrica,Gusii,husband's family,woman in labor / new mother,kin_role,kin_role,insulting_license,non_symmetric,hazing,,"/Gusii_Levine_1994_130_KS:p::/Gusii_Levine_1994_130_KS::Those women who were on good terms with their husband's family would probably be hazed only at first birth, regardless of whether their bridewealth had been paid; those not on good terms with their husband's family would be hazed even on their third and fourth births if bridewealth had not yet legitimized their unions. ... The Gusii women we observed in labor endured this hazing without overt response. ... the hostile laughter and joking sexual abuse that accompany it.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/Gusii_Levine_1994_130_KS:p,585
635c4bf28115,within_group,EasternAfrica,Gusii,maternal grandmother,grandson,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,primary joking partner,,"/Gusii_Levine_1982_53_KS:p::/Gusii_Levine_1982_53_KS::the maternal grandmother who is known in Gusii lore as the primary joking partner for her grandson and the one who calls her daughter's children by their praise-names, that is, the names they are called only at their mother's home and which connote so much warmth and affection that informants report their use brings tears to their eyes.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Gusii_Levine_1982_53_KS:p,584
c07740d6dbc4,within_group,EasternAfrica,Gusii,mature women,novice's mother,group,kin_role,insulting_license,non_symmetric,playful insults,,"/Gusii_Mayer_1952_27_KS:p::/Gusii_Mayer_1952_27_KS::The leading of a girl into seclusion follows the same broad pattern as that in the case of a boy, but some extra episodes are interpolated. In these episodes mature women take part. Their action consists in formalized attacks on and insults directed against the novice's mother. However,28 these are playful or joking insults: the tone is of high good humour.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Gusii_Mayer_1952_27_KS:p,592
a2badf38eceb,within_group,EasternAfrica,Gusii,mother,child / infant,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,maternal joking threats / playful threats,,"/Gusii_Levine_1994_218_KS:p::/Gusii_Levine_1994_218_KS::Sometimes mothers seem to be joking in their threats, and as children get older, they appear not to take them seriously: In one observation, for example, a 30-month-old girl asks her mother for a banana. When the mother says, “Go and get one,” the girl accuses her mother of having eaten her banana; the mother defends her authority by saying, “Today your uncle will beat you hard,” whereupon the little girl says, “I'll run away.”",0.85,0.85,1,1,/Gusii_Levine_1994_218_KS:p,586
6b339f071190,within_group,EasternAfrica,Gusii,novices,girls,age_set,group,joking_relationship,mixed,arousing desire,ogosonia,"/Gusii_Mayer_1952_22_KS:p::/Gusii_Mayer_1952_22_KS::‘Arousing Desire.’ During the first few days after esubo, at which time the wounds of circumcision have not thoroughly healed, parties of girls come to the seclusion huts ‘to trouble the novices’ (as the Gusii say) by what is called ‘arousing desire’ (ogosonia). Removing all their clothes, they dance around the novices in provocative attitudes and challenge them: ‘You boy, you said you would become a young man [omomura] and have intercourse with girls; now, are you ready? Here it is — what you wanted. Come Come and copulate!’ ... The boys do their best to joke in reply.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Gusii_Mayer_1952_22_KS:p,591
0c151b942af0,within_group,EasternAfrica,Gusii,omoηina,grandchildren,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,visit and joke with her,omoηina,"/Gusii_Levine_1980_89_KS:p::/Gusii_Levine_1980_89_KS::After menopause, in her later forties, she should have circumcised grandchildren, and her primary interests are focused on her sons' and daughters' children, who will visit and joke with her and perhaps live with her.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Gusii_Levine_1980_89_KS:p,583
995c0334708b,within_group,EasternAfrica,Gusii,polygynists,monogamous men,group,group,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,ridicule/jokes,,"/Gusii_Hakansson_1997_261_KS:p::/Gusii_Hakansson_1997_261_KS::In the 1950s, to be a monogamist was, for older men, a source of ridicule and jokes at beer parties, and monogamous men were often urged to take a second wife.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/Gusii_Hakansson_1997_261_KS:p,566
3dc055aabd8e,within_group,EasternAfrica,Gusii,small father in the clan,child in the clan,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Gusii_Mayer_1965_33_KS:p::/Gusii_Mayer_1965_33_KS::The general ‘finding out how to call one another’ does more than fix reciprocal etiquette patterns between two people. When a ‘small father in the clan’ and his ‘child in the clan’ meet in company, e.g. at someone else's beer drink or ceremony, one will find himself allotted to the group which behaves familiarly with the host, the other to the group which behaves familiarly with the host's son, or father.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Gusii_Mayer_1965_33_KS:p,594
a1cb05dff326,within_group,EasternAfrica,Gusii,tatamoke,korera,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,bawdy joking / licensed familiarity,tatamoke; korera,"/Gusii_Mayer_1965_32_KS:p::/Gusii_Mayer_1965_32_KS::‘I always used to call Anyiega my tatamoke omoamate (small father in the clan). But now that his second son has married a daughter of the half-brother of my second wife Nyanchama, I am entitled to call Anyiega korera (parent of spouse of child). And now when Anyiega wants to joke bawdily in my presence he calls me korera, though at other times, as my tatamoke, he behaves to me with restraint and will not enter my house.’",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Gusii_Mayer_1965_32_KS:p,593
9ee3d62ea8c0,within_group,EasternAfrica,Gusii,the lads,the novice (omoisia [uncircumcised boy]),group,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,,omoisia,"/Gusii_Mayer_1952_19_KS:p::/Gusii_Mayer_1952_19_KS::As he chokes and splutters, they laugh and shout, and beat him with sticks torn from the bower: ‘Eat, eat, you omoisia [uncircumcised boy]!’ To cry at this stage carries no disgrace comparable to that of flinching during circumcision.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Gusii_Mayer_1952_19_KS:p,589
0b37c9b51f29,within_group,EasternAfrica,Gusii,women,men,group,group,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,women's lighthearted/boisterous behavior versus men's solemn ritual role,,"/Gusii_Levine_1966_64_KS:p::/Gusii_Levine_1966_64_KS::In ceremonies conducted by men, women often take a lighthearted attitude which is recreational for them and inconsistent with the seriousness of the males in charge. ... The female tendency to be boisterous in a formal ritual setting is also exhibited during the leading-in ceremony of the male initiation rites ... Women are expected to be highly expressive and even obstructive on such occasions.",0.75,0.75,1,1,/Gusii_Levine_1966_64_KS:p,581
4a1d45c1f396,within_group,EasternAfrica,Gusii,women accompanying her daughter,mother (host) of the initiate / Bosibori,kin_role,kin_role,insulting_license,non_symmetric,ceremonial license to annoy / sanctioned misbehavior,,"/Gusii_Levine_1966_171_KS:p::/Gusii_Levine_1966_171_KS::When the women accompanying her daughter arrived at Bosibori's house, they pulled up more than half of her potato garden, gorging themselves on raw potatoes and taking others with them. She became furious and tried to stop them, but they said, “This is a happy occasion; we can do anything, even annoy you.” This license is expected, and even when carried to excess, as at Bosibori's, it is not recognized as ground for legal action or any other kind of retribution.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Gusii_Levine_1966_171_KS:p,573
2983fb594215,within_group,EasternAfrica,Gusii,young men courting girls,girls (being courted),group,group,teasing,mixed,,,"/Gusii_Levine_1966_187_KS:p::/Gusii_Levine_1966_187_KS::Young men courting girls jokingly threaten them with forceful violation, and the girls roundly insult them in return.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/Gusii_Levine_1966_187_KS:p,576
1b58d6cafa47,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luguru,Different hamlets in the neighborhood,Different hamlets in the neighborhood,village,village,joking_relationship,symmetric,utani / ugongo,utani; ugongo,"/Luguru_Hamdani_2001_4_KS:p::/Luguru_Hamdani_2001_4_KS::Different hamlets in the neighborhood have a joking relationship called “utani” or “ugongo”. These relationships used to be very important, much more than they are today.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Luguru_Hamdani_2001_4_KS:p,800
9e7ee20ef337,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luguru,Each clan,a joking partner from another clan,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Luguru_Adem_2020_7_KS:p::/Luguru_Adem_2020_7_KS::Each clan had a joking partner from another clan who would be called to conduct certain mortuary rites, usually involving sweeping the floor of the deceased’s hut, washing the corpse and wrapping it in a burial shroud (sanda), and proclaiming the circumstances of death;",0.75,0.75,1,1,/Luguru_Adem_2020_7_KS:p,763
d13976ce2b2c,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luguru,Lamba (rainmaker),adult woman joking partner,person,group,joking_relationship,symmetric,adult woman joking partner,,"/Luguru_Gemignani_2002_265_KS:p::/Luguru_Gemignani_2002_265_KS::In a different area of Mgeta I was told that rainmaking did not involve children, but instead the rainmaker would be always be accompanied by an adult woman joking partner (there is a linkage between clans with preferential marriage and those in which joking relationships occur).",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Luguru_Gemignani_2002_265_KS:p,794
c479982a3fd8,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luguru,Lamba (rainmaker),children joking partners (boys and girls),person,group,joking_relationship,symmetric,children joking partners,,"/Luguru_Gemignani_2002_265_KS:p::/Luguru_Gemignani_2002_265_KS::It is widely agreed that while Lamba was accompanied by one or two adults who were joking partners, his main assistants in this tambiko were children. This was described as consisting of one boy and one girl, or two boys and two girls.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Luguru_Gemignani_2002_265_KS:p,795
9264a074af86,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luguru,Wamangi,Mbunga,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,reciprocal drum-playing / ritual partnership,,"/Luguru_McVicar_1935_62_KS:p::/Luguru_McVicar_1935_62_KS::He considered it a useful instrument for his ngoma (band). Of course, in order to use it properly he had to call in the aid of the Wamangi sib, and thus it came about that the Wamangi formed part of the Wambunga ngoma. As a matter of fact, it is the Wamangi who play the drums when an Mbunga dies, and vice versa.",0.7,0.7,1,1,/Luguru_McVicar_1935_62_KS:p,806
ff74f122e50c,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luguru,Wamangi,Wabunga,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,privileged familiarity / joking relationship; watani; joking relationship,watani,"/Luguru_McVicar_1935_62_KS:p::/Luguru_McVicar_1935_62_KS::Between certain sibs there exists privileged familiarity or a joking relationship, as, for example, between the Wamangi and the Wabunga. || /Luguru_McVicar_1935_64_KS:p::/Luguru_McVicar_1935_64_KS::As a matter of fact, it would seem that the chief pleasure in life for a Mangi is to have a laugh at the expense of an Mbunga or to ‘pull something over on’ an Mbunga,—the Wamangi and the Wabunga being, as we have mentioned, of sibs between which the joking relationship obtains. Such inter-sib watani may do or say whatsoever they like when there is question of their watani.",0.9,0.9,2,2,/Luguru_McVicar_1935_62_KS:p; /Luguru_McVicar_1935_64_KS:p,805;808
2a6150ff1dc3,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luguru,Wamangi,Wambunga,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,watani / kutaniana,watani; mtani; kutaniana,"/Luguru_McVicar_1935_63_KS:p::/Luguru_McVicar_1935_63_KS::The Wamangi are the watani of the Wambunga, and vice versa. ... Sibs between which privileged familiarity or the joking relationship prevails are said to kutaniana.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Luguru_McVicar_1935_63_KS:p,807
03d1776f14cd,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luguru,children of a man,son of father's sister,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,mtani,mtani,"/Luguru_Brain_1971_823_KS:p::/Luguru_Brain_1971_823_KS::She has important functions to play with respect to her brother's daughter at the time of the latter's puberty ceremonies. Since she is the mother of her brother's heir, her son is termed “father” by her brother's children after the brother's decease, although before that time he is given the term of mtani, which, as we have already noted, also means joking partner.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/Luguru_Brain_1971_823_KS:p,775
d79e3e92bc65,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luguru,clans,reciprocating clans,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,ugongo / utani (reciprocal joking relationship),ugongo; utani,"/Luguru_Young_1960_44_KS:p::/Luguru_Young_1960_44_KS::A more important relationship between clans is that of ugongo (L.) or utani (S.), i.e. reciprocal or joking relationship. Not all clans possess a link in this manner, but where they do there is also a trend to preferential marriage between the two linked clans.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/Luguru_Young_1960_44_KS:p,810
de00f050f8de,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luguru,classificatory cross␀c2␀adcousins,classificatory cross␀c2␀adcousins,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,utani or ugonga,/Luguru_Biedelman_1967_28_KS:p::/Luguru_Biedelman_1967_28_KS::and they exist between classificatory cross␀c2␀adcousins.,0.95,0.95,1,1,/Luguru_Biedelman_1967_28_KS:p,769
058454deba10,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luguru,cross-cousin (mtani),cross-cousin (mtani),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,mtani; watani; wagongo,"/Luguru_Brain_1971_822_KS:p::/Luguru_Brain_1971_822_KS::But here the problem arises. The term for a cross-cousin in Luguru is mtani. Naturally many watani will also be wagongo, and so we are left with the puzzle - are cross-cousins called watani because one behaves towards them in a joking fashion, or is the relationship referred to as utani between clans (and tribes) because one behaves as one would towards a cross-cousin?",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Luguru_Brain_1971_822_KS:p,773
36eb89521c1d,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luguru,cross-cousins,cross-cousins,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking partners,,"/Luguru_Gemignani_2002_236_KS:p::/Luguru_Gemignani_2002_236_KS::No matter what happens or the kind of complaints that are delivered (these may be significant, especially since cross-cousins are joking partners), the person must sit without reaction.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Luguru_Gemignani_2002_236_KS:p,793
50754f9ebcb1,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luguru,elder brothers,younger brothers' wives,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,joking relationship,sekuru,"/Luguru_Brain_1971_832_KS:p::/Luguru_Brain_1971_832_KS::younger brothers call their elder brothers sekuru “grandfather”, and have to treat their wives with respect, whereas elder brothers have a joking relationship with their younger brothers' wives.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Luguru_Brain_1971_832_KS:p,777
94555c503943,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luguru,grandparents,grandchildren,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1317_KS:p::/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1317_KS::One type of bantering and joking that is not limited to watani is that between grandparents and grandchildren. The kinship terminology permits alternating generations to refer to each other as “brother” or “sister,” and a young man may tease his grandfather about marrying his wife.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1317_KS:p,786
627f73905a3d,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luguru,initiands,joking partners,age_set,group,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking partners,,"/Luguru_Brain_1980_378_KS:p::/Luguru_Brain_1980_378_KS::On one occasion a large adolescent boy, one of the initiands' joking partners, was carried round clowning the part, to laughter from the crowd.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Luguru_Brain_1980_378_KS:p,780
94bbea6cc30f,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luguru,kigori (young girl),the family,kin_role,group,joking_relationship,symmetric,,kigori,/Luguru_Hamdani_2001_15_KS:p::/Luguru_Hamdani_2001_15_KS::As a young girl (between 9–13) before reaching puberty she was referred to as “kigori”. The family would establish a joking relationship with her while at the same time she would be socially nurtured towards attaining womanhood.,0.8,0.8,1,1,/Luguru_Hamdani_2001_15_KS:p,799
707467f84cf7,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luguru,kindred,joking partners,kin_role,group,joking_relationship,unclear,,,"/Luguru_Brain_1980_378_KS:p::/Luguru_Brain_1980_378_KS::On one occasion cooked rice and catfish were handed round to the kindred and joking partners, who all ate together, men and women, standing up, simulating great haste.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/Luguru_Brain_1980_378_KS:p,781
59c91b66db6d,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luguru,lineage members,his joking partners,lineage,kin_role,joking_relationship,unclear,,,"/Luguru_Gemignani_2002_278_KS:p::/Luguru_Gemignani_2002_278_KS::He listed some of the possible mistakes that this man made, bringing bread and tea for the ancestors to Mbagho's ritual space at Rudewa, as well as the traditional black sheep, having other lineage members eat the food who were not his joking partners.",0.6,0.6,1,1,/Luguru_Gemignani_2002_278_KS:p,796
1d853168c861,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luguru,linked sub-clans,linked sub-clans,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,ugongo / utani,ugongo; utani,"/Luguru_Brain_1971_822_KS:p::/Luguru_Brain_1971_822_KS::Another aspect of the social structure, which must be mentioned is the question of ugongo (Luguru), utani (Swahili), a system of joking relationships between linked sub-clans which occurs all over the culture area",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Luguru_Brain_1971_822_KS:p,771
3a635b9af2bf,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luguru,man,younger brother's wife,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Luguru_Brain_1971_823_KS:p::/Luguru_Brain_1971_823_KS::On the contrary, elder brothers are treated with extreme respect, as are their wives, whereas in the reverse direction we find that men have a joking relationship with younger brothers' wives.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Luguru_Brain_1971_823_KS:p,776
6328a3787b5f,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luguru,matrilineage,clan joking partners (watani),lineage,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,watani,watani,/Luguru_Biedelman_1967_33_KS:p::/Luguru_Biedelman_1967_33_KS::Burial and mortuary rites are conducted by clan joking partners (watani). ... The rites (kutambika) are directed by the head of the matrilineage but some are conducted in part by joking partners (watani).,0.9,0.9,1,1,/Luguru_Biedelman_1967_33_KS:p,770
553cab6a036e,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luguru,members of joking relationship sib's,members of sibs of common origin,clan,clan,joking_relationship,unclear,joking relationship,,"/Luguru_McVicar_1935_65_KS:p::/Luguru_McVicar_1935_65_KS::I have heard it said that nowadays marriage is taking place between such members of joking relationship sib's and also between members of sibs of common origin, but I do not know how true this is, for it must be very new, and I am not acquainted with any specific instance.",0.6,0.6,1,1,/Luguru_McVicar_1935_65_KS:p,809
70856bc79378,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luguru,mgongo,wagongo,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,ugongo (utani),ugongo,"/Luguru_Brain_1978_178_KS:p::/Luguru_Brain_1978_178_KS::Ugongo is an institutionalized joking relationship between linked clans which are conceptualized as being the exact structural equivalents. ... A man frequently marries an mgongo, who should at the same time be a cross-cousin. ... Rites for the spirits, tambiko, can only be performed with the assistance of the joking partners from the linked clan, the wagongo, symbolically indicating the interdependence and complementarity of the two structurally equal and opposite groups.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Luguru_Brain_1978_178_KS:p,778
ec22c837b934,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luguru,mother-in-law,daughter-in-law,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,,,"/Luguru_Gemignani_2002_280_KS:p::/Luguru_Gemignani_2002_280_KS::Mama Mwenda told a funny story about how her mother-in-law once asked her if she was going home to give “food” to Baba Mwenda. When she asked, “What food?” her mother-in-law stated, “the food that you close in a satchel and walk around with everyday”",0.85,0.85,1,1,/Luguru_Gemignani_2002_280_KS:p,798
e8ef0c464732,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luguru,mtani,watani,person,group,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,appropriation rights of watani/mtani,watani/mtani,"/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1318_KS:p::/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1318_KS::It is also permissible to appropriate the possessions of watani, but rather definite rules govern this type of acquisition. For one thing, it should be done openly and in the presence of the owner or his family. Confiscation is most likely to occur at a funeral when watani of the deceased may walk off with a goat, fowl, yams, or even such items as a hoe or panga.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1318_KS:p,788
2e92938b758b,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luguru,mtani elder,young mtani,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,watani / utani,watani/utani,"/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1317_KS:p::/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1317_KS::A high degree of familiarity is permitted between alternate generations, and it is permissible for a youth to deride a mtani elder if at some time during the exchange he shows the proper respect. For example, a elder named Mbewa ... approached the house of a young mtani and the latter started calling to those around to “kill the rat, kill the rat.”",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1317_KS:p,785
0f2ee57abaf6,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luguru,some Luguru clans,some Luguru clans,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,,utani or ugonga,/Luguru_Biedelman_1967_28_KS:p::/Luguru_Biedelman_1967_28_KS::they also exist between some Luguru clans which provide certain ritual and burial services for one another;,0.85,0.85,1,1,/Luguru_Biedelman_1967_28_KS:p,768
400d4359e860,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luguru,son-in-law,his wife's clan,kin_role,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,,mtani,"/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1316_KS:p::/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1316_KS::For one thing, a man is likely to be marrying into the clan of his father or grandfather, but, in any case, he will have been joking with his wife's clan for years.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1316_KS:p,784
8c2e227f79c4,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luguru,sub-clan,sub-clan,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,ugongo / utani (wagongo),ugongo; utani; wagongo,"/Luguru_Brain_1971_823_KS:p::/Luguru_Brain_1971_823_KS::It must be fairly clear that in Uluguru, where all the sub-clans live in contiguous areas of relatively fertile land in the mountains, each sub-clan will have such ties to other such units on all sides of it, these ties being regarded in a variety of ways. On the one hand wagongo are cousins, but they are also affines (cf. Fox 1967, LEACH 1961 and NEEDHAM 1962).",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Luguru_Brain_1971_823_KS:p,774
cf71c327baa9,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luguru,unmarried cross-cousins,unmarried cross-cousins,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,watani / sexual license,watani,"/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1319_KS:p::/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1319_KS::The greatest amount of sexual license is permitted between unmarried cross-cousins, and an affair between these two is rarely condemned except by acculturated Christians. Cross-cousins are always considered watani and may be referred to as such whether or not their clans observe the utani relationship.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1319_KS:p,789
229f25800ce5,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luguru,wagongo,mgongo,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,ugongo (Swahili utani),ugongo,"/Luguru_Brain_1980_372_KS:p::/Luguru_Brain_1980_372_KS::Ugongo is an institutionalized joking relationship between linked clans which are conceptualized as being the exact structural equivalents. ... one frequently marries a mgongo, who should at the same time be a cross-cousin.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Luguru_Brain_1980_372_KS:p,779
b7460dc72eed,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luguru,watani,clan,kin_role,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,utani/watani joking relationship,utani/watani,"/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1320_KS:p::/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1320_KS::The function of the watani, in addition to the joking noted above, is assisting the clan in preparation of the body and interment. ... The watani clan would be offended if they were not notified, for in addition to the slight, it would deprive them of their right to get even regarding confiscation of food.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1320_KS:p,791
d2651fe6aff6,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luguru,watani,clan members,clan,lineage,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,rebuke/teasing during ancestor ceremonies,watani,"/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1321_KS:p::/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1321_KS::During the prayers to the clan ancestors the watani may rebuke or coerce the dead, saying “You who have died, do not come back and cause trouble for the living,” or they may admonish the elders to carry out the ceremony in the prescribed manner so as not to offend the ancestors. Joking may enter in here again, for when the names of ancestors are mentioned that a mtani does not recall, he may enquire “Who are they?”",0.8,0.8,1,1,/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1321_KS:p,792
8b20fd6e653e,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luguru,watani,mtani,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,utani (ugongo),utani,"/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1315_KS:p::/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1315_KS::Every Luguru clan claims to have a utani relationship, involving joking and obligations, with one or more clans in their vicinity. ... The greatest degree of joking is carried on between watani of the same generation, and cross-sexual joking is common, with obscene and suggestive language and horseplay.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1315_KS:p,782
0f2b81ade2b5,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luguru,watani,watani,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,watani joking,watani,"/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1316_KS:p::/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1316_KS::While a Luguru should show deference toward an elder, joking is permitted between all generations of watani, and the only avoidance observed is toward the mother-in-law.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1316_KS:p,783
dcdd33060f0d,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luguru,watani (male),watani (female),kin_role,kin_role,insulting_license,symmetric,joking between watani,watani,/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1319_KS:p::/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1319_KS::The joking between watani that permits highly suggestive or obscene comments thus may be utilized to ascertain the availability of a mtani as a sexual partner.,0.9,0.9,1,1,/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1319_KS:p,790
886c45156339,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luguru,watani clan(s) of the deceased,clan of the deceased,kin_role,clan,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,,watani,"/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1317_KS:p::/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1317_KS::Considerable horseplay by the watani clan or clans of the deceased is permitted and expected at a burial. ... They have been known to pour a pot of water over a wailing woman under these circumstances. Or again, a mtani might say to the clan of the deceased: “You are crying too much. Give me one woman to take to my house so that I can give you a child to resemble the person who is dead.”",0.95,0.95,1,1,/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1317_KS:p,787
272995a83d2a,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luguru,women,husbands,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,,posho,/Luguru_Gemignani_2002_280_KS:p::/Luguru_Gemignani_2002_280_KS::One hears many jokes made by women regarding the “food rations” (posho) that women distribute to their husbands.,0.8,0.8,1,1,/Luguru_Gemignani_2002_280_KS:p,797
c8e1719c664d,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luo,JONDARIA,the village (hosts),group,village,avoidance,non_symmetric,,JONDARIA,/Luo_Wilson_1961_95_KS:p::/Luo_Wilson_1961_95_KS::Most Luo tribes state that the JONDARIA are ignored in the village and are not given food but must wait until their return the next day to their own village before they eat.,0.8,0.8,1,1,/Luo_Wilson_1961_95_KS:p,817
a86f0a59c243,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luo,JONDARIA,the village of the groom,group,village,insulting_license,non_symmetric,licensed liberties,JONDARIA,"/Luo_Wilson_1961_95_KS:p::/Luo_Wilson_1961_95_KS::On the other hand, some state that the JONDARIA have great licence in the village and enter the granaries and take anything edible they find which they cook and eat by themselves.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/Luo_Wilson_1961_95_KS:p,816
3766fe112abb,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luo,JONDARIA,the young men of the groom's village,group,age_set,joking_relationship,symmetric,JONDARIA,JONDARIA,/Luo_Wilson_1961_95_KS:p::/Luo_Wilson_1961_95_KS::During the rest of the day is a period of flirtation and joking between the JONDARIA and the young men of the groom's village.,0.9,0.9,1,1,/Luo_Wilson_1961_95_KS:p,815
836a4ea86959,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luo,brothers of the bride,brothers of the groom,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,DIERO / JODIERO,DIERO; JODIERO; RWADH DIER,"/Luo_Wilson_1961_100_KS:p::/Luo_Wilson_1961_100_KS::35. The brothers of the bride have meanwhile been waiting patiently for the news that the bride has accepted the CHIENO, for now they gather their strongest and most skilled fighters from among their LIBAMBA and their neighbours to take the first head of cattle from the herd of the groom to their own village. This group is known as the JODIERO, the selectors, and they go to select a beast from the herd of the groom. The brothers of the groom naturally resist the selection and the two groups wrestle and fight mock battles with sticks until both parties agree on the beast to be taken.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Luo_Wilson_1961_100_KS:p,814
2464dd526d0c,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luo,jogam,sisters of Adhiambo,group,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,playmates / flirting,jogam,"/Luo_Cohen_1989_106_KS:p::/Luo_Cohen_1989_106_KS::The food was eaten slowly, with some flirting between the jogam and the sisters of Adhiambo and some jokes passed back and forth with the brothers.",0.6,0.6,1,1,/Luo_Cohen_1989_106_KS:p,811
31d7a87b3ea8,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luo,sisters of Adhiambo,brothers,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Luo_Cohen_1989_106_KS:p::/Luo_Cohen_1989_106_KS::The food was eaten slowly, with some flirting between the jogam and the sisters of Adhiambo and some jokes passed back and forth with the brothers.",0.5,0.5,1,1,/Luo_Cohen_1989_106_KS:p,812
8bd8bd1e447a,within_group,EasternAfrica,Luo,the jogam,Adhiambo's people,kin_role,group,joking_relationship,unclear,,jogam,"/Luo_Cohen_1989_108_KS:p::/Luo_Cohen_1989_108_KS::The record-player was brought back to the room and there was music and dancing, with a good deal of teasing and joking between the jogam and Adhiambo's people.",0.6,0.6,1,1,/Luo_Cohen_1989_108_KS:p,813
576369760bb2,within_group,EasternAfrica,Maasai,The elders,smaller boys,group,age_set,teasing,non_symmetric,teasing of younger boys,,"/Maasai_Spencer_1988_73_KS:p::/Maasai_Spencer_1988_73_KS::The elders then called over smaller boys to tease them, playfully dragging them towards the doorway and asking them if they would like to be circumcised.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Maasai_Spencer_1988_73_KS:p,837
c4fd74b3e2d9,within_group,EasternAfrica,Maasai,a band of about ten initiates,the youth (the initiate) led from his mother's hut,age_set,kin_role,teasing,non_symmetric,initiates' teasing of new initiate,,"/Maasai_Spencer_1988_73_KS:p::/Maasai_Spencer_1988_73_KS::a band of about ten initiates, who had recently recovered from the operation and wore their full regalia, led the youth from his mother's hut, singing and holding each arm... They paused at certain points, singing and making pointed references to the fact that none of them had flinched.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Maasai_Spencer_1988_73_KS:p,836
864c2d5ee933,within_group,EasternAfrica,Maasai,age mates,age mates,age_set,age_set,banter,symmetric,supportive joking among age-mates,,"/Maasai_Spencer_1988_147_KS:p::/Maasai_Spencer_1988_147_KS::As the elders dispersed, he remained with a small group of age mates, entertaining them with a display of joking and good humour. They encouraged him, rather as moran closely support an age mate after a bout of shaking.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/Maasai_Spencer_1988_147_KS:p,826
e311a5d45c08,within_group,EasternAfrica,Maasai,bride (new wife),girls (younger women) of the husband's village,kin_role,age_set,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,taunting by girls,,"/Maasai_Spencer_1988_31_KS:p::/Maasai_Spencer_1988_31_KS::She is no longer a girl and now even girls come out to taunt her. These are jokes that point up some of the sombre realities of wifehood, and mark the most radical transition in her life with a totally new role and surrounded by new faces.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/Maasai_Spencer_1988_31_KS:p,834
ded5fe018c3e,within_group,EasternAfrica,Maasai,bride (new wife),women closely related to her husband,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,taunting during reception,,"/Maasai_Spencer_1988_31_KS:p::/Maasai_Spencer_1988_31_KS::She is left by herself outside this village, and is then greeted by the women, who come out to taunt her persistently until she breaks down in tears. They may tease her for being her husband's current favourite who will in her turn be discarded.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Maasai_Spencer_1988_31_KS:p,833
13c4d96cb962,within_group,EasternAfrica,Maasai,courier (clan ‘sister’ or the wife of an age mate),the girl (prospective bride),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,anointment (esiret),esiret,"/Maasai_Spencer_1988_26_KS:p::/Maasai_Spencer_1988_26_KS::The suitor discreetly approaches one of the village wives to be his courier, perhaps a clan ‘sister’ or the wife of an age mate. In an unguarded moment, she calls over the girl and taking her by surprise, traces a smear of the butter from the bridge of her nose to her crown. Realising what has occurred, the girl is expected to rage. She snatches at leaves or grass or anything on hand to wipe off the butter, while the other women and even girls laugh at her.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Maasai_Spencer_1988_26_KS:p,832
03e4bdf970d8,within_group,EasternAfrica,Maasai,father,son,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,joke at the expense of the younger man,,"/Maasai_Spencer_1988_121_KS:p::/Maasai_Spencer_1988_121_KS::If a father quarrels with his son and starts to shiver, the son should run away whatever his age, as if the father has raised his stick. The incident then becomes a joke at the expense of the younger man.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Maasai_Spencer_1988_121_KS:p,824
18a83249668a,within_group,EasternAfrica,Maasai,firestick patrons,moran,group,age_set,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,authoritarian ritual of counter-rebellion / harangue,,"/Maasai_Spencer_1988_147_KS:p::/Maasai_Spencer_1988_147_KS::The response of the patrons throughout the morning was similar to that described for all such occasions when they assemble to harangue the moran. ... During the breaks in moments of pique, certain patrons occasionally cracked their long whips at unsubdued groups of moran as though they were frisky cattle. The moran would dutifully retire to a safer distance, ‘running away’ from the elders' anger, although still sometimes giggling.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Maasai_Spencer_1988_147_KS:p,825
38fd37853091,within_group,EasternAfrica,Maasai,firestick patrons of Terito age-set,manyata-mothers,age_set,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,patrons' mock curses at manyata-mothers,enoonkureeta,"/Maasai_Spencer_1988_164_KS:p::/Maasai_Spencer_1988_164_KS::The patrons are expected to come with long whips and an assortment of warrior headgear in the spirit of a burlesque on moranhood. ... they crack their whips as they approach the manyata, and hurl mock curses and accusations of incest. Their aim is to frighten the manyata-mothers who are less certain that these mock curses are harmless play.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Maasai_Spencer_1988_164_KS:p,829
cd2db9f727f3,within_group,EasternAfrica,Maasai,firestick patrons of Terito age-set,moran,age_set,age_set,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,patron–moran mock aggression at disbanding ceremony,,"/Maasai_Spencer_1988_164_KS:p::/Maasai_Spencer_1988_164_KS::Their firestick patrons of Terito age-set systematically coerced them from this defiant stand. ... Then, striding away from the meeting, the patrons left the moran with little option other than to rush up with fistfuls of grass, forcing the patrons to recant, but at the same time submitting to the inevitable.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Maasai_Spencer_1988_164_KS:p,827
d12aea7d2665,within_group,EasternAfrica,Maasai,manyata moran,elders,age_set,group,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,teased by elders / watched and teased on return,,"/Maasai_Spencer_1988_110_KS:p::/Maasai_Spencer_1988_110_KS::The elders can only demand that visiting moran should behave as guests with an outward respect, and the moran are aware that their comings and goings are being carefully watched from a discreet distance.
...If a manyata moran stays in the elders' village area for more than the occasional night without manyata permission, he must account for himself on his return. ... he can expect to be teased.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Maasai_Spencer_1988_110_KS:p,821
9ef35f30b070,within_group,EasternAfrica,Maasai,manyata moran,manyata moran (returning from elders' villages),age_set,age_set,joking_relationship,symmetric,peer teasing / taunting,,"/Maasai_Spencer_1988_110_KS:p::/Maasai_Spencer_1988_110_KS::When other manyata moran see him returning, they may call out: ‘The donkeys have come.’",0.85,0.85,1,1,/Maasai_Spencer_1988_110_KS:p,823
9c8a4dadbb0b,within_group,EasternAfrica,Maasai,manyata women,manyata moran,group,age_set,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,women teasing returning moran,,"/Maasai_Spencer_1988_110_KS:p::/Maasai_Spencer_1988_110_KS::When other manyata moran see him returning, they may call out: ‘The donkeys have come.’ The manyata women then take up the joke: ‘He's just a donkey, for he ran away to the elders' villages ... Let's grab him and drive him to get some water! Let's put a hide on his back and load him up!’",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Maasai_Spencer_1988_110_KS:p,822
19796b6fa2c9,within_group,EasternAfrica,Maasai,moran,manyata-mothers,age_set,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,moran–mother joking abuse,,"/Maasai_Spencer_1988_164_KS:p::/Maasai_Spencer_1988_164_KS::The abusive idiom of the patrons recalls the first forest feast when joking abuse between moran and their mothers was indirectly at the expense of their absent fathers, whom they were about to desert for the manyata.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Maasai_Spencer_1988_164_KS:p,828
7d95b2b0a761,within_group,EasternAfrica,Maasai,moran,mothers,group,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking at first forest ox,,"/Maasai_Spencer_1988_165_KS:p::/Maasai_Spencer_1988_165_KS::As with the joking between moran and their mothers at the first forest ox (of-the-earplugs), the Matapato acknowledged but did not stress the element of joking abuse.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Maasai_Spencer_1988_165_KS:p,831
dfbf9382c217,within_group,EasternAfrica,Maasai,novice moran,fathers (family heads),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,butt of the joke,,"/Maasai_Spencer_1988_84_KS:p::/Maasai_Spencer_1988_84_KS::The various allusions are to the period when they will leave home and take their mothers to live in the manyata (their warrior village); and their fathers, who are conspicuously absent, are the indirect butt of the joke.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/Maasai_Spencer_1988_84_KS:p,839
531fcba09d64,within_group,EasternAfrica,Maasai,novice moran,mothers,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,possessory teasing / control,manyata,"/Maasai_Spencer_1988_84_KS:p::/Maasai_Spencer_1988_84_KS::If mothers have sexual relations with their own fathers and with the moran friends of their daughters and come under the overbearing protection of their own sons and feed them, then their husbands - the fathers of the moran - are superfluous.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/Maasai_Spencer_1988_84_KS:p,840
f1bfc39cade6,within_group,EasternAfrica,Maasai,patrons,moran,group,group,insulting_license,non_symmetric,disbanding ceremony abuses,enoonkureeta,"/Maasai_Spencer_1988_165_KS:p::/Maasai_Spencer_1988_165_KS::Forty-nine firestick patrons cowl their heads with sheepskin caps, inkureeta, whence the name of the ceremony, enoonkureeta. Their abuses against the moran incorporate such phrases as: ‘become bitter; be finished off; become lost for ever; you have fucked your mothers when they denied us [this right]; I have come and hear and see that your mother has nothing left to fuck’.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Maasai_Spencer_1988_165_KS:p,830
18a2aeabd4fc,within_group,EasternAfrica,Maasai,some women,several young children,group,age_set,teasing,non_symmetric,women's playful teasing of children,,"/Maasai_Spencer_1988_73_KS:p::/Maasai_Spencer_1988_73_KS::Outside, catching this jocular mood, some women seized several young children. One of them started to prise out their lower incisors as if to emphasise to them the ordeal that awaited them, hooting with laughter while the children howled.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/Maasai_Spencer_1988_73_KS:p,838
7b347cac09b9,within_group,EasternAfrica,Maasai,the assembled warriors,the parents of the boy,group,kin_role,insulting_license,non_symmetric,abusive words and blows / reviling,,"/Maasai_Merker_1910_82_KS:p::/Maasai_Merker_1910_82_KS::During the circumcision itself the parents of the boy remain in their hut, for if the latter moans or cries out from the pain, they are punished with abusive 83 63 cont. words and blows by the assembled warriors, because they did not rear their son to have the hardening and self-control necessary for a warrior, while the weakling is reviled for a long time afterwards as eb[unknown] iryo = coward, or t[unknown] or[unknown] on[unknown] o = the bad one.",0.6,0.6,1,1,/Maasai_Merker_1910_82_KS:p,820
3b4012d9bcbb,within_group,EasternAfrica,Maasai,those close to the initiate and others that have recently been circumcised,the initiate,group,kin_role,teasing,non_symmetric,provoking / scorning during circumcision,,"/Maasai_Spencer_1988_73_KS:p::/Maasai_Spencer_1988_73_KS::Those close to the initiate and others that have recently been circumcised may deliberately try to provoke his anger, scorning and threatening him, and distancing themselves from him if he does flinch, but also firming his resolve not to do so.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/Maasai_Spencer_1988_73_KS:p,835
ff4ccef8c9d2,within_group,EasternAfrica,Nuba,a man,his brother's wife,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Nuba_Nadel_1947_285_KS:p::/Nuba_Nadel_1947_285_KS::If a man dreamed in this way about his classificatory sister, his cousin, or possibly his brother's wife, he would think nothing of it and might even joke about it with the heroine of his dream.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/Nuba_Nadel_1947_285_KS:p,903
337e2c03e0d6,within_group,EasternAfrica,Nuba,a man,his classificatory sister,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Nuba_Nadel_1947_285_KS:p::/Nuba_Nadel_1947_285_KS::If a man dreamed in this way about his classificatory sister, his cousin, or possibly his brother's wife, he would think nothing of it and might even joke about it with the heroine of his dream.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/Nuba_Nadel_1947_285_KS:p,901
5ca45578624d,within_group,EasternAfrica,Nuba,a man,his cousin,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,narane,"/Nuba_Nadel_1947_285_KS:p::/Nuba_Nadel_1947_285_KS::If a man dreamed in this way about his classificatory sister, his cousin, or possibly his brother's wife, he would think nothing of it and might even joke about it with the heroine of his dream.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/Nuba_Nadel_1947_285_KS:p,902
dae4fba6c260,within_group,EasternAfrica,Nuba,danndanna (male),danndanna (female),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,danndanna,"/Nuba_Faris_1989_166_KS:p::/Nuba_Faris_1989_166_KS::Sexual joking with danndanna of the opposite sex is appropriate, as it is an appropriate marriage category.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Nuba_Faris_1989_166_KS:p,900
8cdaea971c6a,within_group,EasternAfrica,Nuer,a man,his wife's brother,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,jokingly curse one another,,"/Nuer_EvansPritchard_1951_101_KS:p::/Nuer_EvansPritchard_1951_101_KS::After the birth of children a man and his wife's brother can, if great friends, jokingly curse one another in obscene language.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Nuer_EvansPritchard_1951_101_KS:p,920
fc17c2542372,within_group,EasternAfrica,Nuer,a man,his wife's sister,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joke / exchange abusive language,,/Nuer_EvansPritchard_1951_101_KS:p::/Nuer_EvansPritchard_1951_101_KS::The only in-law whom a man need not at any time treat with reserve is his wife's sister. He can joke with her and even exchange abusive language with her.,0.9,0.9,1,1,/Nuer_EvansPritchard_1951_101_KS:p,921
311fe2bfd193,within_group,EasternAfrica,Nuer,a man's wife,her husband's brothers,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,on joking terms,,"/Nuer_Colson_1952_152_KS:p::/Nuer_Colson_1952_152_KS::For a man's wife, avoidance is impracticable, but she treats her parents-in-law with great respect and, in the early days of marriage, keeps herself apart from them as much as possible. She is on joking terms with her husband's brothers since, among brothers, the wife of one is, in the social sense, the wife of all.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Nuer_Colson_1952_152_KS:p,915
07d96ccdf905,within_group,EasternAfrica,Nuer,"gaatgwanlen, male ortho-cousins, the sons of brothers","gaatgwanlen, male ortho-cousins, the sons of brothers",kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking or teasing relationship,gaatgwanlen,/Nuer_EvansPritchard_1951_159_KS:p::/Nuer_EvansPritchard_1951_159_KS::It is perhaps this contradiction between social bonds and personal feelings which gives rise to the joking or teasing relationship between ortho-cousins.,0.9,0.9,1,1,/Nuer_EvansPritchard_1951_159_KS:p,922
b6ff682fbebe,within_group,EasternAfrica,Nuer,legal representatives of the bridegroom,bride's family,group,group,joking_relationship,symmetric,dwil koam joking,dwil koam,"/Nuer_Howell_19xx_93_KS:p::/Nuer_Howell_19xx_93_KS::After the dancing is over, those who are not closely related to either party go home, but the legal representatives of the bridegroom and the bride's family and some of their close kinsmen, both male and female, go into a but specially prepared for them which is known as the dwil koam for the occasion. There they sit all night joking and conversing with their hosts until dawn.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Nuer_Howell_19xx_93_KS:p,924
1a2fff08811c,within_group,EasternAfrica,Nuer,members of the same age-set,age-mates,age_set,age_set,joking_relationship,symmetric,comradeship; joking and play among age-mates,,"/Nuer_EvansPritchard_1940_257_KS:p::/Nuer_EvansPritchard_1940_257_KS::Members of the same age-set are on terms of entire equality. A man does not stand on ceremony with his age-mates, but jokes, plays, and eats with them at his ease.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Nuer_EvansPritchard_1940_257_KS:p,918
b2d7d5e81ce1,within_group,EasternAfrica,Nuer,members of the same age-set,wife of a man of his own age-set,age_set,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,take liberties / permitted to joke,,/Nuer_EvansPritchard_1936_262_KS:p::/Nuer_EvansPritchard_1936_262_KS::As we have seen at initiation feasts a man is permitted to joke and take liberties with the wife of a man of his own age-set.,0.9,0.9,1,1,/Nuer_EvansPritchard_1936_262_KS:p,917
ce806c8ccfa7,within_group,EasternAfrica,Nuer,mother-in-law,son-in-law,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,mixed,mother-in-law relationship,,"/Nuer_EvansPritchard_1951_100_KS:p::/Nuer_EvansPritchard_1951_100_KS::The mother-in-law relationship among the Nuer is a subject of mirth, and the foulest slips of the tongue are attributed to mothers-in-law when talking to their daughters' husbands.
A man may even joke with his mother-in-law when there are several children.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Nuer_EvansPritchard_1951_100_KS:p,919
e1d2d382d298,within_group,EasternAfrica,Nuer,nei ti gwagh,sons (young men),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,licensed joking with unrelated people,nei ti gwagh,"/Nuer_EvansPritchard_1951_39_KS:p::/Nuer_EvansPritchard_1951_39_KS::The nei ti gwagh, the unrelated people, they can joke with and make love to without fear of censure or retribution, so long as they are not wives of other men, for fornication with an unrelated girl is not regarded with disapprobation.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/Nuer_EvansPritchard_1951_39_KS:p,923
eb4e2302a085,within_group,EasternAfrica,Nuer,ric (age-set members),ric (age-set members),age_set,age_set,joking_relationship,symmetric,,ric,/Nuer_EvansPritchard_1936_261_KS:p::/Nuer_EvansPritchard_1936_261_KS::Age-mates are on terms of complete equality. A man does not stand on ceremony with an age-mate but eats and plays and jokes with him as an equal.,0.9,0.9,1,1,/Nuer_EvansPritchard_1936_261_KS:p,916
19c149eebc04,within_group,EasternAfrica,Nuer,young people of the bridegroom's party,young people of the bride's party,age_set,age_set,joking_relationship,symmetric,dwil twacni / dwil kwen courting badinage,dwil twacni; dwil kwen,"/Nuer_Howell_19xx_93_KS:p::/Nuer_Howell_19xx_93_KS::Another hut, known as the dwil twacni or dwil kwen, is set aside for the young people of both parties, and the bride and bridegroom, their gwan twac and ngi nyal, sit there with their friends and coevals singing and exchanging courting badinage all night through.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Nuer_Howell_19xx_93_KS:p,925
b1d091d73133,within_group,EasternAfrica,Okiek,brother,sister,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Okiek_Huntingford_1951_35_KS:p::/Okiek_Huntingford_1951_35_KS::Between the sexes of the same generation there may be a perfectly innocuous intimacy, between brother and sister, between macokor, mama, and kesenda, which allows any kind of joking and language but stops short of actual sexual intercourse;",0.85,0.85,1,1,/Okiek_Huntingford_1951_35_KS:p,942
a4df019f9ecc,within_group,EasternAfrica,Okiek,children,adults,group,group,joking_relationship,mixed,pesenweek confessional teasing,pesenweek,"/Okiek_Kratz_1991_832_KS:p::/Okiek_Kratz_1991_832_KS::The child instigates a pesenta through disrespect and disobedience, but the actual threat originates in the adult's grudgeful anger. As pesenweek are told and then announced, blame shifts subtly from child to adult. ... it becomes a laughing matter. Adults are usually more than willing scapegoats, adding to the hilarity with other comments.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Okiek_Kratz_1991_832_KS:p,950
f5e0001627e8,within_group,EasternAfrica,Okiek,girls (initiates),audience (adults),kin_role,group,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,pesenweek confessional teasing,pesenweek,"/Okiek_Kratz_1990_458_KS:p::/Okiek_Kratz_1990_458_KS::The confessions can be actuely embarrassing for initiates and uproariously funny for the audience. Girls are asked to describe times when they refused to help with work or to go on errands for adults, and to name those involved.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Okiek_Kratz_1990_458_KS:p,949
cdf1ffae63dd,within_group,EasternAfrica,Okiek,macokor,kesenda,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Okiek_Huntingford_1951_35_KS:p::/Okiek_Huntingford_1951_35_KS::Between the sexes of the same generation there may be a perfectly innocuous intimacy, between brother and sister, between macokor, mama, and kesenda, which allows any kind of joking and language but stops short of actual sexual intercourse;",0.8,0.8,1,1,/Okiek_Huntingford_1951_35_KS:p,944
bde8209c1f92,within_group,EasternAfrica,Okiek,macokor,mama,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Okiek_Huntingford_1951_35_KS:p::/Okiek_Huntingford_1951_35_KS::Between the sexes of the same generation there may be a perfectly innocuous intimacy, between brother and sister, between macokor, mama, and kesenda, which allows any kind of joking and language but stops short of actual sexual intercourse;",0.8,0.8,1,1,/Okiek_Huntingford_1951_35_KS:p,943
3e78ea09123b,within_group,EasternAfrica,Okiek,mama,kesenda,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Okiek_Huntingford_1951_35_KS:p::/Okiek_Huntingford_1951_35_KS::Between the sexes of the same generation there may be a perfectly innocuous intimacy, between brother and sister, between macokor, mama, and kesenda, which allows any kind of joking and language but stops short of actual sexual intercourse;",0.8,0.8,1,1,/Okiek_Huntingford_1951_35_KS:p,945
8aad5f8d8e9d,within_group,EasternAfrica,Okiek,members of his own generation,members of his own generation,age_set,age_set,joking_relationship,symmetric,licensed liberties,"Kicupekei, kiurerencinkei, kiilencinkei","/Okiek_Huntingford_1951_35_KS:p::/Okiek_Huntingford_1951_35_KS::Between a man and the members of his own generation considerable latitude is allowed. As the Dorobo put it, Kicupekei, kiurerencinkei, kiilencinkei, ‘Kumin kon, ngut kum kamet’, amu ki lakok. (“We use bad language to one another, we play together, we say to each other, ‘May your father lie with you, or may you lie with your mother’, because we are children”, i. e. we are all of the same age.)",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Okiek_Huntingford_1951_35_KS:p,941
b9b0c8a04b67,within_group,EasternAfrica,Okiek,men,women,group,group,joking_relationship,mixed,attempts to introduce male priority (joking),,"/Okiek_Kratz_1990_457_KS:p::/Okiek_Kratz_1990_457_KS::Faced with the challenge of women's own corresponding ritual community and ceremonial organization, men sometimes try to introduce an evaluative hierarchy and male priority into the trial and secrets of initiation as well. Some may claim that their pain is greater, or that shared themes in secret songs were taken from them by women. However, these attempts are usually joking or rather half-hearted, in partial recognition of their absurdity.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/Okiek_Kratz_1990_457_KS:p,947
9321e351e187,within_group,EasternAfrica,Okiek,men,women's house of ritual,group,group,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,joking attacks,,"/Okiek_Kratz_1990_458_KS:p::/Okiek_Kratz_1990_458_KS::A certain ambivalence does emerge, however, as the situation highlights the contradiction and brings out the tension ordinarily defused by the dispersal of women. It emerges in the encouraging speeches made to girls, in side comments made during initiation ceremonies, and in joking attacks made by men on the women's house of ritual.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Okiek_Kratz_1990_458_KS:p,948
c04ef784098f,within_group,EasternAfrica,Shilluk,drummers,dancers,group,group,joking_relationship,symmetric,mock terror signal-response,,"/Shilluk_Howell_1946_26_KS:p::/Shilluk_Howell_1946_26_KS::On each day, at the close of the ceremonies the drummers whisked the drums from their holes and rushed them out of the village. This was a signal to the dancers to disperse, and they did so with great speed and with mock terror. The spirit of the late reth was said to return on the drums being drawn, and hands were clapped by the onlookers as before.",0.6,0.6,1,1,/Shilluk_Howell_1946_26_KS:p,962
fe1a68cb63b5,within_group,EasternAfrica,Somali,Abgâl,‘Am[unknown]udi,clan,clan,joking_relationship,mixed,contrast/banter between clans,,"/Somali_Cerulli_1959_92_KS:p::/Somali_Cerulli_1959_92_KS::Ya[unknown]q[unknown]ub: I am Abgâl, I am one of the men who have been governing because of their ancestors... 

. The ‘Am[unknown]udi singer invites the Ya[unknown]q[unknown]ub to leave the city to the citizens; and in turn he jokes about the occupations of the Somalis of the woodland, cameleers or cultivators of the small clearings (g[unknown]of) or in the service of the Government with a badge of a red ribbon at the neck, which was worn at that time (cf. here above, p. 52).",0.8,0.8,1,1,/Somali_Cerulli_1959_92_KS:p,970
9cce42c6a0b7,within_group,EasternAfrica,Somali,Garmagalä,Abg[unknown]al,clan,clan,joking_relationship,unclear,jokes about the tradition of Salamo,,"/Somali_Cerulli_1959_87_KS:p::/Somali_Cerulli_1959_87_KS::The Garmagalä, ‘freed,’ jokes about the tradition of Sal[unknown]amo in order to call his brothers and Negroes, therefore, like him, the proud Abg[unknown]al pastoralists.",0.6,0.6,1,1,/Somali_Cerulli_1959_87_KS:p,969
391846ace142,within_group,EasternAfrica,Somali,Hawiyya peoples,D[unknown]a[unknown]ud,group,clan,banter,unclear,praised for hospitality,,"/Somali_Cerulli_1959_96_KS:p::/Somali_Cerulli_1959_96_KS::The D[unknown]a[unknown]ud are praised for the regard that they show toward guests. Cf. above, p. 95, note 6.",0.5,0.5,1,1,/Somali_Cerulli_1959_96_KS:p,974
4fef63665490,within_group,EasternAfrica,Somali,Hawiyya peoples,D[unknown]ar[unknown]od,group,clan,teasing,non_symmetric,mocked for impulsiveness,,"/Somali_Cerulli_1959_96_KS:p::/Somali_Cerulli_1959_96_KS::The D[unknown]ar[unknown]od, foreign to the Hawiyya peoples, to whom the singer belonged, are mocked for their impulsive reactions.",0.7,0.7,1,1,/Somali_Cerulli_1959_96_KS:p,976
b513e86d675f,within_group,EasternAfrica,Somali,Hawiyya peoples,Hilli[unknown]bi,group,clan,teasing,non_symmetric,mocked for frequent use of purgative,,/Somali_Cerulli_1959_96_KS:p::/Somali_Cerulli_1959_96_KS::And finally in this verse a joke is made about the frequency with which the Hilli[unknown]bi have recourse to a purgative beverage (digo[unknown]o) in their villages.,0.6,0.6,1,1,/Somali_Cerulli_1959_96_KS:p,975
bd4adca6c884,within_group,EasternAfrica,Somali,Hawiyya peoples,Murúsada,group,clan,teasing,non_symmetric,stereotyped as werewolves / mocked,,/Somali_Cerulli_1959_96_KS:p::/Somali_Cerulli_1959_96_KS::The verse refers to the widespread belief that the Murúsada were werewolves; and thus here they seem to have stolen the ferocity of the wild beasts.,0.6,0.6,1,1,/Somali_Cerulli_1959_96_KS:p,973
531b38fb31bb,within_group,EasternAfrica,Somali,"Hawiyya tribes (Hawâdlä, B[unknown]adi ‘Addä, G[unknown]al-[unknown]gä‘el, M[unknown]obil[unknown]en, D[unknown]a[unknown]ud, Hilli[unknown]bi, Murúsada)",Gälädi (Digil),group,clan,joking_relationship,unclear,vaunt/poetic mockery,,"/Somali_Cerulli_1959_93_KS:p::/Somali_Cerulli_1959_93_KS::The middle stanzas are all devoted to Hawiyya tribes: [unknown] Haw[unknown]adlä, B[unknown]adi ‘Addä, G[unknown]al-[unknown]gä‘el, M[unknown]obil[unknown]en, D[unknown]a[unknown]ub, Hilli[unknown]bi, Murúsada. The last hemistich (minkäy ma koyto) imitates the dialect of the G[unknown]alädi.",0.75,0.75,1,1,/Somali_Cerulli_1959_93_KS:p,972
dbc873886759,within_group,EasternAfrica,Somali,nobles,commoners,group,group,insulting_license,non_symmetric,pejorative joking / degradation,"jareer, timo adag","/Somali_Helander_1988_160_KS:p::/Somali_Helander_1988_160_KS::The commoners are often talked about in contemptuous ways. Nearly all of the terms used for them are also pejoratives. ... Still, many of these terms, as well as their exegesis, are considered highly amusing by the nobles.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Somali_Helander_1988_160_KS:p,978
be95c56db392,within_group,NorthernAfrica,BerbersofMorocco,men,women,group,group,joking_relationship,symmetric,seasonal/carnival licensed joking,,"/BerbersofMorocco_Coon_1931_134_KS:p::/BerbersofMorocco_Coon_1931_134_KS::Grape picking is a season of carnival; the people sleep in the vineyards, and after and between intervals in weddings the men and women go up to the vineyards, where they joke, shout and shoot off guns, and play games; the men wrestle together, and the women and men form sides and throw stones at each other.",0.7,0.7,1,1,/BerbersofMorocco_Coon_1931_134_KS:p,494
6afb9eb121ba,within_group,NorthernAfrica,LibyanBedouin,aunt (Dhahab),niece (Kamla),kin_role,kin_role,banter,unclear,mocking / teasing,,"/LibyanBedouin_AbuLughod_1993_235_KS:p::/LibyanBedouin_AbuLughod_1993_235_KS::One evening, having recited some poetry and told some traditional tales for my benefit, Kamla’s aunt Dhahab turned to her niece and asked, “Hey Kamla, have you given Lila any songs?” ... Her aunt mocked her, “What’s this? You’ve become pious?” Everyone laughed as the old aunt continued, “God’s blessings! God’s blessings! So, you’re joining the ‘Beard Family’?”",0.4,0.4,1,1,/LibyanBedouin_AbuLughod_1993_235_KS:p,756
f1a2add4432e,within_group,NorthernAfrica,LibyanBedouin,co-wives,Safiyya,kin_role,person,teasing,non_symmetric,joked,,"/LibyanBedouin_AbuLughod_1986_227_KS:p::/LibyanBedouin_AbuLughod_1986_227_KS::She had recited the other poem in the presence of a number of women, including her co-wives, who said nothing. When I asked her what the poem meant, one co-wife joked, “Go ask ‘the man’ when he comes home.”",0.7,0.7,1,1,/LibyanBedouin_AbuLughod_1986_227_KS:p,753
ba4a161532ed,within_group,NorthernAfrica,LibyanBedouin,men,those linked through women (maternal relatives / affines),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,unclear,,,"/LibyanBedouin_Peters_1990_71_KS:p::/LibyanBedouin_Peters_1990_71_KS::Women, the marriage links they create, the forms of relationships that derive from
marriage, the right of men to joke with others 72 by virtue of a link through
women, their rights to demand favours of men who are not their agnates, their
right to expect contributions to bridewealth or blood money from maternal
relatives — all these, and other forms of behaviour, have been dealt with as
patterns of behaviour which interfere with or disturb agnation, or that cut
across lineage affiliations.",0.7,0.7,1,1,/LibyanBedouin_Peters_1990_71_KS:p,761
a948fff046c5,within_group,NorthernAfrica,LibyanBedouin,mother's brother,sister's son,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,characteristically easy relationship,,"/LibyanBedouin_Peters_1990_72_KS:p::/LibyanBedouin_Peters_1990_72_KS::Their reasoning, however, is that relationships with the mother’s brother are characteristically easy, that they are not demanding, that there is much joking in them, and that to kill within this range is, in terms of the general pattern of behaviour, stupid.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/LibyanBedouin_Peters_1990_72_KS:p,762
5857af7d4b00,within_group,NorthernAfrica,LibyanBedouin,other women in the community,Safiyya,group,person,teasing,non_symmetric,nervous joking,,"/LibyanBedouin_AbuLughod_1986_227_KS:p::/LibyanBedouin_AbuLughod_1986_227_KS::The taped song elicited both the nervous joking of people embarrassed by a display of immodesty and the sympathetic noises of people who cared, understood her situation, and were moved by her recitation.",0.6,0.6,1,1,/LibyanBedouin_AbuLughod_1986_227_KS:p,754
579fdd155eb0,within_group,NorthernAfrica,LibyanBedouin,sister’s son,mother’s brother,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,The sister’s son-mother’s brother relationship,,"/LibyanBedouin_Peters_1990_201_KS:p::/LibyanBedouin_Peters_1990_201_KS::With the mother’s brother a man enjoys all the freedoms he is denied in his relationships with his father and father’s brother. He is permitted to joke with him; they indulge in exchanges of obscenities. ... Small boys pursue their mother’s brother, feign to kick or strike him, and he takes it all in good part.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/LibyanBedouin_Peters_1990_201_KS:p,760
3f0053de73fc,within_group,NorthernAfrica,LibyanBedouin,women,husbands,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,,,/LibyanBedouin_AbuLughod_1986_222_KS:p::/LibyanBedouin_AbuLughod_1986_222_KS::Women make jokes to suggest detachment from and lack of interest in their husbands.,0.7,0.7,1,1,/LibyanBedouin_AbuLughod_1986_222_KS:p,752
e16cf2de2dbc,within_group,NorthernAfrica,LibyanBedouin,young men,father,group,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relations,,"/LibyanBedouin_Mohsen_1974_130_KS:p::/LibyanBedouin_Mohsen_1974_130_KS::In their joking relations, young men usually refer to eachother’s father as being a dog or a theif or a cheat, but none could refer to the mother.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/LibyanBedouin_Mohsen_1974_130_KS:p,757
9067088aa804,within_group,NorthernAfrica,LibyanBedouin,young men,mother,group,kin_role,avoidance,symmetric,taboo / prohibition,,"/LibyanBedouin_Mohsen_1974_130_KS:p::/LibyanBedouin_Mohsen_1974_130_KS::In their joking relations, young men usually refer to eachother’s father as being a dog or a theif or a cheat, but none could refer to the mother.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/LibyanBedouin_Mohsen_1974_130_KS:p,758
59de2afd1083,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Nubians,boys and young men,the groom,age_set,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,,,"/Nubians_Callender_1971_61_KS:p::/Nubians_Callender_1971_61_KS::After the feast, boys and young men teased the groom by forcing him to stand. One of them walked up to him and offered a standard greeting, Salaama aleykam, ya arts. Greeted thus, he rose to his feet to respond properly, and could not sit again until the greeter told him to do so.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Nubians_Callender_1971_61_KS:p,906
919a833bb7e1,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Nubians,fiancé,engaged girl,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,mixed,forbidden contact treated as a joke,,"/Nubians_Callender_1971_50_KS:p::/Nubians_Callender_1971_50_KS::Whatever the rationale underlying this prohibition, most men treated the custom as a joke and plotted to evade the rule. This often turned into a game, with some girls trying to help him while others warned his fiancée.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Nubians_Callender_1971_50_KS:p,905
6fa1d85459ef,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Nubians,groom,bride,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,mixed,agilbaygit (opening the mouth) contest,Agilbaygit,"/Nubians_Callender_1971_66_KS:p::/Nubians_Callender_1971_66_KS::Agilbaygit means literally “opening the mouth.” Most Kenuz seemed to view the transaction in these terms. Like the groom's opening his wife's shogga on their wedding night, it was regarded as a contest. A man might try to trick his wife into speaking by asking her how much money she wanted, and she had been warned to respond to any questions with gestures. A woman who spoke prematurely was teased.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Nubians_Callender_1971_66_KS:p,907
aa1303a09e35,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Nubians,the groups of women,the groups of men,group,group,joking_relationship,symmetric,ṣabāhiyya / 'opening of the mouth' joking,ṣabāhiyya,"/Nubians_AlKatsha_1978_183_KS:p::/Nubians_AlKatsha_1978_183_KS::While the “opening of the mouth” ceremony was going on, much joking and loud laughter issued from both the groups of women and men. Sometimes, peepholes had been made into the bridal quarters so that watchers could report to the group how long the girl was able to bargain and thus delay consummation.",0.7,0.7,1,1,/Nubians_AlKatsha_1978_183_KS:p,904
96dc639fd79f,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Nubians,the two families,the two families,lineage,lineage,joking_relationship,symmetric,mock battle between the two families,,"/Nubians_Kennedy_1977_40_KS:p::/Nubians_Kennedy_1977_40_KS::Dancing recommenced near sunset, the two families involved in the wedding each slaughtered a sheep, and still another procession moved from the groom's to the bride's house amid great shouting, gunshot, and drumming. (In earlier times there was at this point a mock battle between the two families in which the groom and his escort were thrashed with whips.)",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Nubians_Kennedy_1977_40_KS:p,913
f50c85563574,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Nubians,the women charged with bringing a bride to her husband,the groom,group,person,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,substitution prank / horseplay,,"/Nubians_Callender_1971_67_KS:p::/Nubians_Callender_1971_67_KS::Joking and horseplay were also prominent features of the days before the sebu. The women charged with bringing a bride to her husband on the night after their wedding sometimes substituted a boy, whom they had wrapped in a shogga.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Nubians_Callender_1971_67_KS:p,909
d7fa56ed4867,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Nubians,women,newly-married couples,group,group,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,eavesdropping / teasing,,"/Nubians_Callender_1971_67_KS:p::/Nubians_Callender_1971_67_KS::Women eavesdropped on newly-married couples, particularly on their first night, trying to learn how much time elapsed before the bride spoke, and what her first words were.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Nubians_Callender_1971_67_KS:p,911
e8f6f5f6b9bb,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Nubians,women,women,group,group,joking_relationship,symmetric,standard jokes among women,,"/Nubians_Callender_1971_66_KS:p::/Nubians_Callender_1971_66_KS::This practice was also a common source of amusement and the subject of standard jokes among women, such as speculating about how much agilbaygit a man might give before he realized that he had married a woman who was dumb.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/Nubians_Callender_1971_66_KS:p,908
c7b1cd2d7321,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Nubians,young men,the groom,group,person,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,slipper-theft ransom / spying,,"/Nubians_Callender_1971_67_KS:p::/Nubians_Callender_1971_67_KS::Young men used to climb up to push these aside and spy on the pair. They also tried to steal the couples' slippers, which were left outside the door at night—noiselessly slipping up to the door for this purpose, or standing outside the courtyard wall with a fishing rod and hook. If they succeeded, the groom had to give them pigeons as ransom before they would return the slippers.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/Nubians_Callender_1971_67_KS:p,910
625dc4b9146a,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Shluh,am[unknown]ar,men who 'want to be' am[unknown]ar (the am[unknown]ar manqu␀e9),group,group,teasing,non_symmetric,"""but he wants to be one"" teasing",,"/Shluh_Hatt_1993_452_KS:p::/Shluh_Hatt_1993_452_KS::Invariably, my question was announced to the assembly and the comment made that “no, he isne isn't an am[unknown]ar , but he wants to be one”. This, needless to say, provoked great rounds of laughter, and most of all from the am[unknown]ar manqu␀e9 .",0.6,0.6,1,1,/Shluh_Hatt_1993_452_KS:p,963
3b45deced309,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,Aġouh-en-Tehele,Ise□emaren,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking with; fixed gift-exchange,,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_106_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_106_KS::Other vassal clans and in particular the Aġouh-en-Tehele have joking with noble clans and also with clans of the Ise□emaren class. Between the Aġouh-en-Tehele and the Ise□emaren clans a fixed rule of gift-exchange is observed when they visit each other.,0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_106_KS:p,1031
f0e9522c5d7d,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,Aġouh-en-Tehele,noble clans,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking with,,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_106_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_106_KS::Other vassal clans and in particular the Aġouh-en-Tehele have joking with noble clans and also with clans of the Ise□emaren class.,0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_106_KS:p,1030
79102c1f85c4,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,Aġuh-en-tehlé,Ise□emaren,clan,group,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_402_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_402_KS::Most noble Tuareg and vassals will joke with the Ise□emaren, and there is a particular strong joking relationship between the vassal tribe Aġuh-en-tehlé and the Ise□emaren tribes. Such joking relationships are functionally related to joking relationships between relatives and in-laws and a fixed gift-exchange is observed between Aġuh-en-tehlé Tuareg and Ise□emaren Tuareg when they visit each other.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_402_KS:p,1045
bf379b8487a1,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,Aġuh-en-tehlé,Kel □ela (noble Kel □ela Tuareg),clan,group,joking_relationship,symmetric,,tebbillant,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_402_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_402_KS::The Aġuh-en-tehlé tribe has the most intense joking relationship to the noble Kel □ela Tuareg of all vassals. These vassals and the noble Kel □ela engage in shamfights (tebbillant) exactly in the same way as cross-cousins.,0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_402_KS:p,1046
b0b8f00d20ae,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,Dag □ali,brothers and sisters-in-law,clan,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_402_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_402_KS::People of the Dag □ali to-day have no joking relationships with anyone beyond cross-cousins and brothers and sisters-in-law (cf. p. f., ff.).",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_402_KS:p,1042
96e5f3bd3b91,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,Dag □ali,cross-cousins,clan,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_402_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_402_KS::People of the Dag □ali to-day have no joking relationships with anyone beyond cross-cousins and brothers and sisters-in-law (cf. p. f., ff.).",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_402_KS:p,1041
48e858256ee3,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,Ise□emaren,noble Tuareg,group,group,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_402_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_402_KS::Most noble Tuareg and vassals will joke with the Ise□emaren, and there is a particular strong joking relationship between the vassal tribe Aġuh-en-tehlé and the Ise□emaren tribes.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_402_KS:p,1043
b63c025a4eb4,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,Ise□emaren,vassals,group,group,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_402_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_402_KS::Most noble Tuareg and vassals will joke with the Ise□emaren, and there is a particular strong joking relationship between the vassal tribe Aġuh-en-tehlé and the Ise□emaren tribes.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_402_KS:p,1044
df169bde95b0,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,Tuareg,slaves,ethnic group,caste,joking_relationship,mixed,joking relationship,,"/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_445_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_445_KS::In spite of the kinship pattern on which the relationships of Tuareg slaves and slave-owners is based, it is most noteworthy that there is always latent joking between them, as there is between many different classes and castes in Tuareg society. On certain occasions a Tuareg slave owner will joke freely even with his young or older slaves, though he will never joke with his children who have to show their father the outmost respect.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_445_KS:p,1053
2dbb1dac28bc,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,a man inheriting his parents' kinship status,classificatory cross-cousins,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationships,,"/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_456_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_456_KS::it is normal for a man to inherit the kinship status of his parents on their death, in the way that he adopts their joking relationships with their classificatory cross-cousins, but he will never inherit a joking relationship with the true cross-cousins of his deceased father and mother.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_456_KS:p,1059
6c9190ba77a9,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,abobaz,abobaz,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationships with cousins,abobaz,"/Tuareg_Rasmussen_2010_6_KS:p::/Tuareg_Rasmussen_2010_6_KS::The most frequently heard kinship term is abobaz, denoting “cousin,” used in a classificatory sense. ... There are joking relationships with cousins; relationships with affines are characterized by extreme reserve.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tuareg_Rasmussen_2010_6_KS:p,1090
8e4c3a740ac0,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,all Tuareg,slaves,ethnic group,caste,joking_relationship,symmetric,,abubaz (ibubazen),"/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_429_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_429_KS::This is true also of present day joking between noble Tuareg and vassals, and between all Tuareg and various black-skinned people such as the Imeggedezen and even slaves.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_429_KS:p,1050
abe5c87baa6d,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,blacksmiths,other Tuareg (general community),caste,group,banter,unclear,licensed liberties,,"/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1991_43_KS:p::/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1991_43_KS::All Tuareg, except blacksmiths who are free from constraint of reserve and dignity, attempt to measure words, and avoid commenting about everything happening around them.",0.5,0.5,1,1,/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1991_43_KS:p,1082
5832fc9c7b8b,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,brothers and sisters-in-law,brothers and sisters-in-law,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_96_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_96_KS::A joking relationship similar to that of cross-cousins and of brothers and sisters-in-law, including sham-fights and minor “thefts”, existed between all noble Tuareg and all vassals of the Kel Ferouan group.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_96_KS:p,1036
a8f1893b76fc,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,brothers-in-law,sisters-in-law,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,tehan□e□it; adélen,"/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_89_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_89_KS::Among the Kel Ferouan joking between cross-cousins goes together with joking between brothers-in-law and sisters-in-law, and also with avoidance between children-in-law and parents-in-law. || /Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_454_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_454_KS::The same type of joking exists between brothers-in-law and sisters-in-law in Ahaggar and in Ayr.",0.9,0.9,2,2,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_89_KS:p; /Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_454_KS:p,1033;1056
5398a90642c4,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,certain vassal tribes,tribes of the Ise□emaren class,clan,clan,joking_relationship,unclear,institutional joking,,"/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_445_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_445_KS::If on the other hand two tribes have institutional joking with one another, as is the case with certain vassal tribes in relation to tribes of the Ise□emaren class, there will also be joking between their slaves.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_445_KS:p,1052
ee62900c857b,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,cousins,cousins,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Tuareg_Rasmussen_2004_328_KS:p::/Tuareg_Rasmussen_2004_328_KS::many Tuareg described smiths as ‘like women … also like cousins (who practise a joking relationship and also may potentially marry) … you must not get angry at them, but (rather) joke with them’.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/Tuareg_Rasmussen_2004_328_KS:p,1089
47d7a19b939a,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,cross-cousins,brothers and sisters-in-law,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,adélen,adélen; address as abubaz (ibubazen),"/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_429_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_429_KS::A joking-relationship (adélen) related to that of cross-cousins and brothers and sisters-in-law, including sham-fights and minor “thefts”, existed between all noble Tuareg and all vassals of the Kel Ferwan group.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_429_KS:p,1051
dfe7e4ac6363,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,cross-cousins,cross-cousins,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationship,,"/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_106_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_106_KS::The kinship system in Ahaggar is different from that in Air (fig. 8–9), but the rules of kinship behaviour are very much the same. Joking relationship between cross-cousins, and joking and avoidance between in-laws are practised also in Ahaggar. || /Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_89_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_89_KS::Finally cross-cousin marriage is considered the best type of marriage because institutional joking exists between cross-cousins. || /Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_96_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_96_KS::A joking relationship similar to that of cross-cousins and of brothers and sisters-in-law, including sham-fights and minor “thefts”, existed between all noble Tuareg and all vassals of the Kel Ferouan group. || /Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_455_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_455_KS::This type of joking is similar to that of cross-cousins. ... In addition, cross-cousins, as mentioned above, can take each other's property.",0.887,0.95,4,4,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_106_KS:p; /Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_89_KS:p; /Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_96_KS:p; /Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_455_KS:p,1028;1032;1035;1058
161c012da8dd,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,cross-cousins,each other,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_465_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_465_KS::Parallel cousins are termed brothers and sisters, while there is a particular term for cross-cousins and between them an institutional joking relationship which facilitates contact and marriage. || /Tuareg_Rasmussen_1992_353_KS:p::/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1992_353_KS::But joking is also practiced between cross-cousins, who are potential marriage partners, and in certain contexts smiths and former slaves are said to be “like cousins to nobles.”",0.95,0.95,2,2,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_465_KS:p; /Tuareg_Rasmussen_1992_353_KS:p,1061;1086
d3bd71c35c4a,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,cross-cousins,potential marriage partners (cross-cousins),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking with,,"/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1991_41_KS:p::/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1991_41_KS::One is not supposed to express anger directly to anyone, but in particular, in respect toward affines and in joking with cross-cousins, potential marriage partners.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1991_41_KS:p,1079
3acdefd8e64b,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,drum-chief,vassals of the Kel Ferouan group,person,group,joking_relationship,mixed,,,"/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_96_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_96_KS::Like other noble Tuareg he would not only associate with the vassals on intimate terms, but he would also joke with them. ... although joking was reciprocal it is said that the drum-chief could stop joking, in which case the vassals would “be afraid of him”.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_96_KS:p,1039
e67edf1378c2,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,future affines,groom,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,Tagenagen,"/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1991_43_KS:p::/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1991_43_KS::The groom asked his sister where the bride was in Tagenagen because he did not wish to be understood by everyone present; the spectators included many future affines, toward whom he would soon practice extreme respect, but with whom he now could joke.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1991_43_KS:p,1080
a021f08021a4,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,husband,mother's brother's wife,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,,,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_474_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_474_KS::In Ayr the husband changes his joking relations with mother's brother's wife into avoidance.,0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_474_KS:p,1072
5690083da4f2,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,husband's family,wife's family,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_468_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_468_KS::When two people divorce they continue to have each other's relations as in-laws with whom they have joking and avoidance exactly as if they were still married, and the children continue to have their full rights within both families.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_468_KS:p,1069
d7e3814110db,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,in-laws,in-laws,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking and avoidance,,"/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_106_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_106_KS::The kinship system in Ahaggar is different from that in Air (fig. 8–9), but the rules of kinship behaviour are very much the same. Joking relationship between cross-cousins, and joking and avoidance between in-laws are practised also in Ahaggar.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_106_KS:p,1029
4efbc5319a0d,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,male cross-cousin,female cross-cousin,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,institutional joking between cross-cousins; institutional joking relationship,,"/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_459_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_459_KS::The institutionial joking relationship between cross-cousins is considered an important institution in preventing conflicts between married partners. ... There is no doubt at all that cross-cousins of both sexes are extremely good friends. They do not joke just to annoy each other, and when joking they laugh and feel very pleased. || /Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_466_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_466_KS::In case of cross-cousins of different sex, the difference in age does not seem to diminish joking between them, and it is most interesting that the Kel Regulation Of Ferwan Tuareg have an institutional joking relationship in the case of a Marriage mother's brother's wife (cf. p. ).",0.9,0.9,2,2,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_459_KS:p; /Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_466_KS:p,1060;1064
3efe7130ddc8,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,male cross-cousin (groom),female cross-cousin (bride),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationship,,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_474_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_474_KS::Until their marriage the groom and the bride had a joking relationship to one another.,0.95,0.95,1,1,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_474_KS:p,1071
3203d7d5e893,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,male president,ahal attendees,group,group,insulting_license,non_symmetric,inflict forfeits as a joke,ahal,"/Tuareg_Lhote_1944_148_KS:p::/Tuareg_Lhote_1944_148_KS::When everyone has arrived, they elect a male president and a female president, whose job is to see that the usages and customs of the ahal are respected and, as a joke, to inflict forfeits on those who bring on themselves the recriminations of the others.",0.75,0.75,1,1,/Tuareg_Lhote_1944_148_KS:p,1027
6b87ad03de62,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,man,brothers-in-law,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking with brothers-in-law,,"/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_466_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_466_KS::If a man marries a woman with whom he has no blood relationship he will practise joking with his brothers-in-law and sisters-in-law, and avoidance towards his parents-in-law.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_466_KS:p,1066
c88e5612f79a,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,man,mother's brother's wife,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,institutional joking with mother's brother's wife,,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_89_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_89_KS::It is also interesting that in most Kel Ferouan clans there is institutional joking between a man and his mother's brother's wife. || /Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_466_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_466_KS::it is most interesting that the Kel Regulation Of Ferwan Tuareg have an institutional joking relationship in the case of a Marriage mother's brother's wife ... it seems as if joking between a man and his mother's brother's wife serves the purpose of preventing conflicts and creating a bond of friendship between a man and his future mother-in-law.,0.925,0.95,2,2,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_89_KS:p; /Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_466_KS:p,1034;1065
e47ceb539098,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,man,sisters-in-law,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking with sisters-in-law,,"/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_466_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_466_KS::If a man marries a woman with whom he has no blood relationship he will practise joking with his brothers-in-law and sisters-in-law, and avoidance towards his parents-in-law.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_466_KS:p,1067
8904ad165b85,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,maternal uncles,small children,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,play and joke,,"/Tuareg_Rasmussen_2010_7_KS:p::/Tuareg_Rasmussen_2010_7_KS::Fathers are considered disciplinarians, yet other men, particularly maternal uncles, often play and joke with small children.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tuareg_Rasmussen_2010_7_KS:p,1091
d09d3f1b02d9,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,mother's brother's wife,husband's sisters' children,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,mixed,adélen (institutional joking relationship),adélen,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_455_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_455_KS::For in Ayr there is an institutional joking relationship (adélen) with the wives of mother's brothers including the spouses of the classificatory mother's brothers. This type of joking is similar to that of cross-cousins. The initiative for joking is taken by the mother's brother's wife who begins to tease her husband's sisters' children even when they are babies.,0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_455_KS:p,1057
05a1ff547132,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,mother-in-law,wife,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,unclear,joking (vs avoidance),,"/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_475_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_475_KS::There will then already be a relationship of respect between husband and wife, a respect relationship to brothers-in-law and sisters-in-law which continues unchanged, and in Ayr the relationship to the mother-in-law does not switch from joking into avoidance.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_475_KS:p,1075
294cc37df8a5,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,newly-married daughter,mother-in-law,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,,,"/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1991_40_KS:p::/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1991_40_KS::So the verses encapsulate the contemporary conflict between economics and descent, and division between rich and poor nobility. A man loses his material wealth in the long run, and also his self-respect, if he doesn't please his mother-in-law through gifts. Yet official authority figures such as mothers-in-law and marabouts are also the butt of jokes and vengeful fantasies; they are put down indirectly in verbal art of folktales, possession songs, and riddles. The daughter is caught in the middle of the cross-fire between, first her suitor, the marabout, and her parents, and later, between her husband and her mother. She and her mother feed the husband, it is true, but the latter is nonetheless subjected to continual demands for millet and household implements from his mother-in-law, almost at whim. The newly-married daughter cannot please both her new husband and her mother.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1991_40_KS:p,1076
07e27d8e7b93,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,newly-married husband,brothers-in-law,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_474_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_474_KS::To other brothers-in-law and sisters-in-law, joking is adopted only after marriage.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_474_KS:p,1073
b51386366750,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,newly-married wife,sisters-in-law,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_474_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_474_KS::To other brothers-in-law and sisters-in-law, joking is adopted only after marriage.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_474_KS:p,1074
b724d35bccec,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,noble Tuareg,slaves,patronym,caste,joking_relationship,unclear,,,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_445_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_445_KS::The noble Tuareg may also joke with slaves but seem less inclined to do so than Tuareg of other classes.,0.85,0.85,1,1,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_445_KS:p,1054
174397738fbd,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,noble Tuareg,vassals of the Kel Ferouan group,group,group,joking_relationship,mixed,,,"/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_96_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_96_KS::A joking relationship similar to that of cross-cousins and of brothers and sisters-in-law, including sham-fights and minor “thefts”, existed between all noble Tuareg and all vassals of the Kel Ferouan group.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_96_KS:p,1038
932b8d1b31f3,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,noble Tuareg,vassals of the Kel Ferwan group,caste,caste,joking_relationship,mixed,adélen,adélen; address as abubaz (ibubazen),"/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_429_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_429_KS::A joking-relationship (adélen) related to that of cross-cousins and brothers and sisters-in-law, including sham-fights and minor “thefts”, existed between all noble Tuareg and all vassals of the Kel Ferwan group.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_429_KS:p,1048
28ced814a3a4,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,nobles,former slaves,group,group,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1992_353_KS:p::/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1992_353_KS::This ritual mutual and self-debasement evokes servitude yet simultaneously separates and brings together the different social segments... Joking and familiarity are practiced between nobles, smiths, and former slaves, who are not supposed to intermarry.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1992_353_KS:p,1084
9c4f901217f8,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,nobles,smiths,group,caste,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1992_353_KS:p::/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1992_353_KS::Next, men of diverse origins approach the circle of singing smiths, each offering money, tea, sugar, and millet, while smiths mock each social segment. The men perform dances identified with different social strata; for example, noble men do a dance called “Foot, do nothing,” as smiths lament, “feet without shoes,” and sing “I do not want jealousy of slaves or smiths.”",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1992_353_KS:p,1083
13be62708a5a,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,nobles,smiths,caste,caste,joking_relationship,symmetric,protected people / joking relationship,,"/Tuareg_Rasmussen_2004_328_KS:p::/Tuareg_Rasmussen_2004_328_KS::Noble violence against smiths is traditionally rare because they are a protected people against whom it is not appropriate to express anger without denigrating oneself. In my early field research in the 1970s and 80s, many Tuareg described smiths as ‘like women … also like cousins (who practise a joking relationship and also may potentially marry) … you must not get angry at them, but (rather) joke with them’.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tuareg_Rasmussen_2004_328_KS:p,1088
775339d6927f,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,parents-in-law,children-in-law,kin_role,kin_role,avoidance,symmetric,,,"/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_96_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_96_KS::These terms are always used reciprocally, and parents-in-law and children-in-law will avoid each other accordingly.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_96_KS:p,1037
f1013d8cb28d,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,parents-in-law,children-in-law,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,mixed,joking and avoidance between in-laws,,"/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_466_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_466_KS::Joking and avoidance between Tuareg in-laws no doubt constitute a fixed system of behaviour adapted to a particular marriage type ... he will practise joking with his brothers-in-law and sisters-in-law, and avoidance towards his parents-in-law.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_466_KS:p,1068
567f5c2a155c,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,people in terejjek,each other,group,group,joking_relationship,symmetric,terejjek (courtship) joking,terejjek,"/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_465_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_465_KS::The Tuareg seem to admit that one function of joking between cross-cousins is to promote marriages between them, and they say that in the period during which a man and a woman are in love with each other before marriage — a period which is known to the Kel Ferwan Tuareg as terejjek — there will always be joking between them even though the two persons are unrelated.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_465_KS:p,1062
249c91cae2b0,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,person,brothers and sisters-in-law,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_465_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_465_KS::When a man marries a cross-cousin, his cross-cousins will become brothers and sisters-in-law with whom joking continues, and there will always be joking with the brothers and sisters of a spouse unless a man marries a particular kinswoman (cf. p. ).",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_465_KS:p,1063
dbe46be523b2,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,smiths,former slaves,caste,group,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1992_353_KS:p::/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1992_353_KS::The different social segments come together genially to exaggerate their affection... Joking and familiarity are practiced between nobles, smiths, and former slaves, who are not supposed to intermarry.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1992_353_KS:p,1085
e6588736845e,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,smiths,"nobles (""like cousins to nobles"")",caste,group,joking_relationship,unclear,like cousins,,"/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1992_353_KS:p::/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1992_353_KS::But joking is also practiced between cross-cousins, who are potential marriage partners, and in certain contexts smiths and former slaves are said to be “like cousins to nobles.”",0.85,0.85,1,1,/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1992_353_KS:p,1087
d7a249068c57,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,the fathers of the groom,the fathers of the bride,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,institutional joking relationship,,"/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_470_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_470_KS::the fathers of the groom and the bride are cross-cousins and/or brothers-in-law, and consequently there is an institutional joking relationship between them.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_470_KS:p,1070
1e0e1572d505,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,true cross-cousins,classificatory cross-cousins,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,tehan□e□it / adélen,tehan□e□it; adélen,"/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_454_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_454_KS::There is a joking relationship (tehan□e□it in Ahaggar, adélen in Ayr) between all true and classificatory cross-cousins. This joking relationship allows free talk about sexual matters, and when cros-cousins tease each other they frequently refer to matters of sexual life, e.g., “You sleep with old slave women!”",0.95,0.95,1,1,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_454_KS:p,1055
7aba89d9701d,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,vassals,Kel □ela (noble Kel □ela Tuareg),group,group,joking_relationship,symmetric,shamfights,tebbillant,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_402_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_402_KS::These vassals and the noble Kel □ela engage in shamfights (tebbillant) exactly in the same way as cross-cousins.,0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_402_KS:p,1047
63951f5e68ca,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,vassals of the Kel Ferouan group,noble Tuareg of the Kel Fadei,group,group,avoidance,symmetric,,,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_96_KS:p::/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_96_KS::Thus there would be no joking between vassals of the Kel Ferouan group and noble Tuareg of the Kel Fadei. In this latter case there would be avoidance or strict reserve.,0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_96_KS:p,1040
5046aef7b11a,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,women,imalayaden,group,group,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,,imalayaden,"/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1991_40_KS:p::/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1991_40_KS::It says, in effect, “Women, your problems are irritating, but nothing in comparison to the real problem: male outsiders in your tent.” The term used here for young men, imalayaden, is pejorative; it connotes those who deceive women and make false promises to marry, using misleading economic claims to do so.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1991_40_KS:p,1078
a830d1102a3a,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,women,marabouts,group,caste,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,,,"/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1991_40_KS:p::/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1991_40_KS::It also expresses a resentment toward authority figures (marabouts and noble men), who command official respect and yet do not always fulfill their obligations toward women. ... Yet official authority figures such as mothers-in-law and marabouts are also the butt of jokes and vengeful fantasies; they are put down indirectly in verbal art of folktales, possession songs, and riddles.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1991_40_KS:p,1077
d7b9e27775ef,within_group,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,women,men,group,group,insulting_license,non_symmetric,ridicule,,"/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1991_43_KS:p::/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1991_43_KS::On the other hand, men dread women's ridicule because they hold power over men's personal esteem in public reputation as proclaimed in poetry and song, as well as the power to throw them out of the domestic tent.",0.7,0.7,1,1,/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1991_43_KS:p,1081
0eea5c12150f,within_group,SouthernAfrica,bemba,Banungwe,imipashi,clan,group,joking_relationship,symmetric,joke with,ukucena,"/bemba_richards_337_sv:p::/bemba_richards_337_sv::He said it was good to joke with the imipashi as men do with members of the opposite clan (Banungwe) and say to them after calling on them, 'You are the Mpende clan ( mpende is a big fish that eats little ones, mpepe ). ... and it is good to joke ( ukucena ) with the imipashi in this way.'",0.75,0.75,1,1,/bemba_richards_337_sv:p,1116
c883a9bb4412,within_group,SouthernAfrica,bemba,Banungwe,opposite clans (Banungwe),clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,opposite clans,Banungwe,"/bemba_richards_188_sv:p::/bemba_richards_188_sv::Opposite clans performing reciprocal ritual duties and standing in a joking relationship to each other have been reported among several of the Central Comparative Evidence Bantu tribes, 1 but the distribution of this type of clan organization in Central and East Africa is as yet unknown, and the whole phenomenon has never been fully described to my knowledge. I am therefore publishing the following notes on the Banungwe, 2 or opposite clans of the Bemba of N.E. Rhodesia in the hope of eliciting further comparative information on the subject.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/bemba_richards_188_sv:p,1098
ad9ddb956fbe,within_group,SouthernAfrica,bemba,Wild Pig clan (ngulube),royal clan (tree clan ngoma),clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,banungwe,banungwe,"/bemba_richards_192_sv:p::/bemba_richards_192_sv::Thus Chief Matipa on Cilubi Island is buried by the head of the Wild Pig clan ( ngulube ) who are banungwe of the royal clan of that island, which is the tree clan ( ngoma ),",0.9,0.9,1,1,/bemba_richards_192_sv:p,1110
09801aabfdcf,within_group,SouthernAfrica,bemba,a man,his wife's sisters,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,/bemba_richards_131_sv:p::/bemba_richards_131_sv::There are no taboos between a man and his wife’s sisters. He laughs and jokes with them because he may one day marry them.,0.9,0.9,1,1,/bemba_richards_131_sv:p,1096
d999611874fd,within_group,SouthernAfrica,bemba,animal clans,iron people,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,reciprocal relations / banungwe,banungwe,"/bemba_richards_190a_sv:p::/bemba_richards_190a_sv::The animal clans also have reciprocal relations not only with the iron people, but with any mukoa named after an animal smaller than itself which forms its prey ( e.g., the lion and goat)...",0.9,0.9,1,1,/bemba_richards_190a_sv:p,1105
6bd4910fc0b9,within_group,SouthernAfrica,bemba,banungwe (opposite clans),mourning family (mourners),group,group,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,banungwe duties / funeral taunting,banungwe,"/bemba_richards_190a_sv:p::/bemba_richards_190a_sv::The banungwe of a dead man’s family both help and harass him at the burial ceremony.... They also taunt them at the funeral, irritate them and make jokes.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/bemba_richards_190a_sv:p,1108
a1a4e3390fcd,within_group,SouthernAfrica,bemba,crocodile clan,fish clan,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,banungwe / joking antagonism,Mwe mulwani mwe!,"/bemba_richards_190_sv:p::/bemba_richards_190_sv::Banungwe regularly address each other as ‘You, my enemy!’ ( Mwe mulwani mwe! ) and delight in laughter at the habits of the objects, whether animal or plant, of the opposite clan. A member of the crocodile clan will scoff at one of the fish people and deride him as, ‘You! You are just my food ration!’ ( poso, the word used for a government ration given to employees).",0.9,0.9,1,1,/bemba_richards_190_sv:p,1099
58955ab94af3,within_group,SouthernAfrica,bemba,dog clan,Paramount Chief,clan,person,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,protective joking performance,,"/bemba_richards_190_sv:p::/bemba_richards_190_sv::On such occasions the Paramount is protected by the head of the dog clan, who has to dance in front of him, pretending to bark in defence of his master.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/bemba_richards_190_sv:p,1101
ace42d5c5b6b,within_group,SouthernAfrica,bemba,fish clan,Paramount Chief,clan,person,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,formalized court joking / dramatized antagonism,mukabilo,"/bemba_richards_190_sv:p::/bemba_richards_190_sv::At the court of the Paramount Chief this type of joking relationship has been formalized, and the head of the fish clan, a mukabilo, or hereditary priest of the tribal ancestral spirits, must dramatize the joking antagonism between the two clans by appearing in special regalia each time he visits the chief, and threatening him with a broad-bladed fishing spear.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/bemba_richards_190_sv:p,1100
f9ffed0c43aa,within_group,SouthernAfrica,bemba,fish clan,rain people,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,banungwe,banungwe,"/bemba_richards_190a_sv:p::/bemba_richards_190a_sv::The fish clan members are also banungwe to the rain people, since fish only spawn when the rain has begun to fall.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/bemba_richards_190a_sv:p,1103
39aadb803b34,within_group,SouthernAfrica,bemba,iron,elephant,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,banungwe,banungwe,"/bemba_richards_193_sv:p::/bemba_richards_193_sv::Then he stops to think and adds more names, and then proceeds to argue with himself, for instance, that if iron is [p. (b)] hostile to the elephant, it must be the enemy of the leopard, the wild pig or the lion too.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/bemba_richards_193_sv:p,1111
f5d604bf87f2,within_group,SouthernAfrica,bemba,iron,leopard,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,banungwe,banungwe,"/bemba_richards_193_sv:p::/bemba_richards_193_sv::Then he stops to think and adds more names, and then proceeds to argue with himself, for instance, that if iron is [p. (b)] hostile to the elephant, it must be the enemy of the leopard, the wild pig or the lion too.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/bemba_richards_193_sv:p,1112
c9c4d3634323,within_group,SouthernAfrica,bemba,iron,lion,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,banungwe,banungwe,"/bemba_richards_193_sv:p::/bemba_richards_193_sv::Then he stops to think and adds more names, and then proceeds to argue with himself, for instance, that if iron is [p. (b)] hostile to the elephant, it must be the enemy of the leopard, the wild pig or the lion too.",0.75,0.75,1,1,/bemba_richards_193_sv:p,1114
aa11ef19f9b2,within_group,SouthernAfrica,bemba,iron,the wild pig,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,banungwe,banungwe,"/bemba_richards_193_sv:p::/bemba_richards_193_sv::Then he stops to think and adds more names, and then proceeds to argue with himself, for instance, that if iron is [p. (b)] hostile to the elephant, it must be the enemy of the leopard, the wild pig or the lion too.",0.75,0.75,1,1,/bemba_richards_193_sv:p,1113
3e5417c10e16,within_group,SouthernAfrica,bemba,iron people,"clans with the names of animals hunted or killed with iron spears or arrows (elephant, lion, leopard, etc.)",clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,banungwe,banungwe,"/bemba_richards_190a_sv:p::/bemba_richards_190a_sv::The iron people reckon themselves banungwe to members of all clans with the names of animals hunted or killed with iron spears or arrows, i.e., the elephant, lion, leopard, etc.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/bemba_richards_190a_sv:p,1104
3f4cd147b326,within_group,SouthernAfrica,bemba,lion,goat,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,reciprocal relations / banungwe,banungwe,"/bemba_richards_190a_sv:p::/bemba_richards_190a_sv::The animal clans also have reciprocal relations ... with any mukoa named after an animal smaller than itself which forms its prey ( e.g., the lion and goat)...",0.9,0.9,1,1,/bemba_richards_190a_sv:p,1106
f8f6743732ad,within_group,SouthernAfrica,bemba,men of the Crocodile,Mpende clan,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,rag with / joke with,ukucena,"/bemba_richards_337_sv:p::/bemba_richards_337_sv::This, he said, was to please the imipashi ( ukusekeshye mipashi ). When the first fish were caught three were put in the ulufuba for the imipashi . 'Nowadays,' he added, 'we are Christians. We do not build nfuba . We tie knots in the grass instead to imitate ( kupashyana ) an ulufuba . That is our sacred place ( E pa musumba! ) where we put the fish. A native of Shiwa Dandu confirmed this form of the rite. He said it was good to joke with the imipashi as men do with members of the opposite clan (Banungwe) and say to them after calling on them, 'You are the Mpende clan ( mpende is a big fish that eats little ones, mpepe ). You eat mpepe! Muli bene mpende! ) Mulya ne mpepe! This is the way men of the Crocodile rag with the fish clan, pretending they are going to eat them, and it is good to joke ( ukucena ) with the imipashi in this way.'",0.8,0.8,1,1,/bemba_richards_337_sv:p,1115
2287714e3352,within_group,SouthernAfrica,bemba,mfula,"clans with plant names (the millet clans, the gourd, the mushroom clans, the grass, the tree, etc.)",clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,banungwe,banungwe,"/bemba_richards_190a_sv:p::/bemba_richards_190a_sv::the rain clan ( mfula ) counts as its opposite numbers all the clans with plant names (the millet clans, the gourd, the mushroom clans, the grass, the tree, etc.) since plants cannot grow without rain.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/bemba_richards_190a_sv:p,1102
237fb61b9909,within_group,SouthernAfrica,bemba,millet clan,royal crocodile clan,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,banungwe,banungwe,"/bemba_richards_192_sv:p::/bemba_richards_192_sv::A member of the millet clan is still appointed as the Shimwalule, or hereditary burier, of the principal chiefs of the Bemba to-day, and he is still responsible for the final and most powerful rain ceremony... the millet clan are never described simply as the banungwe of the royal crocodile clan,",0.9,0.9,1,1,/bemba_richards_192_sv:p,1109
a0c49fd3b2e7,within_group,SouthernAfrica,bemba,mourning clan,all the clans,clan,group,joking_relationship,unclear,banungwe,banungwe,/bemba_richards_190a_sv:p::/bemba_richards_190a_sv::The members of the mourning clan laughingly assert they are banungwe of all the clans since they are experts in mortuary ritual! —one of the reciprocal functions performed by banungwe .,0.85,0.85,1,1,/bemba_richards_190a_sv:p,1107
e0b55182bf22,within_group,SouthernAfrica,bemba,paired clans,opposite clans,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationships,,"/bemba_richards_34_sv:p::/bemba_richards_34_sv::The Bemba are organized in matrilineal clans distinguished by totemic observances, stereotyped forms of greeting between clan members, joking relationships with paired and opposite clans,",0.7,0.7,1,1,/bemba_richards_34_sv:p,1117
0a04d3416642,within_group,SouthernAfrica,bemba,sons-in-law attached to one household,sons-in-law attached to one household (other co-husbands),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,Ciufi munandi,Ciufi munandi,"/bemba_richards_131_sv:p::/bemba_richards_131_sv::Between the sons-in-law attached to one household there is a feeling of close friendship and familiarity. These young men eat and work together, and the term Ciufi munandi, ‘the man who married with me’, or into the same family, is one of affection and good fellowship.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/bemba_richards_131_sv:p,1097
cc875cb0d502,within_group,SouthernAfrica,ila,"bazhike / mushike (acquired persons, slaves)",owner (called mother's brother) and owner's relatives,caste,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,joking name / kinship term,mushike; bazhike; nabutema; Bantu bulyo,"/ila_tuden_71_sv:p::/ila_tuden_71_sv::A joking name, kinship term, or spirit name—the customary method of referring to a free person—was the accepted procedure of addressing the individual who had been purchased. The slave's owner was called mother's brother; this genealogical fiction was adopted and extended72 to the other relatives.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/ila_tuden_71_sv:p,1119
9d92181c2d03,within_group,SouthernAfrica,ila,members of a misela (musela) age-set,other members of the same misela (musela) age-set,age_set,age_set,joking_relationship,symmetric,,misela (musela),/ila_jaspan_35_sv:p::/ila_jaspan_35_sv::Such sets of people are called misela (sing. musela). Membership is expressed in a joking relationship and by the obligation of mutual aid.,0.6,0.6,1,1,/ila_jaspan_35_sv:p,1118
89b36a24a77b,within_group,SouthernAfrica,khoi,mother's brother,nephew/niece,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,unclear,,//Náob,"/khoi_carstens_63_sv:p::/khoi_carstens_63_sv::Mother's brother, on the other hand, was often addressed as //Náob, a joking term, but also the usual term for grandfather (Hoernlé 1925: 19–22).",0.9,0.9,1,1,/khoi_carstens_63_sv:p,1121
550cc9318d86,within_group,SouthernAfrica,khoi,mother's brother (//naob),sister's son,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,//nuri//as or //nuri//ab ('//nuri exchange'),//nuri//as or //nuri//ab,"/khoi_barnard_186_sv:p::/khoi_barnard_186_sv::The Nama mother's brother (//naob) was the object of intense joking behaviour, including obligatory cattle-snatching and the threat of wife-snatching on the part of a sister's son towards his mother's brother. Among the Nama, this relationship is known as //nuri//as or //nuri//ab (literally ‘//nuri exchange’).",0.95,0.95,1,1,/khoi_barnard_186_sv:p,1120
4b182db878da,within_group,SouthernAfrica,lozi,Mbunda,cross-cousins,ethnic group,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relations,,/lozi_gluckman_277_sv:p::/lozi_gluckman_277_sv::Mbunda have joking relations with cross-cousins.,0.9,0.9,1,1,/lozi_gluckman_277_sv:p,1127
abc4ea0e47e3,within_group,SouthernAfrica,lozi,brothers-in-law,sisters-in-law,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,‘man and wife’ / mulamu,mulamu,"/lozi_gluckman_177_sv:p::/lozi_gluckman_177_sv::Directly related brothers- and sisters-in-law behave freely to each other. They are ‘man and wife’, even when of the same sex, though they more often call each other mulamu than ‘wife’ and ‘husband’. But they should not marry. They joke and tease each other.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/lozi_gluckman_177_sv:p,1126
7d79f307affd,within_group,SouthernAfrica,lozi,father's sister,brother's son,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,my husband / my co-wife,muhalizo,"/lozi_gluckman_176_sv:p::/lozi_gluckman_176_sv::father's sister also calls her brother's son ‘my husband’ and her brother's daughter ‘my co-wife’ ( muhalizo ), as well as ‘my child’.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/lozi_gluckman_176_sv:p,1124
649f810a3f2b,within_group,SouthernAfrica,lozi,grandparents,grandchildren of opposite sex,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/lozi_gluckman_176_sv:p::/lozi_gluckman_176_sv::Grandparents and grandchildren of opposite sex can address each other similarly. Between persons of opposite sex this form of teasing ceases when the youngsters, who find it embarrassing, grow up: they use the terms grandparent, father, mother, and child, out of respect.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/lozi_gluckman_176_sv:p,1125
4a8b57919e8a,within_group,SouthernAfrica,lozi,husband,sister-in-law,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationship,,/lozi_gluckman_149_sv:p::/lozi_gluckman_149_sv::You cannot even do that with a sister-in-law [with whom there is a joking relationship].,0.8,0.8,1,1,/lozi_gluckman_149_sv:p,1122
9f741378d17d,within_group,SouthernAfrica,lozi,mother's brother,sister's son,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,fellow-husband,mufubalume,"/lozi_gluckman_176_sv:p::/lozi_gluckman_176_sv::A mother's brother also calls his sister's daughter ‘my wife’ and his sister's son ‘my fellow-husband’ ( mufubalume ), ... the nephew can sleep in his uncle's hut, fondle his uncle's wife's breasts, and marry her if she wants to when his uncle dies.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/lozi_gluckman_176_sv:p,1123
70811ee4fd37,within_group,SouthernAfrica,ovambo,a man,any girl of his own age in his father's clan,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,my bull (joking address),,/ovambo_loeb_269_sv:p::/ovambo_loeb_269_sv::In this tribe a man may joke with any girl of his own age in his father's clan and call her “my bull.”,0.85,0.85,1,1,/ovambo_loeb_269_sv:p,1131
5672bc1066b8,within_group,SouthernAfrica,ovambo,a youth,his father's sister's daughter,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,unclear,,omuedi,"/ovambo_loeb_107_sv:p::/ovambo_loeb_107_sv::The relationship between a youth and his father's sister's daughter is intimately connected with the moon. The moon commonly is called ohani, but it (and moonlight) may be called omuedi (bull-person).",0.8,0.8,1,1,/ovambo_loeb_107_sv:p,1129
4e5e3e2c8f79,within_group,SouthernAfrica,ovambo,cross-cousins of opposite sexes,cross-cousins of opposite sexes,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,ometzi jangi (my bull),ometzi jangi,/ovambo_loeb_269_sv:p::/ovambo_loeb_269_sv::The reciprocal term ometzi jangi (my bull) is used for cross-cousins of opposite sexes. Cross-cousins married to one another do not have to avoid their parents-in-law.,0.9,0.9,1,1,/ovambo_loeb_269_sv:p,1130
ce7843796d42,within_group,SouthernAfrica,ovambo,father's sister's daughter,mother's brother's daughter,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking and bundling relationship,,"/ovambo_loeb_284_sv:p::/ovambo_loeb_284_sv::Cross-cousins who call each other “bull” stand in a joking and bundling relationship. My informant, an educated school teacher, explained that a man is supposed to take his father's sister's daughter as his first wife, because through her he can inherit from his father. Then as a second wife or as one of his later wives he may marry his mother's brother's daughter.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/ovambo_loeb_284_sv:p,1132
bf4c351f4b33,within_group,SouthernAfrica,ovambo,two cross-cousins of opposite sexes,each other (cross-cousins of opposite sexes),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,call each other “bull”,ohmedi,"/ovambo_loeb_107_sv:p::/ovambo_loeb_107_sv::Although the Kuanyama do not now approve of cross-cousin marriages, traces of the custom remain in speech and action. For instance, customs, proverbs, and relationship terms appear to indicate that paternal cross-cousin marriage was preferred to maternal cross-cousin marriage; the Kuanyama proverb, “Ka xe kedule kaujoko” (Your father is more than your mother) may have been originally an expression of this preference. It is common practice, moreover, for two cross-cousins of opposite sexes to joke with each other and call each other “bull” (ohmedi).",0.9,0.9,1,1,/ovambo_loeb_107_sv:p,1128
80c21e824e37,within_group,SouthernAfrica,ovimbundu,The Ovimbundu of Elende,the people of Bailundu,regional group,regional group,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,have a joke against,,"/ovimbundu_handly_154_sv:p::/ovimbundu_handly_154_sv::The Ovimbundu of Elende have a joke against the people of Bailundu, because the latter on first seeing a cow offered the animal some food on a wooden platter. This story suggests that the Bailundu people, who are of the Ovimbundu confederacy, did not know the animal and its habits as early as did the Ovimbundu themselves.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/ovimbundu_handly_154_sv:p,1135
a46375d8c112,within_group,SouthernAfrica,ovimbundu,a man,his wife's brother,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,mixed,forbidden to eat together or joke,,"/ovimbundu_edwards_103_sv:p::/ovimbundu_edwards_103_sv::Traditionally the relationship between a man and his wife's brother was distant and reserved, the two men being forbidden to eat together or joke. Nowadays the relations between a man and his wife's full or half brother are often very friendly;",0.8,0.8,1,1,/ovimbundu_edwards_103_sv:p,1133
969e04dd9fc3,within_group,SouthernAfrica,ovimbundu,cross-cousins,cross-cousins,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/ovimbundu_edwards_62_sv:p::/ovimbundu_edwards_62_sv::The relation between cross-cousins is one of equality and solidarity, expressed in joking and in burial services.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/ovimbundu_edwards_62_sv:p,1134
36b7911a1e21,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,!Kung,!Kung,ethnic group,ethnic group,joking_relationship,unclear,permitted or forbidden joking between individuals,,"/san_marshall_204_sv:p::/san_marshall_204_sv::The application of kin terms is so extensive that it comes to involve the whole population of the Nyae Nyae region, with the result that a !Kung, by the rules of the system, is specifically permitted or forbidden to joke with every other !Kung he knows.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_marshall_204_sv:p,1160
ec78889cfe70,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,!gu!na,!guma,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationship,!gu!na—!guma,/san_marshall_225_1962_sv:p::/san_marshall_225_1962_sv::Behaviour between a !gu!na and a !guma is as open and free from formal restrictions as any in !Kung society. They have the joking relationship.,0.95,0.95,1,1,/san_marshall_225_1962_sv:p,1242
7543b6efb7d4,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,!hoa,tsau,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,allows joking,,"/san_marshall_217_sv:p::/san_marshall_217_sv::!Hoa and tsau are the two affinal terms (mentioned earlier) which, along with !gun!a (“big [old] name”) and !guma (“small [young] name”), are the only !Kung Kinship Terminology terms that are words rather than terms specific to kinship. !Hoa is the !Kung word for “man,” and tsau the !Kung word for “woman.” As kin terms they are used only between husband and wife.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_marshall_217_sv:p,1221
df953a501405,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,/i!ay (bearers of the name),/i!ay (bearers of the name),group,group,joking_relationship,symmetric,the name relation / joking familiarity,the name relation,"/san_lee_356_1972_sv:p::/san_lee_356_1972_sv::The bonds are particularly strong among men who actually share a common ancestral namesake. For instance, nine of the twelve men named /i!ay in the Dobe area are descended from a single prolific /i!ay. Of him it can truly be said that his name is legion.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/san_lee_356_1972_sv:p,1144
9049b32a5c8b,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,A and B,ego,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,mixed,,tsĩ,"/san_marshall_230_sv:p::/san_marshall_230_sv::A and B, siblings of C, term ego tsĩ, younger sibling, because he is named for their sibling; ego reciprocates giving them the sibling terms.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/san_marshall_230_sv:p,1259
fa61912b67d2,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,A's children,B,kin_role,kin_role,avoidance,symmetric,avoidance category,,"/san_silberbauer_65_sv:p::/san_silberbauer_65_sv::if A is B's joking partner, than A's children and parents (i.e. his two proximate generations) will be in the avoidance category vis-a-vis B, while the grandparents and grandchildren will be in the joking category.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/san_silberbauer_65_sv:p,1377
4cff5faff3e5,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,A's grandchildren,B,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking category,,"/san_silberbauer_65_sv:p::/san_silberbauer_65_sv::if A is B's joking partner, than A's children and parents (i.e. his two proximate generations) will be in the avoidance category vis-a-vis B, while the grandparents and grandchildren will be in the joking category.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/san_silberbauer_65_sv:p,1379
86a430879531,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,A's grandparents,B,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking category,,"/san_silberbauer_65_sv:p::/san_silberbauer_65_sv::if A is B's joking partner, than A's children and parents (i.e. his two proximate generations) will be in the avoidance category vis-a-vis B, while the grandparents and grandchildren will be in the joking category.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/san_silberbauer_65_sv:p,1378
295c950d0338,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,A's parents,B,kin_role,kin_role,avoidance,symmetric,avoidance category,,"/san_silberbauer_65_sv:p::/san_silberbauer_65_sv::if A is B's joking partner, than A's children and parents (i.e. his two proximate generations) will be in the avoidance category vis-a-vis B, while the grandparents and grandchildren will be in the joking category.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/san_silberbauer_65_sv:p,1376
d1a4626f163a,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,Boys,Girls,age_set,age_set,joking_relationship,unclear,,,"/san_marshall_205_sv:p::/san_marshall_205_sv::A boy might say “my wife” or “I am going to marry you” to a girl with whom he could joke, implying that they had had or would have sexual relations; but it would be considered neither proper nor funny to say such a thing to a very young girl, regardless of whether the boy and girl had the joking relationship.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/san_marshall_205_sv:p,1164
de6677d26cc3,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,Brothers,Sisters,kin_role,kin_role,avoidance,symmetric,,,"/san_marshall_339_1959_sv:p::/san_marshall_339_1959_sv::(4) Brothers and sisters do not sit near each other when they reach adolescence and, after marriage, when they have dwelling-places of their own, do not enter each other's scherms (shelters).",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_marshall_339_1959_sv:p,1303
743496331008,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,CH-6,HO-13,person,person,joking_relationship,mixed,joking relation,,/san_sugawara_31_1984_sv:p::/san_sugawara_31_1984_sv::Especially the relations between CH-6 and these 2 adolescent males very properly fall under the concept of the ‘joking relation’.,0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_sugawara_31_1984_sv:p,1404
9461f3e57c06,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,CH-6,KR-12,person,person,joking_relationship,mixed,joking relation,,/san_sugawara_31_1984_sv:p::/san_sugawara_31_1984_sv::Especially the relations between CH-6 and these 2 adolescent males very properly fall under the concept of the ‘joking relation’.,0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_sugawara_31_1984_sv:p,1403
47deb1e7204b,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,E and F,ego,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,mixed,,tsuma ↔ tsu and // ga,/san_marshall_230_sv:p::/san_marshall_230_sv::E and F term ego tsuma because he is named for their father; ego calls them tsu and // ga.,0.85,0.85,1,1,/san_marshall_230_sv:p,1260
4bbed73a3cd0,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,Ego,!umba (affines in ego's generation: offspring's spouses' parents),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,!umba,"/san_marshall_20_1957_sv:p::/san_marshall_20_1957_sv::Ego may joke with affines termed !umba and !undai, who are in ego's generation terminologically as they are his offspring's spouses' parents.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_marshall_20_1957_sv:p,1210
dc273edfe0a5,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,Ego,!undai (affines in ego's generation: offspring's spouses' parents),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,!undai,"/san_marshall_20_1957_sv:p::/san_marshall_20_1957_sv::Ego may joke with affines termed !umba and !undai, who are in ego's generation terminologically as they are his offspring's spouses' parents.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_marshall_20_1957_sv:p,1211
781977180b89,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,Ego,BrWi,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationship,,"/san_marshall_20_1957_sv:p::/san_marshall_20_1957_sv::Among affines, ego has the joking relationship with his or her spouse, and also with BrWi and WiSi, tũn m.s., and SiHu and HuBr, tun!ga w.s., who are all considered suitable persons to marry. || /san_marshall_262_sv:p::/san_marshall_262_sv::Marriage with a WiSi or HuBr is also highly approved. Ego has had the joking relationship with these affines.",0.875,0.9,2,2,/san_marshall_20_1957_sv:p; /san_marshall_262_sv:p,1206;1290
ab1b9c452da3,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,Ego,BrWi,person,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/san_marshall_345_1959_sv:p::/san_marshall_345_1959_sv::A man is expected to take care of his BrWi should she need his care, even though he is not required to marry her. ... Ego has had the joking relationship with these affines.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/san_marshall_345_1959_sv:p,1319
073f0e7a7553,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,Ego,HuBr,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationship,tun!ga w.s.,"/san_marshall_20_1957_sv:p::/san_marshall_20_1957_sv::Among affines, ego has the joking relationship with his or her spouse, and also with BrWi and WiSi, tũn m.s., and SiHu and HuBr, tun!ga w.s., who are all considered suitable persons to marry. || /san_marshall_262_sv:p::/san_marshall_262_sv::Marriage with a WiSi or HuBr is also highly approved. Ego has had the joking relationship with these affines.",0.875,0.9,2,2,/san_marshall_20_1957_sv:p; /san_marshall_262_sv:p,1209;1292
75f2e4119574,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,Ego,HuBr,person,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationship,,"/san_marshall_342_1959_sv:p::/san_marshall_342_1959_sv::Marriages with HuBr or WiSi are highly preferred marriages. This makes ≠Toma ‘feel like’ his WiSiHu, he said, even though he is not married to her, and, as a result, his WiSiDa seems to him like his own daughter. || /san_marshall_345_1959_sv:p::/san_marshall_345_1959_sv::Marriage with a WiSi or HuBr is also highly approved. Ego has had the joking relationship with these affines.",0.875,0.9,2,2,/san_marshall_342_1959_sv:p; /san_marshall_345_1959_sv:p,1309;1321
365b1928eb19,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,Ego,SiHu,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,tun!ga w.s.,"/san_marshall_20_1957_sv:p::/san_marshall_20_1957_sv::Among affines, ego has the joking relationship with his or her spouse, and also with BrWi and WiSi, tũn m.s., and SiHu and HuBr, tun!ga w.s., who are all considered suitable persons to marry.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_marshall_20_1957_sv:p,1208
2fbf038af55b,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,Ego,Spouses of Relatives,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationship,,"/san_marshall_222_sv:p::/san_marshall_222_sv::Spouses of Relatives. When a person marries one of ego's kin or affines, ego drops whatever term he may have previously used for that person and adopts the term appropriate to the spouse of his kin or affine. Ego applies a term that gives him the same joking relationship with both members of the married couple.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_marshall_222_sv:p,1241
4734fc9b6d97,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,Ego,WiSi,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationship,,"/san_marshall_20_1957_sv:p::/san_marshall_20_1957_sv::Among affines, ego has the joking relationship with his or her spouse, and also with BrWi and WiSi, tũn m.s., and SiHu and HuBr, tun!ga w.s., who are all considered suitable persons to marry. || /san_marshall_262_sv:p::/san_marshall_262_sv::Marriage with a WiSi or HuBr is also highly approved. Ego has had the joking relationship with these affines.",0.875,0.9,2,2,/san_marshall_20_1957_sv:p; /san_marshall_262_sv:p,1207;1291
019284dc944c,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,Ego,WiSi,person,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationship,,"/san_marshall_342_1959_sv:p::/san_marshall_342_1959_sv::Throughout the whole system (except among remote name relatives), ego maintains the same joking status with both members of a married couple, for example, with FaBr and FaBrWi, WiSi and WiSiHu, &c. || /san_marshall_345_1959_sv:p::/san_marshall_345_1959_sv::Marriage with a WiSi or HuBr is also highly approved. Ego has had the joking relationship with these affines.",0.9,0.95,2,2,/san_marshall_342_1959_sv:p; /san_marshall_345_1959_sv:p,1308;1320
