8365fb3bea02,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,ho:khwudi,kin,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,unclear,joking relation,ho:khwudi,/san_silberbauer_326_1972_sv:p::/san_silberbauer_326_1972_sv::ho:khwudi:joking relation between kin,0.6,0.6,1,1,/san_silberbauer_326_1972_sv:p,1374
305e7790c7a6,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,homonymous method (individual for whom one is given a term),person given the kin term (ego's referent),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/san_wiessner_114_1988_sv:p::/san_wiessner_114_1988_sv::The homonymous method of applying terms again takes precedence over the generational and one has the same joking relationship as the individual for whom one is given a term. The joking relationship is always reciprocal and as soon as one person has a kin term for another, both know within what bounds they must behave.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_wiessner_114_1988_sv:p,1416
a436660ebec6,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,husband,his wife's kin group,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking/respect status,,"/san_silberbauer_161_1981_sv:p::/san_silberbauer_161_1981_sv::The husband comes under the tutelage of his wife's father in a dependent role, which approximates that of a child in the family. The joking/respect status of the husband within his wife's kin group is the same as hers and vice versa.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_silberbauer_161_1981_sv:p,1351
82eac8e2f28d,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,husband,wife,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationship,,/san_marshall_262_sv:p::/san_marshall_262_sv::and adjusts the joking status by giving to husband and wife the joking relationship as soon as marriage takes place.,0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_marshall_262_sv:p,1289
d041ccbe80fe,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,hxaro partner,joking relative,group,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,hxaro,"/san_wiessner_144_1988_sv:p::/san_wiessner_144_1988_sv::A San may just wait quietly for a return or if his hxaro partner lives nearby and is a joking relative, after a respectable period of time, a minimum of two weeks, the giver may go to the receiver and jokingly remind him that he has a debt.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_wiessner_144_1988_sv:p,1417
b5ce549bd2a9,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,in-laws,respect relationship (older woman and her target),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,unclear,respect relationship,,"/san_shostak_306_sv:p::/san_shostak_306_sv::If the two women are in-laws and have a respect relationship, the situation will be somewhat different. The older woman might sit in her hut and talk loudly to herself: “Does youth need anything to make it beautiful? How is it no one helps this old woman's heart feel young again?”",0.8,0.8,1,1,/san_shostak_306_sv:p,1329
a6fb4c32124d,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,initiation mates,joking partners,age_set,group,joking_relationship,symmetric,"circle of initiation mates, joking partners",,"/san_silberbauer_254_1981_sv:p::/san_silberbauer_254_1981_sv::This, then, is comparable with the generalized-balanced reciprocity that occurs in the circle of initiation mates, joking partners, and others.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/san_silberbauer_254_1981_sv:p,1361
c8aabaa8a9bf,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,joking partners,couples,group,group,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,joking partners,,"/san_howell_231_sv:p::/san_howell_231_sv::Couples who are obviously having coitus in the village can expect to be teased about it later by their “joking” partners, but since some of the members of the village are likely to be “respect” relatives, in whose presence no sexual jokes should be made, it is not easy to integrate coitus into village life.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_howell_231_sv:p,1139
ca8382759c25,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,joking partners,migrants,group,group,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,joking partners,,"/san_silberbauer_173_1981_sv:p::/san_silberbauer_173_1981_sv::His joking partners sniped away at him without result, but matters came to a head over some arrows he had borrowed without returning and his refusal to make any gifts of meat to the owner, contrary to custom.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_silberbauer_173_1981_sv:p,1356
896666f29bc6,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,joking partners,opposite sexes (potential marriageable),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking partners,,"/san_silberbauer_67_sv:p::/san_silberbauer_67_sv::It has been mentioned that joking partners of opposite sexes, if of appropriate ages, are considered to be potentially marriageable. The intimate relationships permitted between them give them a good opportunity68 to get to know each other, and for others to judge their suitability to each other, before marrying.",0.6,0.6,1,1,/san_silberbauer_67_sv:p,1381
eb8f784fa3ea,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,joking relative,avoidance relatives,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,unclear,licensed liberties / borrowing without formality,,"/san_silberbauer_236_1981_sv:p::/san_silberbauer_236_1981_sv::Avoidance relatives may borrow “general use” artifacts after asking permission, but a joking relative may borrow without the formality of a request.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_silberbauer_236_1981_sv:p,1359
97f0b5b8965e,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,joking relatives,joking partners,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking partners / joking relatives,,"/san_silberbauer_296_1981_sv:p::/san_silberbauer_296_1981_sv::In this context joking relatives function as feedback loops that provide an individual with information about others' reactions to his behavior, reducing randomness both in his behavior and his anticipation of its social consequences. The exchanges of joking partners include opportunity for making amends and for extending forgiveness, which, because it is a public performance, gives emotional comfort not only to the partners but to the whole band as well.",0.6,0.6,1,1,/san_silberbauer_296_1981_sv:p,1362
650baa28dc84,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,joking relatives,joking relatives,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relatives,,"/san_silberbauer_238_1981_sv:p::/san_silberbauer_238_1981_sv::Joking relatives are obliged to grant each others' requests for goods and services and to give these priority over normal (i.e., other) exchange obligations.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_silberbauer_238_1981_sv:p,1360
963a80f714f7,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,junior sister,young spouses,kin_role,group,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/san_silberbauer_165_sv:p::/san_silberbauer_165_sv::A junior sister, by contrast, is a joking partner of both the young spouses and spends a good deal of time with them, reinforcing the bond of same-sex siblinghood.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/san_silberbauer_165_sv:p,1354
34b120029689,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,kin,kin (permitted playmates),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,unclear,joking (ho:khwudi),ho:khwudi,"/san_silberbauer_310_1972_sv:p::/san_silberbauer_310_1972_sv::Relationships between kin are categorized as either joking (ho:khwudi) or avoidance (gjiukxekxu), and an individual distinguishes between those of his kin whom he should !ao (fear, respect) and those with whom he may “play.”",0.8,0.8,1,1,/san_silberbauer_310_1972_sv:p,1364
f650d4be8cfe,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,kinsman (quasi-kin),kinsman (term-tied status within triad),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,mixed,,,"/san_silberbauer_145_1981_sv:p::/san_silberbauer_145_1981_sv::An individual may assume a quasi- kinship relationship with a hitherto unrelated person who is kin
... The joking/respect roles in the triad are determined by the respective kinship links, in keeping with term-tied statuses, and work out as congruent triangles.",0.6,0.6,1,1,/san_silberbauer_145_1981_sv:p,1344
d7feeedc363b,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,kinswoman,bride,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,unclear,joking relationship,,"/san_marshall_278_sv:p::/san_marshall_278_sv::Any kinswoman (preferably a young one) who is tall enough and strong enough, whether she herself is married or not, and whether or not she has the joking relationship with the bride, may carry the bride and any may attend her.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/san_marshall_278_sv:p,1296
11ed83f0bb02,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,kinswoman,bride,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relation,,"/san_marshall_356_1959_sv:p::/san_marshall_356_1959_sv::Any (preferably young) kinswoman who is tall enough and strong enough, whether she herself is married or not, and whether or not she has the joking relation with the bride, may carry the bride; and any may attend her.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_marshall_356_1959_sv:p,1324
08f8724b00d5,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,ma:masa or ma:sa,ego,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking category,ma:masa or ma:sa,"/san_silberbauer_312_1972_sv:p::/san_silberbauer_312_1972_sv::The following kin are in the joking category: 
ma:masa or ma:sa (female counterpart of ba:bama, including FSi, MBrW)",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_silberbauer_312_1972_sv:p,1368
46439bb8e77d,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,male,brother,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/san_marshall_206_sv:p::/san_marshall_206_sv::Among siblings, according to a cross and parallel pattern in which a sex differentiation is made, a male jokes with his brother, but not with his sister;",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_marshall_206_sv:p,1167
848f8ee83198,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,male,sister,kin_role,kin_role,avoidance,unclear,,,"/san_marshall_206_sv:p::/san_marshall_206_sv::Among siblings, according to a cross and parallel pattern in which a sex differentiation is made, a male jokes with his brother, but not with his sister;",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_marshall_206_sv:p,1168
2c737635a12b,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,male ego,BrWiBr,kin_role,kin_role,avoidance,unclear,avoidance with BrWiBr,,"/san_marshall_221_sv:p::/san_marshall_221_sv::Thus, for example, a male ego would joke with his BrWiSi since he jokes with his BrWi, and he would refrain from joking with his BrWiBr.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/san_marshall_221_sv:p,1238
1176f3552b98,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,male ego,BrWiSi,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,unclear,joking with BrWiSi,,"/san_marshall_221_sv:p::/san_marshall_221_sv::Thus, for example, a male ego would joke with his BrWiSi since he jokes with his BrWi, and he would refrain from joking with his BrWiBr.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/san_marshall_221_sv:p,1237
860e63f3bb00,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,male ego,WiSi,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,jokes with,WiSi,"/san_marshall_217_sv:p::/san_marshall_217_sv::Husbands and wives are paired: thus, a male ego jokes with his WiSi and WiSiHu but not with his WiBr or WiBrWi;",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_marshall_217_sv:p,1227
c840f7e6a501,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,male ego,WiSiHu,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,jokes with,WiSiHu,"/san_marshall_217_sv:p::/san_marshall_217_sv::Husbands and wives are paired: thus, a male ego jokes with his WiSi and WiSiHu but not with his WiBr or WiBrWi;",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_marshall_217_sv:p,1228
9ebf855b0f21,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,males and females belonging to biologically alternate generations,each other,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/san_sugawara_29_1984_sv:p::/san_sugawara_29_1984_sv::At the same time, contact tends to frequently occur between males and females belonging to biologically alternate generations (X2=7.3516, p&0.01, df=1).",0.85,0.85,1,1,/san_sugawara_29_1984_sv:p,1402
b160b906dbfb,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,males and females belonging to genealogically the same generation,each other,kin_role,kin_role,avoidance,symmetric,,,"/san_sugawara_29_1984_sv:p::/san_sugawara_29_1984_sv::Only for male-female dyads, are significant chi-square values obtained from the distributions of number of dyads in contact among both of the genealogical and biological generation categories. Namely, contact is apt to be avoided between males and females belonging to genealogically the same generation (X2=4.8153, p&0.05, df=1).",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_sugawara_29_1984_sv:p,1401
696d2d788805,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,man,wife's brother,kin_role,kin_role,avoidance,unclear,,,"/san_marshall_206_sv:p::/san_marshall_206_sv::A man jokes with his wife's sister and avoids joking with her brother, as his wife does.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_marshall_206_sv:p,1182
6a521136d501,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,man,wife's sister,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,unclear,,,"/san_marshall_206_sv:p::/san_marshall_206_sv::A man jokes with his wife's sister and avoids joking with her brother, as his wife does.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_marshall_206_sv:p,1181
f2ee9f8e7230,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,man,woman who has the same name as his mother,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,name relationship / joking relationship,name relationship,"/san_marshall_259_1965_sv:p::/san_marshall_259_1965_sv::From the !Kung point of view the custom has two values. It regulates the incest taboo in its furthermost extension, especially the joking relationship that symbolizes the taboo. (For example, a man should not marry or make sexual jokes with a woman who has the same name as his mother.)",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_marshall_259_1965_sv:p,1283
fc80a40a7da9,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,men who are name-relatives (pre-marriage),women who are name-relatives (pre-marriage),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,!gun!a and tũn,!gun!a; tũn,/san_marshall_261_sv:p::/san_marshall_261_sv::In preferred marriages the man and women are name-relatives who have had the joking relationship before marriage and have applied to each other the terms !gun!a and tũn.,0.95,0.95,1,1,/san_marshall_261_sv:p,1288
2fce407bd8e9,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,men who have the joking relationship,other men who have the joking relationship,group,group,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationship,,"/san_marshall_354_1959_sv:p::/san_marshall_354_1959_sv::Too frequent indulgence in sexual intercourse with one's wife, even when no hunt is in progress, is believed to diminish a man's hunting power. Some men believed that once in five nights was best; others thought once in three nights was all right. Men who have the joking relationship constantly play upon the theme of excess, taunting each other: ‘You do not know hunting, you know women’, ‘You cannot leave your wife long enough to hunt.’",0.6,0.6,1,1,/san_marshall_354_1959_sv:p,1323
a27d5eeb2f67,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,n//odima,n//odisa,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationship,n//odima-n//odisa,"/san_silberbauer_149_sv:p::/san_silberbauer_149_sv::What did emerge was that all first marriages (as distinct from subsequent marriages of widowed or divorced persons) had been between boys and girls in a n//odima-n//odisa relationship, which is the terminological equivalent of cross-cousinship and, hence, placed the two in a joking relationship.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_silberbauer_149_sv:p,1347
a39a4126c630,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,n//odima or n//odisa,ego,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking category,n//odima or n//odisa,"/san_silberbauer_312_1972_sv:p::/san_silberbauer_312_1972_sv::The following kin are in the joking category: 
n//odima or n//odisa (grandchildren, the generation after g/wâ)",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_silberbauer_312_1972_sv:p,1369
7348813c4790,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,name relationship (k'õi),tssxõ,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,name relationship follows tssxõ pattern,k'õi,"/san_guenther_80_sv:p::/san_guenther_80_sv::The interaction with ones name relation follows the tssxõ pattern. While the name relations expand the network of kin relations, the joking and avoidance principles stabilize the interrelations of kinsmen.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_guenther_80_sv:p,1138
f827833af9f3,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,name relatives (man),name relatives (woman),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,!gu!na and tũn,!gu!na; tũn,/san_marshall_345_1959_sv:p::/san_marshall_345_1959_sv::In preferred marriages the man and woman are name relatives who have had the joking relationship before marriage and have applied to each other the terms !gu!na and tũn.,0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_marshall_345_1959_sv:p,1318
9c9f01be4c60,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,name-relatives,grandparents/grandchildren (when classified by name),kin_role,kin_role,avoidance,unclear,name-relationship exception (non-joking),,/san_marshall_22_1957_sv:p::/san_marshall_22_1957_sv::It is possible that the culture allows joking between grandparents and grandchildren (with the rather few exceptions when the factor of the name intervenes to classify them in the non-joking category) because joking is associated with a welcome ease and intimacy of relationship...,0.8,0.8,1,1,/san_marshall_22_1957_sv:p,1255
002de9e83a08,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,newcomer woman,attendant visitor,group,group,joking_relationship,symmetric,mock blows / banter,,"/san_sugawara_200_1988_sv:p::/san_sugawara_200_1988_sv::After she sat down at a distance of 1 m from one of the resident women, she exchanged greeting with 3 older residents, one after another. And then, she called to !kaoba, “≠Kai”. Immediately, !kaoba stood up and approached kju:, sitting in face-to-face proximity to her. These two women exchanged mock blows with each other, laughing cheerfully.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/san_sugawara_200_1988_sv:p,1386
1b6aef87ba15,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,nuclear family,collaterals,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,relationship of joking or respect,,"/san_wiessner_106_1994_sv:p::/san_wiessner_106_1994_sv::Standard !Kung kinship terms are structured by relative age and generation, and each term carries with it a relationship of joking or respect that delimits eligibility for marriage and a range of permitted behavior.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/san_wiessner_106_1994_sv:p,1410
4dfc97a4770f,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,old men,the girl,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationship,,"/san_marshall_265_1965_sv:p::/san_marshall_265_1965_sv::The two old men should be free from even the most attenuated, symbolic extension of the incest taboo with respect to the girl, and it follows that they should have what is called the joking relationship with her.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/san_marshall_265_1965_sv:p,1294
78eaa3c3854f,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,one who shares a name with a person's sister,person's sister,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,unclear,like (name-based joking extension),,/san_wiessner_108_1988_sv:p::/san_wiessner_108_1988_sv::The one who shares a name with a person's sister is said to be ‘like’ his sister (note that the San clearly do not say that this person then ‘is’ his sister as the term giving and term receiving groups are kept distinct).,0.7,0.7,1,1,/san_wiessner_108_1988_sv:p,1414
fe6cf63fff65,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,one woman,the husband of another,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,,,"/san_silberbauer_302_1981_sv:p::/san_silberbauer_302_1981_sv::Although I must be circumspect in my communicative behavior toward another man's wife, I am allowed considerable freedom when talking to her husband.",0.6,0.6,1,1,/san_silberbauer_302_1981_sv:p,1363
794cfb6ff58d,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,one's own generation,one's own generation,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/san_wiessner_114_1988_sv:p::/san_wiessner_114_1988_sv::Essentially one is allowed to joke with everybody in one's own generation except siblings of the opposite sex and their spouses, one's spouse's siblings of the same sex and their spouses as well as everybody in alternate generations to yours.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/san_wiessner_114_1988_sv:p,1415
08edb052de8f,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,opposite-sex cross cousins (those called by the terms for grandchildren),ego,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking category,,"/san_tanaka_105_sv:p::/san_tanaka_105_sv::A parent's opposite-sex siblings (those called by the terms for grandparents) and one's cross cousins (those called by the terms for grandchildren) are in the joking category,",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_tanaka_105_sv:p,1408
6b8fc32516f1,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,parent-in-law,child-in-law,kin_role,kin_role,avoidance,symmetric,,,"/san_sugawara_29_1984_sv:p::/san_sugawara_29_1984_sv::Although the parent-in-law and child-in-law are very often in proximity with each other, physical contact is strongly avoided between them.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_sugawara_29_1984_sv:p,1397
0633bd49c923,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,parents,children,kin_role,kin_role,avoidance,symmetric,restriction on joking / non-joking,,"/san_marshall_22_1957_sv:p::/san_marshall_22_1957_sv::It seems that respect in general is not required between siblings; only sexual restraint is required and this is Kinship Regulation Of symbolized by the restriction in joking imposed on siblings of opposite sex, Sex whereas ego may joke with a sibling of the same sex.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/san_marshall_22_1957_sv:p,1252
ae8e0ec9a0cf,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,parents of offspring's spouse,each other,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/san_marshall_258_sv:p::/san_marshall_258_sv::In no category is marriage allowed when joking is prohibited, but in two categories marriage is prohibited while joking is allowed, namely, among cousins and among the parents of offspring's spouse.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_marshall_258_sv:p,1282
3009ef0fc846,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,parents' opposite-sex siblings (those called by the terms for grandparents),ego,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking category,,"/san_tanaka_105_sv:p::/san_tanaka_105_sv::A parent's opposite-sex siblings (those called by the terms for grandparents) and one's cross cousins (those called by the terms for grandchildren) are in the joking category,",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_tanaka_105_sv:p,1407
c2e3e4fe2bcc,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,people sharing their name,ego's nuclear family (core),group,kin_role,joking_relationship,unclear,joking status extended by homonymous method,,/san_wiessner_108_1988_sv:p::/san_wiessner_108_1988_sv::People sharing their name are given a generational term carrying the appropriate joking status (Marshall 1976).,0.6,0.6,1,1,/san_wiessner_108_1988_sv:p,1413
30726203b41e,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,persons of an adjacent upper generation,persons of alternate generations,kin_role,kin_role,unclear,mixed,joking-avoidance relationship,,"/san_sugawara_29_1984_sv:p::/san_sugawara_29_1984_sv::Generally, it has been accepted that the joking-avoidance relationship is closely related to both sex and generation. Namely, ‘reserved’ or ‘respectful’ behavior is required toward the persons of an adjacent upper generation, while relaxed behavior is tolerated toward those of alternate generations.",0.75,0.75,1,1,/san_sugawara_29_1984_sv:p,1400
9a106833f5e5,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,persons who have the same name,each other,group,group,joking_relationship,symmetric,name-relationship,!gu!na,"/san_marshall_23_1957_sv:p::/san_marshall_23_1957_sv::Whatever may be the answer to these questions, the !Kung have put to good use the belief that persons who have the same name partake of each other's entities. This belief has given them the name-relationship.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_marshall_23_1957_sv:p,1263
1a73b217e31d,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,same-sex siblings,each other (sibling dyad),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationship,,"/san_silberbauer_143_1981_sv:p::/san_silberbauer_143_1981_sv::In general, all kin are in a joking relationship except one's parents and opposite-sex siblings and their classificatory and fictional equivalents, who are in the avoidance/respect category.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/san_silberbauer_143_1981_sv:p,1332
ef7d43d529c1,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,same-sex siblings (for a male ego: brothers kiyaxom and dabaxom),ego,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationship,,"/san_tanaka_105_sv:p::/san_tanaka_105_sv::Siblings are divided according to sex; for a male ego, his brothers (kiyaxom and dabaxom) are held in a joking relationship and his sisters (kiyaxosi and dabaxosi) in an avoidance relationship.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_tanaka_105_sv:p,1406
35b8a652e7eb,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,secondary affines,person for whom they were named,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,homonymous joking status correspondence,,"/san_marshall_230_sv:p::/san_marshall_230_sv::Some of ego's secondary affines would be named for his primary affines and some, possibly, for his offspring. These children would be ego's secondary Naming affines, and ego would apply generational terms to them which correspond in joking status to the term he applies to the person for whom they were named.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_marshall_230_sv:p,1256
17d0b5f9460d,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,sibling (male),sibling (female),kin_role,kin_role,avoidance,symmetric,restriction on joking / non-joking,,"/san_marshall_22_1957_sv:p::/san_marshall_22_1957_sv::It seems that respect in general is not required between siblings; only sexual restraint is required and this is Kinship Regulation Of symbolized by the restriction in joking imposed on siblings of opposite sex, Sex whereas ego may joke with a sibling of the same sex.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_marshall_22_1957_sv:p,1253
24b078fd1757,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,siblings (same sex),siblings (same sex),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking partners,,"/san_silberbauer_312_1972_sv:p::/san_silberbauer_312_1972_sv::Siblings, whether own or classificatory, are avoidance kin if of opposite sex and joking partners if of same sex.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/san_silberbauer_312_1972_sv:p,1372
ec887e274fcf,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,siblings of the opposite sex,each other,kin_role,kin_role,avoidance,symmetric,,,"/san_sugawara_29_1984_sv:p::/san_sugawara_29_1984_sv::In summary in comparing close consanguineous or affinal relations, it is evident that contact is far less frequent between the siblings of the opposite sex than between those of the same sex.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/san_sugawara_29_1984_sv:p,1398
5a6e5d3291de,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,"siblings-in-law (i.e., ego and ego's sibling's spouses or spouse's sibling)",each other,kin_role,kin_role,avoidance,symmetric,,,"/san_sugawara_29_1984_sv:p::/san_sugawara_29_1984_sv::This is also true for the relations between siblings-in-law (i.e., ego and ego's sibling's spouses or spouse's sibling).",0.8,0.8,1,1,/san_sugawara_29_1984_sv:p,1399
f600533fc8dd,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,siblings-in-law of the opposite sex,siblings-in-law of the opposite sex,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationship,giba-ku,"/san_sugawara_187_1988_sv:p::/san_sugawara_187_1988_sv::The typical pattern of giba-ku is frequently observed between the residents of the same sex in very affinitive relationships with each other. In the context of visiting, the most interesting is the giba-ku interaction between siblings-in-law of the opposite sex.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_sugawara_187_1988_sv:p,1383
caee57b738d9,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,spouses' siblings and siblings' spouses,collateral kin,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,same kinship joking/respect terms,,/san_wiessner_106_1994_sv:p::/san_wiessner_106_1994_sv::Only spouses' siblings and siblings' spouses are given terms that do not distinguish them from collateral kin.,0.85,0.85,1,1,/san_wiessner_106_1994_sv:p,1412
1e0452bb40fe,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,strangers,local people (hosts/contacts),group,group,avoidance,unclear,,koa,/san_marshall_22_1957_sv:p::/san_marshall_22_1957_sv::When they meet strangers they do not dare to joke until they find out whether or not it is proper for them to do so. They gave us !Kung names. This placed us in the scheme of things and people could know what their status was with us.,0.7,0.7,1,1,/san_marshall_22_1957_sv:p,1254
645c3bdfef04,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,the couples' own children,couples,kin_role,group,avoidance,symmetric,respect (no-joking) relations,respect,"/san_howell_231_sv:p::/san_howell_231_sv::The most important “respect” relations are the couples' own parents and own children, who are precisely the people who are likely to be closest to them in the sleeping arrangements of the village (Shostak 1976).",0.85,0.85,1,1,/san_howell_231_sv:p,1141
7b142c39cbcf,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,the couples' own parents,couples,kin_role,group,avoidance,symmetric,respect (no-joking) relations,respect,"/san_howell_231_sv:p::/san_howell_231_sv::but since some of the members of the village are likely to be “respect” relatives, in whose presence no sexual jokes should be made, it is not easy to integrate coitus into village life. The most important “respect” relations are the couples' own parents and own children,",0.85,0.85,1,1,/san_howell_231_sv:p,1140
5085b249854e,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,the men of the village,girls approaching puberty,group,age_set,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/san_shostak_269_sv:p::/san_shostak_269_sv::As a girl begins to mature, the men of the village offer running commentaries on the changes in her body—obvious in a culture where the breasts are not covered—and joke about wanting to marry her or to run away with her.",0.7,0.7,1,1,/san_shostak_269_sv:p,1327
842825ddabe8,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,the offending husband or wife,his or her joking partners,person,group,joking_relationship,unclear,joking partners,,"/san_silberbauer_156_1981_sv:p::/san_silberbauer_156_1981_sv::An adulterous spouse is quietly told by friends to mend his or her ways, or if it can be done without precipitating marital disaster, the offending husband or wife is publicly teased by his or her joking partners.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/san_silberbauer_156_1981_sv:p,1350
313ead7f5f60,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,the party,a young man who kills much meat,group,person,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,heavy joking and derision to level arrogance,,"/san_lee_246_1979_sv:p::/san_lee_246_1979_sv::The party, of course, has no intention of abandoning the kill. The heavy joking and derision are directed toward one goal: the leveling of potentially arrogant behavior in a successful hunter. ... ""When a young man kills much meat, he comes to think of himself as a chief or a big man, and he thinks of the rest of us as his servants or inferiors. We can't accept this. We refuse one who boasts, for someday his pride will make him kill somebody. So we always speak of his meat as worthless.""",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_lee_246_1979_sv:p,1142
c62dd5e4cadd,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,tssxõ,basic kin relations,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,"relation of closeness, affection and mutual help (tssxõ)",tssxõ,"/san_guenther_80_sv:p::/san_guenther_80_sv::In addition, there are three broad relationships that cut across or modify the 11 specific relations. These are an avoidance relationship (dau'i); a relation of closeness, affection and mutual help (tssxõ)—as well as of obscene joking and teasing—",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_guenther_80_sv:p,1137
0e9e80c13a48,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,tun!ga (woman-speaking),affines,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,tun!ga (woman-speaking),tun!ga,"/san_marshall_23_1957_sv:p::/san_marshall_23_1957_sv::Tun!ga is applied to consanguineous kin and affines and allows joking, woman-speaking.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/san_marshall_23_1957_sv:p,1265
19bf86726ff6,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,tun!ga (woman-speaking),consanguineous kin,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,tun!ga (woman-speaking),tun!ga,"/san_marshall_23_1957_sv:p::/san_marshall_23_1957_sv::Tun!ga is applied to consanguineous kin and affines and allows joking, woman-speaking.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/san_marshall_23_1957_sv:p,1264
e14ff09659fe,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,uncle/aunt in 'cross' position,nephew/niece,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationship,,"/san_sugawara_26_1984_sv:p::/san_sugawara_26_1984_sv::This high frequency of dyads in proximity might be due to the fact that all of these relations between uncle/aunt and nephew/niece are ‘cross’ relations (i.e., relations between ego and ego's mother's brother or ego's father's sister). In other words, the principle that the joking relationship is to be applied to the uncle or aunt in ‘cross’ position, though they are belonging to the adjacent generation to ego's, may be reflected in the above result.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_sugawara_26_1984_sv:p,1396
8250de0ae194,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,visiting band,home band,group,group,joking_relationship,mixed,,,"/san_silberbauer_180_1981_sv:p::/san_silberbauer_180_1981_sv::Having numerical superiority and also a generally “pushing” manner, they dominated the home band after a few weeks. The remarks of joking partners across band boundaries became more and more pointed, cooperative ventures virtually ceased, and games, dances, and discussion increasingly became one-band occasions.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_silberbauer_180_1981_sv:p,1358
2a19668ab3c7,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,wife's brother's wife,husband's sister's husband,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,unclear,,,/san_marshall_275_1965_sv:p::/san_marshall_275_1965_sv::Marriage with the wife's brother's wife or the husband's sister's husband is also forbidden; the reason for this lies in a complexity of the joking relationship.,0.6,0.6,1,1,/san_marshall_275_1965_sv:p,1295
09ba24afb5b6,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,woman,husband's brother,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,unclear,,,"/san_marshall_206_sv:p::/san_marshall_206_sv::A woman jokes with her husband's brother and avoids joking with his sister, as her husband does.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_marshall_206_sv:p,1183
27036a1660f3,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,woman,husband's sister,kin_role,kin_role,avoidance,unclear,,,"/san_marshall_206_sv:p::/san_marshall_206_sv::A woman jokes with her husband's brother and avoids joking with his sister, as her husband does.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_marshall_206_sv:p,1184
9fffb5c3c7eb,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,≠ xwa:,N/udukhwe,person,person,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationship,,/san_silberbauer_172_1981_sv:p::/san_silberbauer_172_1981_sv::“There are men so mean as to deny meat to a gjiba:xuma [younger brother] who has helped all day with work.” ... (≠ xwa: and N/udukhwe were classificatory brothers).,0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_silberbauer_172_1981_sv:p,1355
f1d64a3b9291,within_group,SouthernAfrica,san,≠oma (bearers of the name),≠oma (bearers of the name),group,group,joking_relationship,symmetric,the name relation / joking relation,the name relation,"/san_lee_356_1972_sv:p::/san_lee_356_1972_sv::The basic principle is that bearers of the same name have a special affectionate relation with one another, and they use the kin terms “old name—young name” regardless of the actual biological connection. It obtains even in cases where there is no traceable connection at all. In the Dobe area, 22 of the men are named ≠oma, and whenever ≠omas meet they enjoy the familiarity of the joking relation.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/san_lee_356_1972_sv:p,1143
eb7ec47d025b,within_group,SouthernAfrica,shona,Maromo,Marutenga,kin_role,person,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,,,"/shona_bullock_81_sv:p::/shona_bullock_81_sv::extended to some extent with Marutenga, Paratema's wife; and if Maromo exceeds its limits with her, it is she who will be blamed by Paratema, for there is a very cordial bond between sekuru and muzukuru.",0.7,0.7,1,1,/shona_bullock_81_sv:p,1426
0da482cd1e38,within_group,SouthernAfrica,shona,Maromo,Paratema,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,mixed,,muzukuru,"/shona_bullock_81_sv:p::/shona_bullock_81_sv::To Paratema, Maromo is muzukuru, his sister's son, and he is privileged in many respects above the sons of the house. He takes precedence over them at meals, sitting next his sekuru, and, in a joking way, may rob him even of his raiment.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/shona_bullock_81_sv:p,1425
2956c6cfe3c7,within_group,SouthernAfrica,shona,Maromo,Whera,person,person,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/shona_bullock_81_sv:p::/shona_bullock_81_sv::He is on excellent terms with her, and she runs out to greet him when she sees him coming, calling out “Our husbands have arrived”, and takes his impedimenta, putting them in her hut. He is the honoured guest during his visit.",0.6,0.6,1,1,/shona_bullock_81_sv:p,1427
74fe9e1972fd,within_group,SouthernAfrica,shona,Sekuru,muzukuru,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationship,,"/shona_holleman_46_sv:p::/shona_holleman_46_sv::I think it is clear that the ‘joking relationship’, between grandparents and grandchildren, that is, between members and uterine members of a lineage, has a logical and structural basis in Hera kinship.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/shona_holleman_46_sv:p,1436
53291ab5cc3f,within_group,SouthernAfrica,shona,growing Karanga girl,her sisters' husbands,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationship,,"/shona_aschwanden_78_1982_sv:p::/shona_aschwanden_78_1982_sv::At the beginning of puberty, the girls are separated from the boys, but despite this it is then that the first sexual “concessions” are made and the young girls learn more about their future sex-life. The growing Karanga girl is permitted to mix with her sisters' husbands, to eat and joke with them, the latter including the exchange of obscenities. This is what is called a joking relationship.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/shona_aschwanden_78_1982_sv:p,1422
1e9dff779434,within_group,SouthernAfrica,shona,husband,ritual friend,kin_role,group,banter,symmetric,licensed banter,,/shona_fortune_72_sv:p::/shona_fortune_72_sv::The most mature love poetry appears to be that composed during marriage. In the examples collected the praises form part of the licensed banter between ritual friends. Each one praises his own wife for her qualities and criticizes the wife of his friend.,0.9,0.9,1,1,/shona_fortune_72_sv:p,1428
acc957f18912,within_group,SouthernAfrica,shona,man,varamu,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,varamu,"/shona_aschwanden_74_1982:p::/shona_aschwanden_74_1982::Adolescent boys have hardly any sexual contact with girls. However, possible partners in intimate relationships (which may lead to sexual intercourse) are the varamu — those women with whom a man can have a joking relationship (cf below).",0.7,0.7,1,1,/shona_aschwanden_74_1982:p,1420
14000c347158,within_group,SouthernAfrica,shona,muzukuru,Tendai,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,so-called joking relationship,,"/shona_aschwanden_175_1982_sv:p::/shona_aschwanden_175_1982_sv::Tendai treats Muzhara as kindly as she would treat Farai, thus establishing a so-called joking relationship with him.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/shona_aschwanden_175_1982_sv:p,1419
0fa73800be71,within_group,SouthernAfrica,shona,muzukuru,dead man's clan,kin_role,clan,joking_relationship,unclear,the joking relationship,,"/shona_ashwanden_277_1987_sv:p::/shona_ashwanden_277_1987_sv::By drinking the “beer of the water”, however, the muzukuru represents still something else. He demonstrates, the Karanga explain, that he himself reaps the “benefit” of the “amniotic fluid” because he can marry women from the dead man's clan (cf p. *, the joking relationship).",0.6,0.6,1,1,/shona_ashwanden_277_1987_sv:p,1423
b055bc153d3e,within_group,SouthernAfrica,shona,ritual friends,ritual friends,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,special type of scolding among ritual friends,,"/shona_gelfand_62_1968_sv:p::/shona_gelfand_62_1968_sv::There is, however, a special type of scolding among ritual friends which takes the form of a joke and is not taken seriously.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/shona_gelfand_62_1968_sv:p,1434
ee563eb42958,within_group,SouthernAfrica,shona,ritual friends,the bereaved,group,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,ritual friends consoling and joking,,"/shona_gelfand_42_1979_sv:p::/shona_gelfand_42_1979_sv::The ritual friends try to console the bereaved, and they do most of the cooking and even make jokes.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/shona_gelfand_42_1979_sv:p,1431
632f8f9898ef,within_group,SouthernAfrica,shona,ritual friends (sahwira),ritual friends (sahwira),group,group,joking_relationship,symmetric,sahwira,sahwira,/shona_gelfand_59_1967_sv:p::/shona_gelfand_59_1967_sv::The only time a person can call another a witch is when two ritual friends ( sahwira) joke with one another and do not take the matter seriously.,0.9,0.9,1,1,/shona_gelfand_59_1967_sv:p,1433
274393a4a304,within_group,SouthernAfrica,shona,the nephew (muzukuru),his aunt,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationship,muzukuru; muroora,"/shona_gelfand_41_1973_sv:p::/shona_gelfand_41_1973_sv::Thus a nephew may become familiar with his aunt because he holds this position in the family and enjoys this peculiar freedom in his uncle's village. He can joke with his aunt who can retort in the same vein saying that he (the muzukuru) is a muroora, implying that he is like a young wife as he is descended from her husband's sister.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/shona_gelfand_41_1973_sv:p,1429
270431593efd,within_group,SouthernAfrica,shona,the nephew (muzukuru),his cousins' wives,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationship,muzukuru,"/shona_gelfand_41_1973_sv:p::/shona_gelfand_41_1973_sv::However, the joking relationship is much more in evidence between the nephew and his cousins' wives.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/shona_gelfand_41_1973_sv:p,1430
17a3a8b0f0e6,within_group,SouthernAfrica,shona,the sister's son,his mother's brother,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,mixed,ukama hwokutamba,ukama hwokutamba,"/shona_bourdillon_49_sv:p::/shona_bourdillon_49_sv::This relationship is cordial and intimate: the Shona refer to it as ukama hwokutamba (‘a relationship of playing’) and anthropologists call it a joking relationship. The sister's son can normally avail himself of anything belonging to his mother's brother without even asking for it, and no action should be taken against him.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/shona_bourdillon_49_sv:p,1424
bc8ce25ef929,within_group,SouthernAfrica,shona,the youth,his elder brother's wife,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationship,,"/shona_aschwanden_75_1982:p::/shona_aschwanden_75_1982::During this period of his life the boy is also given more thorough instruction on sexual matters by some older members of the family such as, for instance, an elder brother and his wife. With the latter the youth is on special terms — the so-called “joking relationship”.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/shona_aschwanden_75_1982:p,1421
cfdfa4705a7a,within_group,SouthernAfrica,shona,two individuals unrelated to one another,sahwira (each other),person,person,joking_relationship,symmetric,"sahwira (ritual bondship, joking relationship)",sahwira,"/shona_gelfand_51_1968_sv:p::/shona_gelfand_51_1968_sv::Of great value to an African is the bond of friendship formed between two individuals unrelated to one another. This lifelong friend is called sahwira ... Social anthropologists have referred sometimes to such a friendship as a joking relationship, thus stressing the fact that the friends can tease or rag one another without either losing his temper.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/shona_gelfand_51_1968_sv:p,1432
f4a93013e089,within_group,SouthernAfrica,shona,vakarahwe,vakwasha,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,,"mukarahwe, mukwasha, tezwara","/shona_holleman_46_sv:p::/shona_holleman_46_sv::The basis of this relationship is partly brought out by the term mukarahwe. The literal translation, ‘stone-sister’, is also a literal description of the position of a ‘father-in-law’ in his son-in-law's kraal... The general basis for this relationship is, therefore, clearly established: the vakarahwe are entitled to respectful treatment from their vakwashabecause they are the ‘higher’ and the ‘elder’ ones.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/shona_holleman_46_sv:p,1437
f7d27a13b117,within_group,SouthernAfrica,shona,vasahwira,vasahwira,group,group,joking_relationship,symmetric,ritual friends,vasahwira,/shona_gelfand_84_1973_sv:p::/shona_gelfand_84_1973_sv::The first takes the form of a joke and is permitted only among ritual friends ( vasahwira) who allow derogatory things to be said by each other without their causing annoyance.,0.9,0.9,1,1,/shona_gelfand_84_1973_sv:p,1435
57d2c11db9f2,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tonga,Baleya,Bantanga,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking-partners,,"/tonga_colson_177_1967_sv:p::/tonga_colson_177_1967_sv::Upper River clans do not have joking-partners, with the possible exception of the Baleya and Bantanga,",0.9,0.9,1,1,/tonga_colson_177_1967_sv:p,1446
83078e76f1e0,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tonga,Children,sisters' children,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,cross-cousin; licensed familiarity,,"/tonga_colson_55_1958_sv:p::/tonga_colson_55_1958_sv::The same generation of basyanausi also has responsibility for and authority over the generation of their sisters' sons, but this authority is different in kind from that which it exercises over its children. Children and sisters' children therefore are in somewhat of an equivocal position in relation to one another. They call one another ‘cross-cousin’ and have a relationship of licensed familiarity.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/tonga_colson_55_1958_sv:p,1464
fec6281812fc,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tonga,Middle River clans,other Middle River clans,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,clan joking-partners,,"/tonga_colson_22_1967_sv:p::/tonga_colson_22_1967_sv::Middle River clans are linked together as clan joking-partners in a manner comparable to that found among the Plateau Tonga,",0.85,0.85,1,1,/tonga_colson_22_1967_sv:p,1454
35172eb5918d,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tonga,Middle River man,wives of his mother's brothers,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking basis with affinal wives,,/tonga_colson_22_1967_sv:p::/tonga_colson_22_1967_sv::A Middle River man is on a joking basis with the wives of both his sister's sons and his mother's brothers.,0.9,0.9,1,1,/tonga_colson_22_1967_sv:p,1456
f3c2582545b4,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tonga,Middle River man,wives of his sister's sons,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking basis with affinal wives,,/tonga_colson_22_1967_sv:p::/tonga_colson_22_1967_sv::A Middle River man is on a joking basis with the wives of both his sister's sons and his mother's brothers.,0.9,0.9,1,1,/tonga_colson_22_1967_sv:p,1455
e7a2503bbb41,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tonga,Those not of this generation,his generation,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking partners and equals,,"/tonga_colson_55_1958_sv:p::/tonga_colson_55_1958_sv::A child is bound primarily to its own father and through him to his generation whether of the same branch or not. It is to this generation that a person owes respect and to its members he turns for assistance. Those not of this generation are joking partners and equals, and would be treated as ‘fathers’ only in the absence of any member of the paternal generation.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/tonga_colson_55_1958_sv:p,1465
c2ff3ed72438,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tonga,a clan,father's clan,clan,clan,joking_relationship,unclear,clan joking partner,,"/tonga_colson_53_1967_sv:p::/tonga_colson_53_1967_sv::In Middle River region, I had a quasi-kinship position based on the fact that years before the Plateau Tonga had playfully allocated me to a clan and a father's clan. Almost everyone could therefore address me as a fellow clansman, a child of the clan, a grandmother through clan, an affine, or as a clan joking partner.",0.7,0.7,1,1,/tonga_colson_53_1967_sv:p,1463
a185051ba4aa,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tonga,a lineage,various others of its own clan,lineage,clan,alliance,symmetric,silufulo partners,silufulo,/tonga_colson_177_1967_sv:p::/tonga_colson_177_1967_sv::a lineage is linked with various others of its own clan in a permanent alliance as silufulo partners. Silufulo can be translated as ‘calabash man’ and refers to the calabash ladle with which offerings are poured.,0.85,0.85,1,1,/tonga_colson_177_1967_sv:p,1447
c8963ea2dbff,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tonga,a man,his mother's brother's wife,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,free joking relationship,,"/tonga_colson_100_1967_sv:p::/tonga_colson_100_1967_sv::Here a man has a free joking relationship with his mother's brother's wife, or with his sister's son's wife, and he is often suspect of being her lover.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/tonga_colson_100_1967_sv:p,1441
56b595236955,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tonga,a man,his sister's son's wife,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,free joking relationship,,"/tonga_colson_100_1967_sv:p::/tonga_colson_100_1967_sv::Here a man has a free joking relationship with his mother's brother's wife, or with his sister's son's wife, and he is often suspect of being her lover.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/tonga_colson_100_1967_sv:p,1442
e03b8d0e147e,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tonga,basimukowa,basyanausi,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationship basis,,"/tonga_colson_322_1958_sv:p::/tonga_colson_322_1958_sv::for the son would have become a ‘brother-in-law’ of his father's generation, and thus on a joking relationship basis with them. The old rules which prevented the two generations of a matrilineal group from appearing as basimukowa and basyanausi in the same marriage also prevented the possibility of such conflicts.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/tonga_colson_322_1958_sv:p,1459
22ad76ba5ac6,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tonga,children of brother,children of sister,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,friendly joking partners,,/tonga_colson_327_1958_sv:p::/tonga_colson_327_1958_sv::It is possible that the change reflects the increased tension between the children of brother and sister—for they now see themselves on many occasions as rivals for the wealth of the single man through whom they trace their relationship rather than as friendly joking partners.,0.8,0.8,1,1,/tonga_colson_327_1958_sv:p,1461
698d95bdfa2f,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tonga,clan,clan,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,bujwanyina (joking-relationship),bujwanyina,"/tonga_colson_178_1967_sv:p::/tonga_colson_178_1967_sv::Briefly, each clan is linked with a number of others in a permanent joking-relationship known as bujwanyina. Clan joking-partners perform various services for one another, particularly during funerals.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/tonga_colson_178_1967_sv:p,1448
15ac91ce69a7,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tonga,clan,clan joking-partners,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,clan joking-partners,,"/tonga_colson_325_1958_sv:p::/tonga_colson_325_1958_sv::This is still done in clan joking-partners for a public shaming of the culprit.
...joking-partners have a general obligation to cast slighting remarks which touch upon one another's misdeeds.",0.7,0.7,1,1,/tonga_colson_325_1958_sv:p,1460
6ccfac6a6ac3,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tonga,clan,paired clan,clan,clan,joking_relationship,unclear,bujwanyina,bujwanyina,"/tonga_colson_15_1958_sv:p::/tonga_colson_15_1958_sv::Each clan is linked or paired with several others individually in what is known as bujwanyina, a relationship of which joking is only one aspect of a wider range of duties and rights.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/tonga_colson_15_1958_sv:p,1445
03bfb953c096,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tonga,clan of the sikatongo,Clans in a joking-relationship with the clan of the sikatongo,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking-relationship,,/tonga_colson_179_1967_sv:p::/tonga_colson_179_1967_sv::Clans in a joking-relationship with the clan of the sikatongo have their set duties in the building or repair of a shrine and in the various rites performed at the shrine.,0.85,0.85,1,1,/tonga_colson_179_1967_sv:p,1451
d801841e14cb,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tonga,clans,the same clans paired together in all the territory stretching from the Zambezi to the Plateau,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,clan joking-relationship,,/tonga_colson_179_1967_sv:p::/tonga_colson_179_1967_sv::they are a constant one with the same clans paired together in all the territory stretching from the Zambezi to the Plateau.,0.9,0.9,1,1,/tonga_colson_179_1967_sv:p,1450
872b18073f73,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tonga,cross-cousins,affines of the same generation (mukwaasi),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,mukwaasi / comparable to cross-cousins,mukwaasi,"/tonga_colson_58_1958_sv:p::/tonga_colson_58_1958_sv::All alike are mukwaasi, and they share a joking relationship comparable to that between cross-cousins. Indeed, the term for affine of this generation is interchangeable with that for cross-cousin and for spouse.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/tonga_colson_58_1958_sv:p,1471
ee01abe17841,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tonga,cross-cousins,cross-cousins,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,free and easy joking,,"/tonga_colson_268_1958_sv:p::/tonga_colson_268_1958_sv::The free and easy joking which marks the dealings of cross-cousins is very different from the relatively restrained behaviour which spouses should observe towards one another in public. || /tonga_colson_56_1958_sv:p::/tonga_colson_56_1958_sv::This last, however, causes little difficulty since joking and easy familiarity pertains to all such relationships.",0.9,0.9,2,2,/tonga_colson_268_1958_sv:p; /tonga_colson_56_1958_sv:p,1458;1466
b7e84336cb7e,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tonga,grandfather,grandchild,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,appropriate joking,,"/tonga_colson_268_1958_sv:p::/tonga_colson_268_1958_sv::A small child may rush home in dismay under the teasing of an old man or woman, to be told by its mother that this is appropriate joking because the teaser is his ‘grandfather’ or ‘grandmother’ and that he must joke back.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/tonga_colson_268_1958_sv:p,1457
83222a1f4e0c,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tonga,grandfathers' matrilineal groups,their grandchildren,lineage,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/tonga_colson_56_1958_sv:p::/tonga_colson_56_1958_sv::Between men and women and their grandfathers, and indeed all members of the grandfathers' matrilineal groups, there exists a joking relationship comparable in many ways to that which is found between cross-cousins.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/tonga_colson_56_1958_sv:p,1467
88dd46aaff4c,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tonga,grandmother,grandson,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relations,,"/tonga_cliggett_114_sv:p::/tonga_cliggett_114_sv::This grandmother-grandson relationship represents a common form of “joking relations,” as research throughout Africa documents (Colson 1951; Murdock 1959; Stucki 1995).",0.9,0.9,1,1,/tonga_cliggett_114_sv:p,1440
0d133edc2507,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tonga,matrilineal group,clan joking-partners,lineage,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,clan joking-partners,,"/tonga_colson_145_1958_sv:p::/tonga_colson_145_1958_sv::If a person tries to commit suicide and fails, then his matrilineal group must find a goat to kill for him so that the clan joking-partners can come and joke with him and eat the meat.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/tonga_colson_145_1958_sv:p,1444
7baa8cf104af,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tonga,men,women,group,group,joking_relationship,symmetric,masabe dancing cross-dressing,masabe,"/tonga_colson_140_1958_sv:p::/tonga_colson_140_1958_sv::During masabe dancing, which may be performed either as a curing ritual or as pure entertainment, either men or women may dress as members of the opposite sex. Women masabe leaders carry an axe.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/tonga_colson_140_1958_sv:p,1443
7f309d508fb4,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tonga,mother's brother,father,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,mixed,mukwaasi,mukwaasi,"/tonga_colson_58_1958_sv:p::/tonga_colson_58_1958_sv::Eventually as mother's brother and father of the same children, they may be at odds, but equally the existence of these children forms a common bond between them whatever happens to the marriage.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/tonga_colson_58_1958_sv:p,1470
98054fc14e0f,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tonga,paternal cross-cousins,father's mother's brother,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,unclear,,,"/tonga_colson_56_1958_sv:p::/tonga_colson_56_1958_sv::Her brother is usually called ‘grandfather’, though he is likely to be treated with familiarity since he is on the same generation level as paternal cross-cousins, who are equated with him, and he is not of the father's generation.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/tonga_colson_56_1958_sv:p,1468
c6c0ec1eaecc,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tonga,silufulo lineage (grandchildren),lineage of the dead (lineage mates of the dead),lineage,lineage,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,grandchildren (ritual jesters) / clan joking-partners,silufulo,"/tonga_colson_180_1967_sv:p::/tonga_colson_180_1967_sv::Whenever joking is institutionalized in a ritual context, the duty is performed by ‘grandchildren'. These are never the grandchildren of the lineage involved; they are grandchildren of the silufulo lineage. During a funeral grain from the store of the dead man or woman is poured upon the grave. The grandchildren of the silufulo lineage are the ones to scramble for it while they jest with one another and with lineage mates of the dead.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/tonga_colson_180_1967_sv:p,1453
0d2c923ecc39,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tonga,sister's husband,brother's wife,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,mukwaasi,mukwaasi,"/tonga_colson_58_1958_sv:p::/tonga_colson_58_1958_sv::Sister's husband and brother's wife (both sexes speaking), husband's sister, husband's brother, wife's brother and wife's sister are all equated, and with them are grouped spouse's grandparents and grandchild's spouse. All alike are mukwaasi, and they share a joking relationship comparable to that between cross-cousins.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/tonga_colson_58_1958_sv:p,1469
a115ce6d4c75,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tonga,those of the same clan,clan joking-partners,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,clan joking-partners,,"/tonga_colson_45_1958_sv:p::/tonga_colson_45_1958_sv::Those of the same clan, or those who were clan joking-partners, ought not to enslave or hold one another in slavery, but they could turn a blind eye to encroachments upon the life or freedom of one who could only cite his membership in a clan as a claim upon them.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/tonga_colson_45_1958_sv:p,1462
8e0cc3a8eb5c,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tsonga,a man,those of his elder brothers (tinamu),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,tinamu,tinamu,/tsonga_sunod_248_1927_sv:p::/tsonga_sunod_248_1927_sv::One does not marry a ñwingi! ... A man will keep away from his younger brother's wives (bengi) and play with those of his elder brothers (tinamu).,0.95,0.95,1,1,/tsonga_sunod_248_1927_sv:p,1481
8cee79ff42bf,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tsonga,a woman,his younger brothers (tinamu),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,tinamu,tinamu,"/tsonga_sunod_248_1927_sv:p::/tsonga_sunod_248_1927_sv::A woman will generally respect the elder brothers of her husband (bengi), and hold very free intercourse with his younger brothers (tinamu), and vice-versa.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/tsonga_sunod_248_1927_sv:p,1480
41b80614a401,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tsonga,a woman (the man's wife),his younger brothers (the husband's younger brothers),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,bungunya,bungunya,"/tsonga_jaques_347_sv:p::/tsonga_jaques_347_sv::Reciprocally also, a woman will avoid her husband's elder brothers, and joke (bungunya) with his younger brothers.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/tsonga_jaques_347_sv:p,1475
7d9ebbdc2aae,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tsonga,hahane,nephews and nieces,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,confidente / great familiarity,hahane,"/tsonga_jaques_340_sv:p::/tsonga_jaques_340_sv::For the majority of the Thonga people, a great familiarity with the hahane seems to be the rule. All kinds of jokes can be made in her presence, and a girl, even a boy, will not be ashamed to tell her all the foolish things they have done. She is the proper “confidente” of her nephews and nieces.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/tsonga_jaques_340_sv:p,1472
47dbc192fdbb,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tsonga,husband (my),all the girls belonging to my bakoñwana,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,tinamu / bakoñwana,bakoñwana,"/tsonga_junod_247_1927_sv:p::/tsonga_junod_247_1927_sv::As already pointed out, all the girls belonging to my bakoñwana are in the same position towards me. I can marry them all… if I am rich enough to “lobola” them.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/tsonga_junod_247_1927_sv:p,1478
8cbf03480baa,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tsonga,husband (my),daughter of my wife's brother (Shaputa versus Mboza),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,nsati,nsati,/tsonga_junod_247_1927_sv:p::/tsonga_junod_247_1927_sv::In this first category is included the daughter of my wife's brother (Shaputa versus Mboza). With her the relations are perhaps even more free than with my wife's younger sisters. I call her nsati. The intimacy is so great that I can hold her breasts (tamele mabele).,0.9,0.9,1,1,/tsonga_junod_247_1927_sv:p,1477
78cb9a63819f,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tsonga,husband (my),wives of my elder brothers,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,tinamu,tinamu,"/tsonga_junod_247_1927_sv:p::/tsonga_junod_247_1927_sv::The second category of tinamu includes those women whom I might marry, not on account of the prior right of a group to the women of another group, but because I might inherit them. They are the wives of my elder brothers. If their husbands die, I am their legal heir. Relations with them are easy. We joke together, but there is more reserve towards these tinamu than towards those of the first category.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/tsonga_junod_247_1927_sv:p,1479
1e0d36f263c6,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tsonga,maternal uncles and their wives,ntukulu,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,,kokwana,"/tsonga_jaques_343_sv:p::/tsonga_jaques_343_sv::It is not our intention to repeat here the excellent account given by Mr. Junod of the nature of the relationship between a kokwana and a ntukulu. (See Life, I, p. – .) I shall only, as a complement to the description of this pattern of behaviour, give a typical account by one of my informants.

(b) The maternal uncles and their wives.—Among the Ronga, Zulu, Sotho, Pedi and Venda, these uncles have the special name of malume. Thongas, of the Transvaal, under the influence of these tribes, use also the appellation of malume, but the proper Thonga term is kokwana. In this relationship, familiarity attains its maximum.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/tsonga_jaques_343_sv:p,1474
ba686e999067,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tsonga,mother's brother,sister's son,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,unclear,mother's brother–sister's son relationship,,"/tsonga_webster_268_sv:p::/tsonga_webster_268_sv::Perhaps this too, has its role model and legitimacy in the now fading mother's brother-sister's son relationship among the Thonga, so celebrated in the anthropological literature.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/tsonga_webster_268_sv:p,1485
07ec57c8600f,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tsonga,ntukulu,maternal uncles,kin_role,kin_role,insulting_license,non_symmetric,very free; right of teasing,ntukulu,"/tsonga_junod_214_1927:p::/tsonga_junod_214_1927::The ntukulu, as we shall see, is very free with his maternal uncles. He has the right of teasing them, even insulting them.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/tsonga_junod_214_1927:p,1476
cda56e30466e,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tsonga,older women,migrant men,age_set,group,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,secular ritualised joking relationship,,"/tsonga_webster_266_sv:p::/tsonga_webster_266_sv::There is a definite sense in which women close ranks in solidarity against migrant men and attempt to restore them to lowered status by what has become a new form of a secular ritualised joking relationship. They also recognise very clearly their own dependence on the men's migrant earnings, which partly explains why it is the older women, many of whom are widows or divorcees ... who are the main perpetrators of this joking relationship, while younger women tend to be willing and amused witnesses.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/tsonga_webster_266_sv:p,1484
ab72fde4a4aa,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tsonga,paternal grandparents,maternal grandparents,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,"masebe, Ngwatsweku or Ntswalane","/tsonga_jaques_343_sv:p::/tsonga_jaques_343_sv::It may be recorded here that a special pattern regulates the behaviour of my paternal and maternal grandparents towards one another. My paternal grandfathers call my maternal grandfather and grandmother, masebe, in the Khosen country (Portuguese East), Ngwatsweku or Ntswalane in the Transvaal. My maternal grandparents use the same term when addressing my paternal grandparents. The behaviour is of cordial and jocular familiarity. The men joke especially with the women of the other couple.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/tsonga_jaques_343_sv:p,1473
13e1c680dafc,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tsonga,women,men,group,group,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,gendered joking / humiliation; new form of joking relationship (male/female),,"/tsonga_webster_265_sv:p::/tsonga_webster_265_sv::Rock stripping is strictly a female preserve. Any man happening by is subjected to the most humiliating insults, invariably of a sexual nature.

Women have developed the technique of humiliation to a fine art. It is the most frequently used weapon in the gender context and is played out in public, whenever there is a suitable audience. || /tsonga_webster_268_sv:p::/tsonga_webster_268_sv::The new form of joking relationship, with overtones of sexual innuendo, personal licence, and humiliation has arisen out of women's resentment of migrant labour. ... The new form, with its almost ritualised antagonism is particularly apposite, for the relationship which spawns it is the pivotal one in male/female interaction today.",0.875,0.9,2,2,/tsonga_webster_265_sv:p; /tsonga_webster_268_sv:p,1482;1486
a8e6222d12bb,within_group,SouthernAfrica,tsonga,women,returning migrants,group,group,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,hostile parody / humiliation,,"/tsonga_webster_265_sv:p::/tsonga_webster_265_sv::Particularly abrasive and hostile humour is reserved for returning migrants, especially if they are prone to parading around the community in new clothes, fancy shoes and mirrored sunglasses. Drink parties, or the communal waterhole, serve as the stage where women of all ages act out elaborate charades—parodying such dandies, playing with particular relish if one such unfortunate is present.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/tsonga_webster_265_sv:p,1483
c12b83a39e51,within_group,SouthernAfrica,zulu,a bride,"her HSis, her Sis-in-l",kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,exception for joking,,"/zulu_raum_106_sv:p::/zulu_raum_106_sv::The bride's respectful conduct towards her F-in-l is clearly defined in speech avoidance.  She must not talk with her HF at all is the first generalized statement made.  ... She avoids any talk that may be considered disrespectful.  Such submissiveness is extended to all in-laws: she may not joke with any of them except her HSis, her Sis-in-l.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/zulu_raum_106_sv:p,1501
a9993ecba28f,within_group,SouthernAfrica,zulu,a man,his brother's wives,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,playmates,,"/zulu_krige_33:p::/zulu_krige_33::""My brother's wives would be my playmates, and if I find that they have cooked something, I would take it without their permission unless it was intended for my brother. I am allowed to give them work to do for me, e.g., the weaving of a mat or a bead decoration for my sticks.""",0.9,0.9,1,1,/zulu_krige_33:p,1499
1bd66ee993ff,within_group,SouthernAfrica,zulu,a man,his wife's sisters,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,allowed to joke widely,,"/zulu_krige_33:p::/zulu_krige_33::""Similarly all the sisters of my wife are allowed to joke widely with me, while there is some strain between me and my brother-in-law""",0.9,0.9,1,1,/zulu_krige_33:p,1500
59f5e4494372,within_group,SouthernAfrica,zulu,abazala,abazala,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,ukulawula,ukulawula,"/zulu_reader_156_sv:p::/zulu_reader_156_sv::It is between abazala that the reciprocal joking relationship (ukulawula) formerly obtained, wherein relatives of opposite sexes could take conversational liberties with one another to the extent of insult and indecency.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/zulu_reader_156_sv:p,1502
21f3a6ae6a8f,within_group,SouthernAfrica,zulu,boys (young men),girls (pagan girls / young women),group,group,joking_relationship,symmetric,,soma,"/zulu_reader_175_sv:p::/zulu_reader_175_sv::There is accordingly an aggressive as well as a compliant element in relations between the sexes during adolescence. ... Pagan girls, as they come into the love-game, accordingly learn from their elder sisters the methods of quick repartee and ridiculing young men by words. Correspondingly the latter, by listening to their brothers and older men, learn how to kilela the girls—to entrap them in speech.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/zulu_reader_175_sv:p,1504
2107553dff5e,within_group,SouthernAfrica,zulu,brothers-in-law,sisters-in-law,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,abalamu,abalamu,"/zulu_vilazaki_44_sv:p::/zulu_vilazaki_44_sv::Among the abalamu, i.e. brothers-in-law and sisters-in-law, there is an easy familiarity and joking relationship which marks them as potential spouses. At a gathering among the Qadi, a young man was talking to a group of girls and making quite a lot of verbal play on the word lala, i.e. to sleep, which can also mean sexual intercourse; and an interested person immediately remarked: “Obviously you are abalamu since this fellow is so fond of verbal play on the word lala.”",0.9,0.9,1,1,/zulu_vilazaki_44_sv:p,1505
4d67b99a78d9,within_group,SouthernAfrica,zulu,father's mother,grandchild,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,mixed,may be teased and played with (lesser),,"/zulu_krige_27_sv:p::/zulu_krige_27_sv::The father's mother, called by the same terms as the mother's mother, may also be teased and played with, but the relationship with her is not quite so good as that between the mother's mother and the grandchild.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/zulu_krige_27_sv:p,1498
779e2368b901,within_group,SouthernAfrica,zulu,mother's mother,grandchild,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,"petting, spoiling, play and tease","umakhulu, umamekhulu, ukhulu, ukoko","/zulu_krige_27_sv:p::/zulu_krige_27_sv::The mother's mother, umakhulu, umamekhulu or simply ukhulu, or ukoko, is the one who pets and spoils you, with whom you may play and whom you may tease.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/zulu_krige_27_sv:p,1497
f0ba51f37ecc,within_group,SouthernAfrica,zulu,okанina,okанina,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/zulu_reader_156_sv:p::/zulu_reader_156_sv::The okanina, the children of the mamekazi, mother's sister, rank for practical purposes in the same degree of closeness as the abazala, the children of the babekazi or of the malume. All the above remarks about abazala can be applied to okanina, the latter being distinguished by a special term to emphasize that they are children of one's mother's sister, that is, like children of one's own mother.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/zulu_reader_156_sv:p,1503
f85bcc00e96f,within_group,SouthernAfrica,zulu,umNdlunkulu,powerful men,group,group,insulting_license,non_symmetric,verbally harass and taunt,omula,"/zulu_hanretta_403_sv:p::/zulu_hanretta_403_sv::UmNdlunkulu could verbally harass and taunt men to an extent usually permitted to most women only during their omula puberty ceremony, and powerful men who were allowed verbally to assault most women were forced to demur in regard to umNdlunkulu.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/zulu_hanretta_403_sv:p,1496
ac0c89b20f4b,within_group,WesternAfrica,Akan,brother,sister,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Akan_Fortes__273_KS:p::/Akan_Fortes__273_KS::Brothers and sisters may joke with each other, tease each other, and even quarrel, as they are prone to do in childhood, without causing offence.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Akan_Fortes__273_KS:p,392
64bee1b9dcce,within_group,WesternAfrica,Akan,brothers,sisters,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking and chaffing,,"/Akan_Mead_303_KS:p::/Akan_Mead_303_KS::Joking and chaffing are permitted between brothers and sisters, between a man and his wife's sister, and between a woman and her husband's brother. || /Akan_Mead_303_NK:p::/Akan_Mead_303_NK::Joking and chaffing are permitted between brothers and sisters, between a man and his wife's sister, and between a woman and her husband's brother.",0.9,0.9,2,2,/Akan_Mead_303_KS:p; /Akan_Mead_303_NK:p,394;397
beb4ea8d8b38,within_group,WesternAfrica,Akan,brothers and sisters,each other,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,easy informality of manners and dress,,"/Akan_Fortes_273_NK:p::/Akan_Fortes_273_NK::Brothers and sisters may joke with each other, tease each other, and even quarrel, as they are prone to do in childhood, without causing offence.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Akan_Fortes_273_NK:p,390
7eebdd8dcb79,within_group,WesternAfrica,Akan,elderly women traders,male toddlers and preschoolers,group,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,,,"/Akan_Clark_19_nk:p::/Akan_Clark_19_nk::very similar joking aimed at male toddlers and preschoolers was a popular pastime, proposing marriage and kin relations considered hilarious because of the boy's age and incomprehension.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/Akan_Clark_19_nk:p,389
7ab819fb5281,within_group,WesternAfrica,Akan,lender,borrower,group,group,joking_relationship,unclear,joking allusion,"Wo de wo tuo bo mmokwa gye abasa a, ete","/Akan_Ratray_371_KS:p::/Akan_Ratray_371_KS::there is a joking allusion to the custom which runs, Wo de wo tuo bo mmokwa gye abasa a, ete (‘When you lend your gun in hope of getting a foreleg, it will burst’).",0.6,0.6,1,1,/Akan_Ratray_371_KS:p,400
30e8317986c3,within_group,WesternAfrica,Akan,male related consanguineally or affinally,female related consanguineally or affinally,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationship,adamfo,"/Akan_Warren_34_KS:p::/Akan_Warren_34_KS::The Bono-Twi term adamfo[unavailable] (usually translated into English as “friend”) can be used to describe relationships between individuals of the same sex; if used by a person to describe a relationship with someone of the opposite sex, that person would usually be related either consanguineally or affinally in such a way that sexual intercourse between the two persons would be either taboo or considered highly deviant. In only this latter sense, involving a joking relationship, may there be platonic, “friendly” relationships between the sexes.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Akan_Warren_34_KS:p,402
68eff7e3f5f2,within_group,WesternAfrica,Akan,man,his wife's sister,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking and chaffing,,"/Akan_Mead_303_KS:p::/Akan_Mead_303_KS::Joking and chaffing are permitted between brothers and sisters, between a man and his wife's sister, and between a woman and her husband's brother. || /Akan_Mead_303_NK:p::/Akan_Mead_303_NK::Joking and chaffing are permitted between brothers and sisters, between a man and his wife's sister, and between a woman and her husband's brother.",0.9,0.9,2,2,/Akan_Mead_303_KS:p; /Akan_Mead_303_NK:p,395;398
eb66de53d5cf,within_group,WesternAfrica,Akan,mother-in-law,son-in-law,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,mother-in-law tags / sayings,"ase, fie yenko","/Akan_Rattray_99_NK:p::/Akan_Rattray_99_NK::There is a well-known saying which when repeated generally causes a sly giggle, ‘ ase, fie yenko’, which might be rendered by ‘You don't catch us going to mother-in-law's hut’. ... An Ashanti would not ordinarily eat out of the same dish as his mother-in-law.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Akan_Rattray_99_NK:p,401
8660e399fc8e,within_group,WesternAfrica,Akan,one elderly woman,her middle-aged daughter,person,person,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,/Akan_Clark_19_nk:p::/Akan_Clark_19_nk::One elderly women trader continually startled me by interjecting comments on the theme of “My husband! You will bring me cloth!” into my peaceful conversations with her middle-aged daughter at their stall.,0.8,0.8,1,1,/Akan_Clark_19_nk:p,388
c31ac687a398,within_group,WesternAfrica,Akan,woman,her husband's brother,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking and chaffing,,"/Akan_Mead_303_KS:p::/Akan_Mead_303_KS::Joking and chaffing are permitted between brothers and sisters, between a man and his wife's sister, and between a woman and her husband's brother. || /Akan_Mead_303_NK:p::/Akan_Mead_303_NK::Joking and chaffing are permitted between brothers and sisters, between a man and his wife's sister, and between a woman and her husband's brother.",0.9,0.9,2,2,/Akan_Mead_303_KS:p; /Akan_Mead_303_NK:p,396;399
6c30043dc1d3,within_group,WesternAfrica,Bambara,Koulibali,Kéita,patronym,patronym,joking_relationship,symmetric,senãnku,dyo benatye / senãnkuya; senãnkuya,"/Bambara_119_NK:p::/Bambara_119_NK::Thus, a Koulibali is a Kéita's senãnku among the Bambara; Bozo and Dogon as well as Bozo and Somono are also senãnku || /Bambara_Dieterlin_119_KS:p::/Bambara_Dieterlin_119_KS::Thus, a Koulibali is a Kéita's senãnku among the Bambara;",0.9,0.9,2,2,/Bambara_119_NK:p; /Bambara_Dieterlin_119_KS:p,456;466
acb44bd634cf,within_group,WesternAfrica,Bambara,Kouloubali,Diara,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,ally (senãnku) / joking relationship,senãnku,"/Bambara_Dieterlin_108_KS:p::/Bambara_Dieterlin_108_KS::On the other hand, relationships commonly known as “joking relationships” are established between families respecting different taboos and bearing different names. ... One of the men, who belonged to the Diara family, asked them their family names. They answered that they had none. “What does that matter?” he replied. “You will live on a great mountain and take the name of Kouloubali.” They then asked the name of their ally (senãnku).",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Bambara_Dieterlin_108_KS:p,465
3d26f6e9c42d,within_group,WesternAfrica,Bambara,blacksmiths,other Blacks,caste,group,joking_relationship,unclear,senankuya relationships,senankuya,"/Bambara_Dieterlen_157_NK:p::/Bambara_Dieterlen_157_NK::Every blacksmith is regarded as the holder of an excellent tere. He is well-intentioned, affable, and never hurts anyone. It is easy to make friends with him, to joke with him, even if one knows him but a short time. The senankuya relationships which he has with other Blacks also contribute to the cordiality of these ties.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/Bambara_Dieterlen_157_NK:p,464
5485bd4cd9ff,within_group,WesternAfrica,Bambara,children of father's sister,children of mother's brother,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,kalame,"/Bambara_Pacques_65_KS:p::/Bambara_Pacques_65_KS::The children of the ntene-muso are called kalame, and they call the children of their mother's brother kalame. The kalame-wjoke and insult each other when they meet.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Bambara_Pacques_65_KS:p,471
a48ed41430aa,within_group,WesternAfrica,Bambara,children of father's sister (kalame),children of mother's brother (kalame),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,kalame,"/Bambara_65_NK:p::/Bambara_65_NK::The children of the ntene-muso are called kalame, and they call the66 children of their mother's brother kalame. The kalame-wjoke and insult each other when they meet.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Bambara_65_NK:p,461
7ec1da540fec,within_group,WesternAfrica,Bambara,numu (blacksmiths),other Blacks,caste,group,joking_relationship,symmetric,senankuya,senankuya,"/Bambara_Dieterlen_153_NK:p::/Bambara_Dieterlen_153_NK::They practice endogamy, maintain joking relationships, senankuya, with other Blacks, and are in charge of certain cults dedicated to ancestors and to Faro.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/Bambara_Dieterlen_153_NK:p,463
ec71cc0d9b8c,within_group,WesternAfrica,Dogon,Dogon men,his maternal uncle's wife,group,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,mà[unavailable]u,mà[unavailable]u,"/Dogon_Calame_373_NK:p::/Dogon_Calame_373_NK::Dogon tell sexual jokes to women with whom they have joking relationships (mà[unavailable]u), which includes his maternal uncle's wife, and the wives of friends and brothers, but they do not exchange them with their own wives.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Dogon_Calame_373_NK:p,508
47016778b0ad,within_group,WesternAfrica,Dogon,Dogon men,the wives of brothers,group,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,mà[unavailable]u,mà[unavailable]u,"/Dogon_Calame_373_NK:p::/Dogon_Calame_373_NK::Dogon tell sexual jokes to women with whom they have joking relationships (mà[unavailable]u), which includes his maternal uncle's wife, and the wives of friends and brothers, but they do not exchange them with their own wives.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Dogon_Calame_373_NK:p,510
a116ab73dfd7,within_group,WesternAfrica,Dogon,Dogon men,the wives of friends,group,group,joking_relationship,symmetric,mà[unavailable]u,mà[unavailable]u,"/Dogon_Calame_373_NK:p::/Dogon_Calame_373_NK::Dogon tell sexual jokes to women with whom they have joking relationships (mà[unavailable]u), which includes his maternal uncle's wife, and the wives of friends and brothers, but they do not exchange them with their own wives.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Dogon_Calame_373_NK:p,509
f6165264d8b4,within_group,WesternAfrica,Dogon,boys,girls,age_set,age_set,joking_relationship,symmetric,mock-relationship,,"/Dogon_Parin_59_NK:p::/Dogon_Parin_59_NK::If boys and girls have a mock-relationship with one another while they are sharing some work such as harvesting po, the innuendos are even more obscene.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Dogon_Parin_59_NK:p,524
67844d1b8cb7,within_group,WesternAfrica,Dogon,couples,couples,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking between couples,,"/Dogon_Calame_375_NK:p::/Dogon_Calame_375_NK::Although frequent joking between couples would give the impression that their relationships are always harmonious, in reality there remains a latent tension. Dogon men live in constant fear that their wives will leave them, and Dogon women are aware that on leaving they will be left entirely to their own resources.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Dogon_Calame_375_NK:p,511
917e83b8a0ea,within_group,WesternAfrica,Dogon,kú¸nõ,twins,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,“joking relationship”,mà[unavailable]u,/Dogon_Calame_280_NK:p::/Dogon_Calame_280_NK::Relations between the kú¸nõ and twins are maintained by a sort of “joking relationship” (mà[unavailable]u). They traditionally exchange jokes on this theme.,0.9,0.9,1,1,/Dogon_Calame_280_NK:p,506
c08e811b984c,within_group,WesternAfrica,Dogon,paternal family,maternal family,lineage,lineage,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Dogon_Parin_59_NK:p::/Dogon_Parin_59_NK::It explains why, according to legend, the two mocking each other are usually regarded as the paternal family and the maternal family.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Dogon_Parin_59_NK:p,525
20fc883a64e3,within_group,WesternAfrica,Dogon,very young children,the Fox (manu),age_set,group,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationship,manu,"/Dogon_Griaule_289_NK:p::/Dogon_Griaule_289_NK::This idea of the expansion of the human domain, progressively impinging upon that of the Fox (who show the way), is illustrated or inaugurated (one may say) in children's games. The role of the very young children, who have a “joking relationship” with the Fox ( manu), is to play with his “earth,” both to look for him and to chase him away.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Dogon_Griaule_289_NK:p,521
42e698da33cc,within_group,WesternAfrica,Dogon,wife of the maternal uncle,wife of the grandfather (nani),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,unclear,,,"/Dogon_Parin_59_NK:p::/Dogon_Parin_59_NK::Kinfolk who must be treated in this way are the ones who, in other circumstances, might be thought of as a married couple, such as the brother's wives or those with whom a sexual approach would be the same as incest, such as the wife of the maternal uncle and the wife of the grandfather ( nani).",0.7,0.7,1,1,/Dogon_Parin_59_NK:p,526
05fd86dfce99,within_group,WesternAfrica,Dogon,women,two young men,group,age_set,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationships,,"/Dogon_Calame_402_NK:p::/Dogon_Calame_402_NK::Two women beat the dance rhythms, with more or less competence; usually they merely begin the rhythms and then get two young men with whom they have joking relationships to carry it on by offering them beer.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Dogon_Calame_402_NK:p,512
cfde25df4716,within_group,WesternAfrica,Fon,brothers-in-law,each other,kin_role,group,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Fon_Herskovits_151_NK:p::/Fon_Herskovits_151_NK::Brothers-in-law are often very close friends, and sisters-in-law usually joke with each other, but this is only an indication of the freedom bred of more or less intimate everyday contact.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/Fon_Herskovits_151_NK:p,531
09c533a21862,within_group,WesternAfrica,Fon,friends and relatives,boys of a village,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,teasing / joking,,"/Fon_Herskovits_296_NK:p::/Fon_Herskovits_296_NK::The ceremony is public, and friends and relatives stand about teasing the boys and joking with them, in order to dissipate any squeamishness, and more than that, to make them ashamed of showing fear.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/Fon_Herskovits_296_NK:p,535
e6d48c0d6b19,within_group,WesternAfrica,Fon,man,brothers of his grandfather,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,unclear,,,"/Fon_Herskovits_151_NK:p::/Fon_Herskovits_151_NK::A man may not joke with his father's brothers, but he may do so with the brothers of his grandfather.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Fon_Herskovits_151_NK:p,533
12f29ff428c0,within_group,WesternAfrica,Fon,man,mother-in-law,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Fon_Herskovits_151_NK:p::/Fon_Herskovits_151_NK::A man may joke with his mother-in-law, in spite of the fact that the feeling between mother-in-law and son-in-law is ordinarily not too cordial. It was stated, indeed, that mothers-in-law and sons-in-law joke all the more for this reason.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Fon_Herskovits_151_NK:p,529
22a814602fe7,within_group,WesternAfrica,Fon,men (holding the boys' shoulders),boys of a village,group,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,holders / steadyers (ritual interaction),,"/Fon_Herskovits_296_NK:p::/Fon_Herskovits_296_NK::Ordinarily, two men are beside each boy, holding his shoulders to steady him. The wound is washed on the third day, and again the organ is pinched so that undue scarification will not occur, and this is repeated daily until the wound is healed.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/Fon_Herskovits_296_NK:p,536
9b48fc854d8d,within_group,WesternAfrica,Fon,mother-in-law,daughter-in-law,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Fon_Herskovits_151_NK:p::/Fon_Herskovits_151_NK::A mother-in-law may joke with her daughter-in-law, because the mothers of both the bride and the groom supervise the first sexual contacts between a man and his wife.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Fon_Herskovits_151_NK:p,530
69b49c66ea4a,within_group,WesternAfrica,Fon,operator,boys of a village,person,kin_role,teasing,non_symmetric,horse-play / teasing by operator,,"/Fon_Herskovits_296_NK:p::/Fon_Herskovits_296_NK::The operator, according to this account, goes through some horse-play with the boys before he actually operates. He may, for example, take hold of the foreskin and tug at it, all the while watching his charge to see if the boy is nervous; and then tug harder still, or he will place the knife on the thigh of his subject, and look into his face to see if he shows fear.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Fon_Herskovits_296_NK:p,534
5f1032577a95,within_group,WesternAfrica,Fon,sisters-in-law,each other,kin_role,group,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Fon_Herskovits_151_NK:p::/Fon_Herskovits_151_NK::Brothers-in-law are often very close friends, and sisters-in-law usually joke with each other, but this is only an indication of the freedom bred of more or less intimate everyday contact.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/Fon_Herskovits_151_NK:p,532
d9a49de6d0b7,within_group,WesternAfrica,Fon,wives,husband's brothers,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,playmate relationship,,"/Fon_Herskovits_151_NK:p::/Fon_Herskovits_151_NK::Thus, a woman may joke with all her husband's brothers, whether these brothers have the same mother or not, regardless of age, the reason for this being that when the husband dies, some of his wives become the wives of his brothers.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Fon_Herskovits_151_NK:p,528
73013883f4f7,within_group,WesternAfrica,Fon,woman,husband's grandfather,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,unclear,playmate relationship,asúchi,"/Fon_Herskovits_151_NK:p::/Fon_Herskovits_151_NK::In the present instance, information concerning these configurations developed from an analysis of the significance of the terms employed by a person toward his spouse's grandparents. In address, a woman calls her husband's grandfather asúchi, “my husband,” and this usage reflects the existence of a playmate relationship",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Fon_Herskovits_151_NK:p,527
ba5260b31c19,within_group,WesternAfrica,Fulani,Dadi,Mariyama,person,person,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationship (cross-cousin),denDiraaBe,"/Fulani_Kaartinen_121_NK:p::/Fulani_Kaartinen_121_NK::Dadi, one of my friends from the city who works as a migrant laborer, is back to stay at his mother's house for a few weeks. He is a little older than I am, and our relationship has developed into a kind of a joking relationship, almost as if we were cross-cousins (denDiraaBe).",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Fulani_Kaartinen_121_NK:p,542
e1010b35735e,within_group,WesternAfrica,Fulani,WoDaaBe cross-cousins,WoDaaBe cross-cousins,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking-relationships,,"/Fulani_Kristin_159_NK:p::/Fulani_Kristin_159_NK::Young men, for example, who stand in culturally defined joking-relationships (either in the same age group or cross-cousins) often refer to one another as Hausa or Tuaregs, intended then as a friendly insult.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Fulani_Kristin_159_NK:p,547
105fc4c081c7,within_group,WesternAfrica,Fulani,WoDaaBe young men in the same age group,WoDaaBe young men in the same age group,age_set,age_set,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking-relationships,,"/Fulani_Kristin_159_NK:p::/Fulani_Kristin_159_NK::Young men, for example, who stand in culturally defined joking-relationships (either in the same age group or cross-cousins) often refer to one another as Hausa or Tuaregs, intended then as a friendly insult.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Fulani_Kristin_159_NK:p,548
73b63e8aedcc,within_group,WesternAfrica,Fulani,a married woman,her younger brothers-in-law,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Fulani_Dupire_72_NK:p::/Fulani_Dupire_72_NK::Thus, deferent towards her husband's parents and her elder brothers, a woman is on equal terms often of an affectionate and friendly nature with her sisters-in-law, and has a joking relationship with her younger brothers-in-law.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Fulani_Dupire_72_NK:p,541
3027b82b04d2,within_group,WesternAfrica,Fulani,brother's children,sister's children,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,dendiraagal,dendiraagal,"/Fulani_Dupire_349_NK:p::/Fulani_Dupire_349_NK::Even within Bororo society this relationship is asymmetrical, the brother's children playing the role of “masters” in relation to the sister's children, called the “slaves.”",0.95,0.95,1,1,/Fulani_Dupire_349_NK:p,538
2228a92956a2,within_group,WesternAfrica,Fulani,brothers,sister's children,lineage,lineage,joking_relationship,symmetric,dendiraagal / cross-cousinship,Dendiraagal,"/Fulani_Kaartinen_7_NK:p::/Fulani_Kaartinen_7_NK::Dendiraagal, “cross-cousinship”, between two distinct “lineages” can imply either alliance and a joking relationship, or a competitive opposition between the groups. Two primary lineages that have intermarried in the past and are thus related through cognatic ties are “brothers” and “sister's children”, or “men” and “women” towards each other (Dupire 1962, – ).",0.8,0.8,1,1,/Fulani_Kaartinen_7_NK:p,543
8e89bde92dc3,within_group,WesternAfrica,Fulani,daughter-in-law,husband's younger brothers,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationship,“old fellow” and “my wife”,"/Fulani_Dupire_71_NK:p::/Fulani_Dupire_71_NK::She has, on the other hand, a joking relationship with her husband's younger brothers, and they call each other “old fellow” and “my wife”.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Fulani_Dupire_71_NK:p,540
aa5fb95e96f0,within_group,WesternAfrica,Hausa,Abdullahi,Habu,person,person,joking_relationship,symmetric,abokin wasa,abokin wasa,"/Hausa_Smith_169_NK:p::/Hausa_Smith_169_NK::he gave the office to this mother’s brother, and after the latter’s death he gave the office to this mother’s brother’s son, Habu, a joking relation ( abokin wasa

Government
Institutions
).",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Hausa_Smith_169_NK:p,655
506556478614,within_group,WesternAfrica,Hausa,Baba of Karo,Gambo,person,person,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relations,,"/Hausa_Baba_212_NK:p::/Hausa_Baba_212_NK::Her mother was my father’s sister, we were joking relations.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Hausa_Baba_212_NK:p,604
72ce2d4211f5,within_group,WesternAfrica,Hausa,"Buzaye, the Sokoto people",blacksmiths,ethnic group,caste,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relation (ethnic–occupational),,"/Hausa_Baba_243_NK:p::/Hausa_Baba_243_NK::'Yardada a daughter of the Buzaye, the Sokoto people, so won’t she joke with a blacksmith’s daughter? ... Talle is chosen to act as ‘grandmother’ as she is a joking relation of ‘Yardada’s, as also are grandparents. This joking relationship is of the kind between ethnic and occupational groups, not between real kinsfolk.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Hausa_Baba_243_NK:p,609
3a209e39df68,within_group,WesternAfrica,Hausa,Ego,children of Ego's gogo,person,kin_role,joking_relationship,unclear,abokan wasa,abokan wasa,/Hausa_Smith_43_NK:p::/Hausa_Smith_43_NK::The children of Ego’s gogo and kawu are Ego’s joking relations (abokan wasa) and preferred spouses in the marriage known as auren zumunta (see p. 49).,0.95,0.95,1,1,/Hausa_Smith_43_NK:p,658
145fda88ecbd,within_group,WesternAfrica,Hausa,Ego,children of Ego's kawu,person,kin_role,joking_relationship,unclear,abokan wasa,abokan wasa,/Hausa_Smith_43_NK:p::/Hausa_Smith_43_NK::The children of Ego’s gogo and kawu are Ego’s joking relations (abokan wasa) and preferred spouses in the marriage known as auren zumunta (see p. 49).,0.95,0.95,1,1,/Hausa_Smith_43_NK:p,659
15763e119cf6,within_group,WesternAfrica,Hausa,Ego,cross-cousins,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,abokan wasa (joking relations),abokan wasa,"/Hausa_Smith_41_NK:p::/Hausa_Smith_41_NK::Ego’s cross-cousins of either sex are his abokan wasa (joking relations), and there is not much difference in the affective or social content of the relationships between go and his paternal or maternal grandparents.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Hausa_Smith_41_NK:p,657
2837f8b0d249,within_group,WesternAfrica,Hausa,Giwa,Audu,person,person,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Hausa_Baba_151_NK:p::/Hausa_Baba_151_NK::Very well, Giwa consulted with her husband's younger brother Audu, she coaxed him, she persuaded her joking relation, they talked the matter over until he agreed.",0.7,0.7,1,1,/Hausa_Baba_151_NK:p,603
f5dd89a2f1bc,within_group,WesternAfrica,Hausa,K w áryá[unknown]wá[unknown],Zá[unknown]fá[unknown]wá[unknown],clan,clan,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,,,"/Hausa_Greenberg_203_NK:p::/Hausa_Greenberg_203_NK::One clan at least, the K w áryá[unknown]wá[unknown], state that they are descendants of a Baribari (Kanuri) slave, the great-great-grandfather of the present mature generation, who married a daughter of a member of the Zá[unknown]fá[unknown]wá[unknown] clan whom the K w áryá[unknown]wá[unknown] consider their superior cross-cousins.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/Hausa_Greenberg_203_NK:p,637
ec346b39b466,within_group,WesternAfrica,Hausa,Sarkin Rafi,Badakuwa,person,person,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationship,,"/Hausa_Besmer_78_NK:p::/Hausa_Besmer_78_NK::His wife is Nana’aishe who causes stomach ailments and incessant itching and the joking relationship he has with Badakuwa, his younger brother’s wife, has been inflated into an adulterous one.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Hausa_Besmer_78_NK:p,625
d556b9e0b33a,within_group,WesternAfrica,Hausa,Tarno,chief,group,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relations,,/Hausa_Smith_132_NK:p::/Hausa_Smith_132_NK::Thus Tarno and the chief were often cross-cousins and joking relations.,0.9,0.9,1,1,/Hausa_Smith_132_NK:p,654
c5e0969546fc,within_group,WesternAfrica,Hausa,Uwa,Baba,person,person,joking_relationship,symmetric,kawa,kawa,/Hausa_Baba_151_NK:p::/Hausa_Baba_151_NK::Uwa was my kawa .,0.8,0.8,1,1,/Hausa_Baba_151_NK:p,602
e789083321e0,within_group,WesternAfrica,Hausa,a man,his elder brother’s wife,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,privileged teasing,,"/Hausa_Baba_21_NK:p::/Hausa_Baba_21_NK::There are also certain other joking relations, such as a man and his elder brother’s wife, who behave in a similar way.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/Hausa_Baba_21_NK:p,607
7452cf64bf44,within_group,WesternAfrica,Hausa,a man,his older sibling’s spouse,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking,,"/Hausa_Greenberg_204_NK:p::/Hausa_Greenberg_204_NK::The other joking relationships between individuals are between grandparents and grandchildren, between parents and their youngest child (who in this as in other respects is assimilated to a grandchild), between a man and his older sibling’s spouse and a woman and her older sibling’s spouse.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Hausa_Greenberg_204_NK:p,640
4ba81ed226fa,within_group,WesternAfrica,Hausa,a person,BrDa,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relations,,"/Hausa_Faulkingham_139_NK:p::/Hausa_Faulkingham_139_NK::While in the abstract, my informants denied that joking relations exist between a person and FaSi and MoBr, their own joking behavior toward these relatives and towards BrSo, BrDa, SiSo, and SiDa contradicted their statements.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/Hausa_Faulkingham_139_NK:p,631
