343e26792ed4,within_group,WesternAfrica,Hausa,male cross-cousin (opposite sex pair),female cross-cousin (opposite sex pair),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relatives,,"/Hausa_Greenberg_207_NK:p::/Hausa_Greenberg_207_NK::This is only a passing phase, since among the Moslem Hausa as a whole, cross-cousins are considered joking relatives, a survival of pagan conditions, while parallel cousins are not, in spite of the preferred status accorded to this type of marriage.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Hausa_Greenberg_207_NK:p,643
224948687044,within_group,WesternAfrica,Hausa,male parallel-cousin,female parallel-cousin,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,abo kan wa sa (joking relatives),abo kan wa sa,"/Hausa_Greenberg_207_NK:p::/Hausa_Greenberg_207_NK::A pagan explaining what would be involved in his conversion said that he would now consider his parallel cousins as 'abo kan wa sa, “joking relatives,” and marry his former clan mates.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/Hausa_Greenberg_207_NK:p,644
9161e71e5841,within_group,WesternAfrica,Hausa,mallams,hunters,group,group,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Hausa_Smith_43_NK:p::/Hausa_Smith_43_NK::butchers and blacksmiths (mahauta, makira), mallams and hunters [unavailable], maharba), barber-doctors and blacksmiths (wanzamai, makira);",0.8,0.8,1,1,/Hausa_Smith_43_NK:p,666
b22a239bb77e,within_group,WesternAfrica,Hausa,occupational groups,occupational groups,group,group,joking_relationship,symmetric,abokin wasa,Abokin wasa,"/Hausa_Hill_203_NK:p::/Hausa_Hill_203_NK::Abokin wasa. The ‘joking-relationship’ between certain friends, occupational groups, and categories of kin, the latter including cross-cousins, grandparents (and grandchildren) and a younger brother of a husband or wife. || /Hausa_Smith_44_NK:p::/Hausa_Smith_44_NK::Joking relationships between occupational groups were accompanied by preferred marriages between members of the groups, and a strong feeling of zumunta or mutual co-operation. Like all joking relationships, these were expressed annually in the custom of collecting kudin shara.",0.9,0.9,2,2,/Hausa_Hill_203_NK:p; /Hausa_Smith_44_NK:p,646;676
712a9c840deb,within_group,WesternAfrica,Hausa,"one clan (d an wâ, “son of the older brother”)","partner clan (d án k ánwa, “son of the younger sister”)",clan,clan,joking_relationship,mixed,’abó[unknown]kán wa[unknown]sá[unknown] (paired clans),’abó[unknown]kán wa[unknown]sá[unknown],"/Hausa_Greenberg_203_NK:p::/Hausa_Greenberg_203_NK::This usage is extended to pairs of clans between whom there is traditional intermarriage. All clans have at least one partner in such a relationship, occasionally — several, and a pair of clans thus connected are said to be ’abó[unknown]kán wa[unknown]sá[unknown] to each other. Of the two clans, one is said to be d an wâ[unknown], “son of the older brother,” and the other one d án k ánwa[unknown], “son of the younger sister”; and any member of the latter clan, as the inferior, is privileged to take from any member of the former.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Hausa_Greenberg_203_NK:p,636
