between a man and his wife's dzomanci; and between a woman and her

father-in-law and her mother-in-law's sister's sons.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Nupe_Nadel_242_NK:p,935
b1f044ff7122,within_group,WesternAfrica,Nupe,a woman,her father-in-law,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,dzomanci / joking relationship,dzomanci,"/Nupe_Nadel_242_NK:p::/Nupe_Nadel_242_NK::between a man and his wife's dzomanci; and between a woman and her

father-in-law and her mother-in-law's sister's sons.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Nupe_Nadel_242_NK:p,936
022e0d4f8f0c,within_group,WesternAfrica,Nupe,a woman,her mother-in-law's sister's sons,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,dzomanci / joking relationship,dzomanci,"/Nupe_Nadel_242_NK:p::/Nupe_Nadel_242_NK::between a man and his wife's dzomanci; and between a woman and her

father-in-law and her mother-in-law's sister's sons.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Nupe_Nadel_242_NK:p,937
843b61323348,within_group,WesternAfrica,Nupe,age-grade association under its leader,age-group,age_set,age_set,joking_relationship,symmetric,,navũ,"/Nupe_Nadel_241_NK:p::/Nupe_Nadel_241_NK::The girls come in little groups, while the boys and young men are formed into companies, each composed of an age-grade association under its leader.... now and again this clowning gives way to a mock battle, when the boys will thrust and throw the torches at one another, age-group fighting age-group, not seriously, but keenly and with marked excitement.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/Nupe_Nadel_241_NK:p,929
ffe1bb10d5bd,within_group,WesternAfrica,Nupe,boys and young men,the girls,group,group,teasing,mixed,,,"/Nupe_Nadel_241_NK:p::/Nupe_Nadel_241_NK::There is cruder horseplay, too, the boys trying to seize the girls or to bump into them as though unwittingly; while in the shadows and under the bridge couples pursue their amorous play unseen. But though there is plenty of teasing and joking, the language is never obscene.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Nupe_Nadel_241_NK:p,928
b4bbcac0c578,within_group,WesternAfrica,Nupe,cross-cousins,cross-cousins,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,dzomanci / joking relationship,dzomanci,"/Nupe_Nadel_242_NK:p::/Nupe_Nadel_242_NK::Thus the joking relationship obtains between grandparents and grandchildren of the opposite sex; between cross cousins; between a man and his

elder brothers' wives;",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Nupe_Nadel_242_NK:p,932
456a405e241b,within_group,WesternAfrica,Nupe,daughters-in-law,the deceased,kin_role,person,joking_relationship,unclear,joking relations,,"/Nupe_Nadel_126_NK:p::/Nupe_Nadel_126_NK::This, the Nupe will tell you, is quite in order: for the workers are always in-laws and ‘joking relations’ of the deceased (daughters-in-law or the wives of younger brothers), and are hence exempt from mourning.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Nupe_Nadel_126_NK:p,926
518634204f63,within_group,WesternAfrica,Nupe,grandparents,grandchildren of the opposite sex,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,dzomanci / joking relationship,dzomanci,"/Nupe_Nadel_242_NK:p::/Nupe_Nadel_242_NK::Thus the joking relationship obtains between grandparents and grandchildren of the opposite sex; between cross cousins; between a man and his

elder brothers' wives;",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Nupe_Nadel_242_NK:p,931
9f00727ca21f,within_group,WesternAfrica,Nupe,occupational groups,other occupational groups (within Nupe communities),group,group,joking_relationship,unclear,,,"/Nupe_Nadel_242_NK:p::/Nupe_Nadel_242_NK::Joking relationship also occurs outside the kindred, between certain Nupe sections and other tribes, or within Nupe communities

between occupational groups.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/Nupe_Nadel_242_NK:p,939
dd4695e02c07,within_group,WesternAfrica,Nupe,the threefold dynastic division of the city,rival territory,regional group,regional group,joking_relationship,symmetric,factional jealousies / mock battle,navũ,"/Nupe_Nadel_241_NK:p::/Nupe_Nadel_241_NK::The three different bathing places are chosen in accordance with the threefold dynastic division of the city, and are visited by the age-grade associations in the respective local division.... Thus the torch processions invariably led to organized and heated fights between the three groups; on the way back from the river, especially, one would ambush the other or try to invade the rival territory.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/Nupe_Nadel_241_NK:p,930
4c1575a5e7d5,within_group,WesternAfrica,Nupe,wives of younger brothers,the deceased,kin_role,person,joking_relationship,unclear,joking relations,,"/Nupe_Nadel_126_NK:p::/Nupe_Nadel_126_NK::This, the Nupe will tell you, is quite in order: for the workers are always in-laws and ‘joking relations’ of the deceased (daughters-in-law or the wives of younger brothers), and are hence exempt from mourning.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Nupe_Nadel_126_NK:p,927
ad7842064381,within_group,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,Ba'ari,Gorogo,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking partnership,,"/Tallensi_Fortes_91_NK:p::/Tallensi_Fortes_91_NK::Each clan among the Talis, and in some cases a particular maximal lineage, has one or more joking partnerships with other clans or lineages. Thus Ba'ari has a joking partnership with Gorogo, Zubiuŋ with Sakpee and Gundaat, Kpata'ar with Wakii, Degal with Samiit, and so forth.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/Tallensi_Fortes_91_NK:p,1006
ffeeb62329a6,within_group,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,Chief of Tongo,Gbizug t□ndaana,lineage,lineage,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Tallensi_Fortes_94_NK:p::/Tallensi_Fortes_94_NK::The Chief of Tongo and the Gbizug t□ndaana, on the rare occasions when they meet in public, will interrupt a long and ceremonious discussion with a95 jesting altercation about their mythologically founded bonds of mutual coercion.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tallensi_Fortes_94_NK:p,1023
d390bf600b5a,within_group,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,Degal,Samiit,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking partnership,,"/Tallensi_Fortes_91_NK:p::/Tallensi_Fortes_91_NK::Thus Ba'ari has a joking partnership with Gorogo, Zubiuŋ with Sakpee and Gundaat, Kpata'ar with Wakii, Degal with Samiit, and so forth.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/Tallensi_Fortes_91_NK:p,1010
c89fd454b9f3,within_group,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,Father's mother,child,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Tallensi_Fortes_207_NK:p::/Tallensi_Fortes_207_NK::Father's mother is the acme of indulgence, for unlike mother she never has to forbid or scold and feels no Techniques Of responsibility for a child's conduct.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/Tallensi_Fortes_207_NK:p,996
f29b8064d502,within_group,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,Gbizug,Tongo,lineage,lineage,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Tallensi_Fortes_94_NK:p::/Tallensi_Fortes_94_NK::Gbizug and Tongo men, for example, have a habit of doing this. The Chief of Tongo and the Gbizug t□ndaana, on the rare occasions when they meet in public, will interrupt a long and ceremonious discussion with a95 jesting altercation about their mythologically founded bonds of mutual coercion.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tallensi_Fortes_94_NK:p,1022
596097c38e78,within_group,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,Kpata'ar,Gundaat,lineage,lineage,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Tallensi_Fortes_94_NK:p::/Tallensi_Fortes_94_NK::Elders of Kpata'ar and Gundaat, which have a similar politico-ritual relationship to that of Tongo and Gbizug, often tease one another in the same way when they meet to perform a ceremony together.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tallensi_Fortes_94_NK:p,1024
089071cab358,within_group,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,Kpata'ar,Wakii,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking partnership,,"/Tallensi_Fortes_91_NK:p::/Tallensi_Fortes_91_NK::Thus Ba'ari has a joking partnership with Gorogo, Zubiuŋ with Sakpee and Gundaat, Kpata'ar with Wakii, Degal with Samiit, and so forth.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/Tallensi_Fortes_91_NK:p,1009
a747185b8ca5,within_group,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,Zubiuŋ,Gundaat,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking partnership,,"/Tallensi_Fortes_91_NK:p::/Tallensi_Fortes_91_NK::Thus Ba'ari has a joking partnership with Gorogo, Zubiuŋ with Sakpee and Gundaat, Kpata'ar with Wakii, Degal with Samiit, and so forth.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/Tallensi_Fortes_91_NK:p,1008
4da4180f0389,within_group,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,Zubiuŋ,Sakpee,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking partnership,,"/Tallensi_Fortes_91_NK:p::/Tallensi_Fortes_91_NK::Thus Ba'ari has a joking partnership with Gorogo, Zubiuŋ with Sakpee and Gundaat, Kpata'ar with Wakii, Degal with Samiit, and so forth.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/Tallensi_Fortes_91_NK:p,1007
4b3ad93387b7,within_group,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,a man,his siblings-in-law,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationship,,/Tallensi_Fortes_120_NK:p::/Tallensi_Fortes_120_NK::A man has a joking relationship with his siblings-in-law similar to that of his wife with his sisters and distant clansmen.,0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tallensi_Fortes_120_NK:p,989
34d54243b80d,within_group,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,a man,his wife's siblings (in the widest classificatory sense of all her clan-siblings),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,Conventionalized joking,,"/Tallensi_Fortes_273_NK:p::/Tallensi_Fortes_273_NK::Conventionalized joking between a man and his wife's siblings (in the widest classificatory sense of all her clan-siblings), and of a woman with her husband's sisters and his more distant clansmen, is another indication of the social equivalence of siblings.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tallensi_Fortes_273_NK:p,998
d378918ee5b6,within_group,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,a woman,her husband's more distant clansmen,kin_role,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,Conventionalized joking,,"/Tallensi_Fortes_273_NK:p::/Tallensi_Fortes_273_NK::Conventionalized joking between a man and his wife's siblings (in the widest classificatory sense of all her clan-siblings), and of a woman with her husband's sisters and his more distant clansmen, is another indication of the social equivalence of siblings.",0.8,0.8,1,1,/Tallensi_Fortes_273_NK:p,1000
04896c66a8b4,within_group,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,a woman,her husband's sisters,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,Conventionalized joking,,"/Tallensi_Fortes_273_NK:p::/Tallensi_Fortes_273_NK::Conventionalized joking between a man and his wife's siblings (in the widest classificatory sense of all her clan-siblings), and of a woman with her husband's sisters and his more distant clansmen, is another indication of the social equivalence of siblings.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tallensi_Fortes_273_NK:p,999
33e28c361b77,within_group,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,a woman (wife),"sisters-in-law (p(ɔ)□akie, pl. p(ɔ)□akies)",kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joke and play,p(ɔ)□akie,"/Tallensi_Fortes_93_NK(1):p::/Tallensi_Fortes_93_NK(1)::With them, often her equals in years and her companions in work, she can laugh and play and joke.  Like herself, they are already, or are fated to be, married out of their natal families to ‘strangers’.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tallensi_Fortes_93_NK(1):p,1021
cc9f04a12331,within_group,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,clansman,clansman's wife,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,affinal/complementary teasing,,"/Tallensi_Fortes_91_NK:p::/Tallensi_Fortes_91_NK::A man will tease his wife's brother (dakii) or sister (dakii-p(ɔ)k) or his sister's husband (dakii) or a clansman's wife in this way, and a woman her brother's wife (p(ɔ)□akii) or her husband's sister (p(ɔ)□akii), to the amusement of both.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/Tallensi_Fortes_91_NK:p,1015
fd7a3767922d,within_group,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,father's sister (ba-p(ɔ)k / pug(ə)r(ə)ba),nephew/niece (brother's child),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,ba-p(ɔ)k; pug(ə)r(ə)ba,/Tallensi_Fortes_336_nk:p::/Tallensi_Fortes_336_nk::A person recognizes the sister of any man in the inner lineage or medial lineage whom he calls ‘father’ as his ba-p(ɔ)k. ... In theory the joking relationship holds for granchild and grandfather's sister.,0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tallensi_Fortes_336_nk:p,1004
a7a020b3f83a,within_group,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,grandchildren,maternal grandparents (ma-kpeem),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,ma-kpeem,"/Tallensi_Fortes_301_nk:p::/Tallensi_Fortes_301_nk::As will be remembered, grandchildren joke with their maternal grandparents.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tallensi_Fortes_301_nk:p,1002
e15d9fb93dfa,within_group,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,grandfather's sister,grandchild,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,/Tallensi_Fortes_336_nk:p::/Tallensi_Fortes_336_nk::In theory the joking relationship holds for granchild and grandfather's sister.,0.85,0.85,1,1,/Tallensi_Fortes_336_nk:p,1005
7179ed80b4b7,within_group,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,grandparent,grandchild,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,mutual ragging / joking relationship,kooh,"/Tallensi_17_NK:p::/Tallensi_17_NK::In this case equality, comatibility and partisanship, as if in league against the generation between them, are emphasized, more particularly by the joking relationship—mutual ragging among the Tallensi—permitted between grandparent and grandchild. || /Tallensi_Fortes_197_nk:p::/Tallensi_Fortes_197_nk::You cannot tease or joke with your parent or your child, the Tallensi say, because you do so with your grandparent or grandchild.",0.9,0.9,2,2,/Tallensi_17_NK:p; /Tallensi_Fortes_197_nk:p,984;992
ce4627496bde,within_group,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,grandparents,grandchildren,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,classical joking relationship,,"/Tallensi_Fortes_222_NK:p::/Tallensi_Fortes_222_NK::As in many tribal societies, and consistently with this point of view, the restraint that is customary between parents and children is counterbalanced by a classical joking relationship between grandparents and grandchildren.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tallensi_Fortes_222_NK:p,997
5025d2174913,within_group,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,his wife,distant clansmen,kin_role,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationship,,/Tallensi_Fortes_120_NK:p::/Tallensi_Fortes_120_NK::A man has a joking relationship with his siblings-in-law similar to that of his wife with his sisters and distant clansmen.,0.85,0.85,1,1,/Tallensi_Fortes_120_NK:p,991
ce4c27528202,within_group,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,his wife,her sisters,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationship,,/Tallensi_Fortes_120_NK:p::/Tallensi_Fortes_120_NK::A man has a joking relationship with his siblings-in-law similar to that of his wife with his sisters and distant clansmen.,0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tallensi_Fortes_120_NK:p,990
58489d07be6f,within_group,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,man,sister's husband (dakii),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,affinal teasing,dakii,"/Tallensi_Fortes_91_NK:p::/Tallensi_Fortes_91_NK::A man will tease his wife's brother (dakii) or sister (dakii-p(ɔ)k) or his sister's husband (dakii) or a clansman's wife in this way, and a woman her brother's wife (p(ɔ)□akii) or her husband's sister (p(ɔ)□akii), to the amusement of both.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tallensi_Fortes_91_NK:p,1012
83698029cf5e,within_group,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,man,wife's brother (dakii),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,affinal teasing,dakii,"/Tallensi_Fortes_91_NK:p::/Tallensi_Fortes_91_NK::As we have noted, the natives compare them with the joking relationship of affines of the same generation. A man will tease his wife's brother (dakii) or sister (dakii-p(ɔ)k) or his sister's husband (dakii) or a clansman's wife in this way, and a woman her brother's wife (p(ɔ)□akii) or her husband's sister (p(ɔ)□akii), to the amusement of both.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tallensi_Fortes_91_NK:p,1011
f3fd4aeef200,within_group,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,maternal grandparents,grandchild,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationship,ba-kpeem / ma-kpeem / yaab / yaab-p(ɔ)k,"/Tallensi_Forte_236_NK:p::/Tallensi_Forte_236_NK::This joking relationship holds for both sets of grandparents, and serves the purpose of reconciling the potentially conflicting elements in their relationships.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tallensi_Forte_236_NK:p,986
de80b8e1537f,within_group,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,parent,child,kin_role,kin_role,avoidance,symmetric,,kooh,"/Tallensi_Fortes_197_nk:p::/Tallensi_Fortes_197_nk::In particular, the familiarity between parents and children never goes so far as mutual teasing, as in a joking relationship. If a native is asked about this he frowns and says that it would be most offensive for a son or daughter to tease or joke with (kooh) a parent or vice versa.",0.95,0.95,1,1,/Tallensi_Fortes_197_nk:p,993
3c6e04192cdb,within_group,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,parents' parents,Grandchildren,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationship,,"/Tallensi_Fortes_207_NK:p::/Tallensi_Fortes_207_NK::Deference and respect for their conjugal privacy, amounting to a mild avoidance associated with the incest taboo is proper in relation to parents. But with parents' parents one has a joking relationship, which reconciles respect for the paternal grandfather's authority with the affectionate trust in the relationship of alternate generations...",0.95,0.95,1,1,/Tallensi_Fortes_207_NK:p,994
074113019d05,within_group,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,paternal grandfather,Grandchildren,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Tallensi_Fortes_207_NK:p::/Tallensi_Fortes_207_NK::But with parents' parents one has a joking relationship, which reconciles respect for the paternal grandfather's authority with the affectionate trust in the relationship of alternate generations...",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tallensi_Fortes_207_NK:p,995
100e8c2b89d0,within_group,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,paternal grandparents,grandchild,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking relationship,ba-kpeem / ma-kpeem / yaab / yaab-p(ɔ)k,"/Tallensi_Forte_236_NK:p::/Tallensi_Forte_236_NK::This joking relationship holds for both sets of grandparents, and serves the purpose of reconciling the potentially conflicting elements in their relationships.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tallensi_Forte_236_NK:p,985
4650b7518d64,within_group,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,patrilineal grandparent,grandchild of the same sex,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,privileged familiarity / joking,,"/Tallensi_Forte_236_NK:p::/Tallensi_Forte_236_NK::With it goes an intimacy and mutual trust, more particularly between patrilineal grandparent and grandchild of the same sex, such as is not found between any other kinsfolk, close or distant.",0.85,0.85,1,1,/Tallensi_Forte_236_NK:p,987
96e5d9b6f5bc,within_group,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,siblings-in-law,each other,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,,"/Tallensi_Forte_236_NK:p::/Tallensi_Forte_236_NK::Cf. the similar usage between siblings-in-law, p. . Joking in the present case reconciles the familiarity permissible on the grounds of personal interest and the social equivalence of alternate generations, with the respect due to grandparents because of their age and their lineage status.",0.6,0.6,1,1,/Tallensi_Forte_236_NK:p,988
113b73a88b0c,within_group,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,the men whom his ah(ə)b calls ‘father’,a sister's son,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,,ah(ə)b,"/Tallensi_Fortes_296_NK:p::/Tallensi_Fortes_296_NK::The men whom his ah(ə)b calls ‘father’ he calls ‘grandfather’, and he jokes with them;",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tallensi_Fortes_296_NK:p,1001
65369dbffbe5,within_group,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,wife's sister,brother-in-law,kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,joking (licensed with wife's sister),,"/Tallensi_Fortes_334_nk:p::/Tallensi_Fortes_334_nk::It would be grossly improper, for example, to joke with her as one jokes with a wife's sister; and to strike or abuse her, even if she were much younger than oneself, would be a sin.",0.7,0.7,1,1,/Tallensi_Fortes_334_nk:p,1003
7f0ec8f62815,within_group,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,woman,brother's wife (p(ɔ)□akii),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,affinal teasing,p(ɔ)□akii,"/Tallensi_Fortes_91_NK:p::/Tallensi_Fortes_91_NK::A man will tease his wife's brother (dakii) or sister (dakii-p(ɔ)k) or his sister's husband (dakii) or a clansman's wife in this way, and a woman her brother's wife (p(ɔ)□akii) or her husband's sister (p(ɔ)□akii), to the amusement of both.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tallensi_Fortes_91_NK:p,1013
2c6b732fbbe7,within_group,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,woman,husband's sister (p(ɔ)□akii),kin_role,kin_role,joking_relationship,symmetric,affinal teasing,p(ɔ)□akii,"/Tallensi_Fortes_91_NK:p::/Tallensi_Fortes_91_NK::A man will tease his wife's brother (dakii) or sister (dakii-p(ɔ)k) or his sister's husband (dakii) or a clansman's wife in this way, and a woman her brother's wife (p(ɔ)□akii) or her husband's sister (p(ɔ)□akii), to the amusement of both.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Tallensi_Fortes_91_NK:p,1014
7bdc32aa0821,within_group,WesternAfrica,Wolof,Diop,Ndiaye,patronym,patronym,joking_relationship,symmetric,kall; kall / joking greeting,kall,"/Wolof_181_nk:p::/Wolof_181_nk::A. Diop!(B’s name)

B. Ndiaye! (A’s name)

Diop!

Ndiaye!

A. Sant ba neehul!

That surname is not pleasing!

B. Neeh na kaay.

It is so pleasing.

A. Lekk tyeb!

(The Diops) eat rice! [luxury food; this
means they are greedy]

B. Ndiaye rek, nyoo ko lekk!

Only the Ndiayes, it is they who eat it! || /Wolof_182_nk:p::/Wolof_182_nk::A. Diop! (B’s name)

Diop! (B’s name)

B. Sa sant Ndiaye neehul!

Your surname Ndiaye is not pleasing!

The joking relationship provides, however, only a very limited opportunity to express equality. The kall involves only certain pairs of surnames, and any one person has only one or two surname groups in which to find joking partners.",0.925,0.95,2,2,/Wolof_181_nk:p; /Wolof_182_nk:p,1092;1094
02d34a214834,within_group,WesternAfrica,Wolof,griot,young or low-ranking noble,group,kin_role,joking_relationship,unclear,,kall,"/Wolof_181_nk:p::/Wolof_181_nk::It is rare to see a joking greeting between a griot and the Imam of the mosque, for instance, although such a greeting might well occur between a griot and a young or low-ranking noble.",0.65,0.65,1,1,/Wolof_181_nk:p,1093
2053ec36052b,within_group,WesternAfrica,Yoruba,the grave diggers,his children,group,kin_role,joking_relationship,non_symmetric,joke while they work,,"/Yoruba_67_nk:p::/Yoruba_67_nk::If the deceased was elderly, the grave diggers joke while they work to make his children forger some of their sorrow and laugh a little; but if he was their own age or younger, they do not joke while they work and they do not ask for food and drink.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/Yoruba_67_nk:p,1095
5b04c55b385d,within_group,WesternAfrica,wolof,Diop,Ndiaye,patronym,patronym,joking_relationship,symmetric,kall (joking relationship),kall,"/wolof_180_nk:p::/wolof_180_nk::Certain pairs of surnames, and the kin groups which bear them, stand in a special relationship to each other, called kall ‘jokingrelationship.’ The families Diop and Ndiaye, for example, are in jokingrelationship because, supposedly, ‘the Diops are the mothers of the Ndiayes.’",0.9,0.9,1,1,/wolof_180_nk:p,1487
ca06aa91e1f7,within_group,WesternAfrica,wolof,Gei,Seka,clan,clan,joking_relationship,symmetric,,b[unknown]r[unknown]m,"/wolof_55_nk:p::/wolof_55_nk::In clan relationships, the Njai are the b[unknown]r[unknown]m of the Gei, the Gei b[unknown]r[unknown]m of the Seka, the Seka b[unknown]r[unknown]m of the Job, and so on.",0.9,0.9,1,1,/wolof_55_nk:p,1492
